@prefix ex: <urn:studium:> .
@prefix ns1: <urn:studium:rel:> .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-a-basis-for-v-need-not-contain-a-basis-for-a-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) Every basis for $S$ is part of a basis for $V$ .",
        "(d) A basis for V need not contain a basis for S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "24. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space, and let $S$ be a subspace of $V$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "A basis for the whole space need not contain a basis for a given subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "全空间的基不必包含子空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness V = V_2, S = span{(1, 1)} and the basis {(1, 0), (0, 1)} are constructed by the model; Apostol states only the assertion to be proved." ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.09" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 24(c) states that every basis for a subspace S extends to a basis for V, and Exercise 24(d) denies the converse: in V = V_2 let S be the line spanned by (1, 1), and take the basis {(1, 0), (0, 1)} for V. No nonempty subset of that basis is a basis for S, because neither (1, 0) nor (0, 1) lies on the line x = y, and the subsets of size 2 span V and not S. The asymmetry is that extension is a choice one is free to make while a prescribed basis has already made it wrongly." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-a-whole-line-of-right-zeros> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (x_{1},x_{2} + y_{2}),$ $a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (ax_{1},ax_{2}).$",
        "If the set is not a linear space, indicate which axioms are violated." ;
    ex:nameEn "Without commutativity the zero element is no longer unique: a whole line of right zeros" ;
    ex:nameZh "交换律一失效,零元不再唯一:一整条右零元直线" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The family of right zeros (t, 0), the witness x = (1, 5) and the observation that Theorem 15.1 uses Axiom 3 at exactly this step are the model's." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-zero-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-states-the-zero-only-as-a-right-neutral-element> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "In the structure of Exercise 31(c), where (x_1, x_2) + (y_1, y_2) = (x_1, x_2 + y_2), every element of the form (t, 0) satisfies x + (t, 0) = (x_1, x_2) for all x, so there are infinitely many zero elements and Theorem 15.1 fails. The proof of Theorem 15.1 gets O_1 + O_2 = O_1 and O_2 + O_1 = O_2 from Axiom 5 and then needs the commutative law to identify the two, and it is exactly Axiom 3 that fails here. Axiom 6 fails as well against any fixed choice of zero, since x + (-1)x = (x_1, 0) depends on x: for x = (1, 5) the sum is (1, 0), not (0, 0)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-addition-reading-one-coordinate-from-each-summand> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (|x_{1} + x_{2}|,|y_{1} + y_{2}|),\\qquad a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (|ax_{1}|,|ax_{2}|).$",
        "In each case determine whether or not $S$ is a linear space with the operations of addition and multiplication by scalars defined as indicated." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(d) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (|x_{1} + x_{2}|,|y_{1} + y_{2}|),\\qquad a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (|ax_{1}|,|ax_{2}|).$" ;
    ex:nameEn "An addition that reads one coordinate from each summand fails commutativity and associativity" ;
    ex:nameZh "各取一个加项一个分量的加法既不交换也不结合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses x = (1, 0), y = (0, 0), x = y = z = (1, 0), x = (-1, 0) and the computed values (0, 1), (2, 1), (1, 2) are all the model's." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-in-the-scalar-product-destroys-the-identity> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-4-associative-law-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "On the set of ordered pairs of real numbers with (x_1, x_2) + (y_1, y_2) = (|x_1 + x_2|, |y_1 + y_2|), Axiom 3 fails at x = (1, 0) and y = (0, 0), where x + y = (1, 0) but y + x = (0, 1). Axiom 4 fails at x = y = z = (1, 0): (x + y) + z = (2, 1) while x + (y + z) = (1, 2). Axiom 5 fails too, because the first coordinate of every sum is an absolute value and so cannot equal the first coordinate of x = (-1, 0)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-addition-that-keeps-only-the-left-first-coordinate-is-noncommutative> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (x_{1},x_{2} + y_{2}),$ $a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (ax_{1},ax_{2}).$",
        "31. Let $S$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x_{1}, x_{2})$ of real numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "An addition that keeps only the left first coordinate is not commutative" ;
    ex:nameZh "只保留左项第一分量的加法不满足交换律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses x = (1, 0), y = (0, 0), a = b = 1 and the values (2, 0) and (1, 0) are the model's." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-addition-that-discards-the-second-coordinate-has-no-zero> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "On the set of ordered pairs of real numbers with (x_1, x_2) + (y_1, y_2) = (x_1, x_2 + y_2) and the usual scaling, Axiom 3 fails at x = (1, 0) and y = (0, 0): x + y = (1, 0) but y + x = (0, 0). Axiom 9 fails at a = b = 1 and x = (1, 0), where (a + b)x = (2, 0) but ax + bx = (1, 0). Axiom 4 does hold, so associativity is no guarantee of commutativity." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-affine-line-missing-the-origin> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "24. All vectors $(x, y, z)$ in $V_{3}$ with $y = 5x$ .",
        "25. All vectors $(x, y, z)$ in $V_{3}$ with $3x + 4y = 1$ , $z = 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "An affine line that misses the origin fails Axioms 1, 2, 5 and 6" ;
    ex:nameZh "不过原点的仿射直线同时失去 1、2、5、6" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses (1/3, 0, 0), (-1, 1, 0), their sum (-2/3, 1, 0) and the value 3x + 4y = 2 are all computed by the model; Apostol gives only the defining equation." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-union-of-two-coordinate-planes-fails-additive-closure> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "The set of vectors (x, y, z) in V_3 with 3x + 4y = 1 and z = 0 is a line that does not pass through the origin, so Axiom 5 fails outright: 3(0) + 4(0) = 0, not 1. Axiom 1 fails at the pair (1/3, 0, 0) and (-1, 1, 0), both in the set, whose sum (-2/3, 1, 0) satisfies 3x + 4y = 2; Axiom 2 fails at 2(1/3, 0, 0) = (2/3, 0, 0), which satisfies 3x + 4y = 2. The homogeneous version of the same condition, as in Exercises 22 and 24, is a subspace, so the inhomogeneous right-hand side 1 is the whole defect." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-an-identity-hides-a-dependence-among-transcendental-functions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(e) $\\{e^{x}, e^{-x}, \\cosh x\\}$ .",
        "(g) $\\{\\cos^{2} x, \\sin^{2} x\\}$ .",
        "(h) $\\{1, \\cos 2x, \\sin^{2} x\\}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "23. Let $V$ be the linear space consisting of all real-valued functions defined on the real line." ;
    ex:nameEn "A trigonometric or hyperbolic identity hides a linear dependence among unlike-looking functions" ;
    ex:nameZh "一个恒等式把貌似无关的函数变成相关集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The two explicit dependence relations, the dimension 2 for each span, and the independence of {cos^2 x, sin^2 x} are all supplied by the model; Apostol only asks the question." ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.09" ;
    ex:statement "In the space of real-valued functions on the real line, two of the sets of Exercise 23 are dependent for a reason invisible in their notation: cosh x = (1/2)e^x + (1/2)e^{-x} makes {e^x, e^{-x}, cosh x} dependent with span of dimension 2, and cos 2x = 1 - 2 sin^2 x makes {1, cos 2x, sin^2 x} dependent with span of dimension 2 as well. Neither dependence can be read off from the fact that the three functions are pairwise nonproportional, which is the standing trap: pairwise independence is not independence. The neighbouring set {cos^2 x, sin^2 x} is independent, so having squares of trigonometric functions is not itself the cause." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-anisotropy-forces-a-definite-form> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "2. Suppose we retain the first three axioms for a real inner product (symmetry, linearity, and homogeneity) but replace the fourth axiom by a new axiom (4'): $(x, x) = 0$ if and only if x = O.",
        "Prove that either $(x, x) > 0$ for all $x \\neq O$ or else $(x, x) < 0$ for all $x \\neq O$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "2. Suppose we retain the first three axioms for a real inner product (symmetry, linearity, and homogeneity) but replace the fourth axiom by a new axiom (4'): $(x, x) = 0$ if and only if x = O.",
        "In the space spanned by $\\{x, y\\}$ , find an element $z \\neq O$ with $(z, z) = 0$ .]" ;
    ex:nameEn "Replacing positivity by nondegeneracy still forces a definite form" ;
    ex:nameZh "把正定性换成非退化性仍迫使形式是定的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-isotropic-element-in-a-mixed-sign-plane> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 2 keeps symmetry, additivity and homogeneity but weakens axiom 4 to (4'): (x, x) = 0 if and only if x = O. The conclusion is that either (x, x) > 0 for every nonzero x or else (x, x) < 0 for every nonzero x. So the strict inequality in Apostol's axiom 4 is not an extra demand beyond nondegeneracy; up to an overall sign it is equivalent to it." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-cyclic-dependence-hides-behind-distinct-supports> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "2. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{4}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "x _ {1} = (1 1, 0, 0), \\quad x _ {2} = (0, 1, 1, 0), \\quad x _ {3} = (0, 0, 1, 1), \\quad x _ {4} = (1, 0, 0, 1)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "2. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{4}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "x _ {1} = (1 1, 0, 0), \\quad x _ {2} = (0, 1, 1, 0), \\quad x _ {3} = (0, 0, 1, 1), \\quad x _ {4} = (1, 0, 0, 1)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Four vectors with distinct supports that are nonetheless dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "支撑各不相同却依然相依的四个向量" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-witness-must-be-exhibited-not-merely-counted>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-n-independent-elements-form-a-basis> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthonormalizing-a-dependent-list-yields-a-shorter-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "In Exercise 2(a) the four vectors of V_4 each have their two nonzero entries in a different pair of positions, which makes them look independent, yet they satisfy the cyclic relation x_1 - x_2 + x_3 - x_4 = O and span only a three-dimensional subspace. Having as many vectors as the dimension of the ambient space and no two of them proportional is not enough for independence; the count-based half of Theorem 15.7 needs independence as a hypothesis, not as a conclusion." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-degree-exactly-k-inside-p-n-still-fails-closure> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "14. $f(0) + f'(0) = 0$ . 19. $f$ has degree $\\leq k$ , where $k < n$ , or $f = 0$ .",
        "15. $f(0) = f(1)$ . 20. $f$ has degree $k$ , where $k < n$ , or $f = 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $P_{n}$ denote the linear space of all real polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , where n is fixed." ;
    ex:nameEn "Adjoining the zero polynomial repairs Axioms 2 and 5 but not Axiom 1" ;
    ex:nameZh "补上零多项式救回公理 2 和 5,救不回公理 1" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses f(t) = t^k and g(t) = 1 - t^k, the k = 0 caveat, and the dimension k + 1 of the Exercise 19 subspace are all computed by the model; Apostol only poses the two exercises." ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-fails-axiom-2-at-the-scalar-zero>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.09" ;
    ex:statement "In P_n, the set of polynomials of degree exactly k, with k < n, together with the zero polynomial, is not a subspace: Axiom 1 fails at f(t) = t^k and g(t) = 1 - t^k, both of degree k, whose sum is the constant 1, of degree 0 and not in the set unless k = 0. Adjoining f = 0 does repair the two failures that the degree-exactly-n set of Section 15.3 suffers, namely closure under the scalar 0 and the absence of a zero element, so Axiom 1 is the only surviving defect. Exercise 19 relaxes degree k to degree at most k on the same index and is a subspace of dimension k + 1, which localizes the entire failure in the word exactly." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-derivative-pairing-is-blind-to-constants> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $(f,g) = \\int_0^1 f'(t)g'(t)dt.$",
        "12. In the linear space of all real polynomials, determine whether or not $(f,g)$ is an inner product if $(f,g)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) $(f,g) = \\int_0^1 f'(t)g'(t)dt.$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Degenerate pairing: the integral of the product of derivatives" ;
    ex:nameZh "退化配对:导数之积的积分" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-single-point-evaluation-is-degenerate>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "On the space of all real polynomials, (f, g) = integral over [0, 1] of f'(t) g'(t) dt is symmetric and bilinear but fails positivity, because every nonzero constant polynomial has vanishing derivative and hence vanishing self-pairing. Its null space is exactly the constants, so the formula becomes an honest inner product once the space is cut down to polynomials with f(0) = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-dropping-the-conjugate-destroys-positivity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f, g) = \\int_ {a} ^ {b} w (t) f (t) \\overline {{g (t)}} d t,",
        "17. Prove that the space of all complex-valued functions continuous on an interval $[a, b]$ becomes a unitary space",
        "where $w$ is a fixed positive function, continuous on $[a, b]$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(f, g) = \\int_ {a} ^ {b} w (t) f (t) \\overline {{g (t)}} d t,",
        "17. Prove that the space of all complex-valued functions continuous on an interval $[a, b]$ becomes a unitary space",
        "where $w$ is a fixed positive function, continuous on $[a, b]$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "In a unitary function space the conjugate on the second factor is what saves positivity" ;
    ex:nameZh "复函数空间中,第二个因子上的共轭正是保住正定性的东西" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:weighted-integral-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 17 makes the complex-valued continuous functions on [a, b] a unitary space via the integral of w(t) f(t) times the conjugate of g(t). Omitting the conjugate would give the integral of w f^2, which for f identically i equals minus the integral of w, a negative number; the diagonal values would not even be real. The conjugate is therefore not a convention but the condition under which axiom 4 can be stated." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-exponential-weight-is-what-tames-the-unbounded-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) Prove that the integral for $(f,g)$ converges absolutely for each pair of functions $f$ and $g$ in $V$ .",
        "(b) If $x_{n}(t) = t^{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ , prove that $(x_{n}, x_{m}) = (m + n)!$ .",
        "11. In the linear space of all real polynomials, define $(f,g) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}f(t)g(t)dt$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) Prove that the integral for $(f,g)$ converges absolutely for each pair of functions $f$ and $g$ in $V$ .",
        "(a) Prove that this improper integral converges absolutely for all polynomials $f$ and $g$ .",
        "11. In the linear space of all real polynomials, define $(f,g) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}f(t)g(t)dt$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "On an unbounded interval the weight is what makes the pairing exist" ;
    ex:nameZh "在无界区间上,是权函数使配对得以存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-weight-makes-improper-integral-converge> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:weighted-integral-inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-convergence-must-be-proved-before-the-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercises 11 and 14 pair functions on [0, infinity) by the improper integral of e^{-t} f(t) g(t). The unweighted integral of a product of polynomials over [0, infinity) diverges for every nonzero pair, so on an unbounded interval the weight is not a refinement of the plain integral inner product but the only thing that makes any pairing at all. Exercise 11(b) records the resulting values (x_n, x_m) = (m + n)! for the powers." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-inhomogeneous-endpoint-condition-breaks-closure> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "5. All $f$ with $f(1) = 1 + f(0)$ .",
        "In Exercises 3, 4, and 5, each function has domain containing 0 and 1." ;
    ex:nameEn "An inhomogeneous condition on the values at 0 and 1 breaks both closure axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "端点值的非齐次条件破坏两条封闭公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses f(x) = x and 2f are chosen by the model; Apostol states only the exercise." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:example-11-functions-vanishing-at-a-point> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "The set of real-valued functions with domain containing 0 and 1 that satisfy f(1) = 1 + f(0), with the usual pointwise addition and scaling, is not a linear space: Axiom 1 fails because f(x) = x and g(x) = x both lie in the set while (f + g)(1) = 2 but 1 + (f + g)(0) = 1, and Axiom 2 fails because (2f)(1) = 2 but 1 + (2f)(0) = 1. Axiom 5 also fails, since the zero function gives 0 on the left and 1 on the right. The neighbouring Exercises 3 and 4 impose homogeneous conditions on the same two values and are linear spaces, so the whole failure is caused by the additive constant 1." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-inner-product-axiom-diagnosis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In case $(f,g)$ is not an inner product, indicate which axioms are violated.",
        "In case $(x, y)$ is not an inner product, tell which axioms are not satisfied.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "12. In the linear space of all real polynomials, determine whether or not $(f,g)$ is an inner product if $(f,g)$ is defined by the formula given.",
        "In case $(x, y)$ is not an inner product, tell which axioms are not satisfied.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:nameEn "Diagnosing which inner-product axiom a candidate formula violates" ;
    ex:nameZh "诊断候选公式违反了内积的哪一条公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-and-product-of-integrals-on-polynomials>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-in-second-slot-breaks-symmetry>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-polarization-formula-is-twice-the-dot-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercises 1 and 12 ask, for a list of candidate formulas, whether each is an inner product and, if not, exactly which axioms fail. The exercises exhibit several distinct failure patterns: a formula can satisfy symmetry and positivity yet fail homogeneity, and another can satisfy symmetry and bilinearity yet fail positivity. So a formula must be tested axiom by axiom rather than accepted or rejected wholesale." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-integral-inequality-breaks-the-scalar-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "13. All $f$ integrable on $[0, 1]$ with $\\int_0^1 f(x) dx = 0$ .",
        "14. All $f$ integrable on $[0, 1]$ with $\\int_0^1 f(x) dx \\geq 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "An integral inequality breaks the scalar axioms while addition stays closed" ;
    ex:nameZh "积分不等式条件下加法封闭但数乘失效" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness f(x) = 1 and its integral value 1 are computed by the model." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-increasing-functions-fail-under-negative-scalars> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "The set of functions integrable on [0, 1] with the integral of f over [0, 1] at least 0 is closed under addition, but Axiom 2 fails: the constant function f(x) = 1 has integral 1 and lies in the set, while (-1)f has integral -1 and does not. Axiom 6 fails on the same element for the same reason. Replacing the inequality by the equality of Exercise 13 restores a genuine linear space, so the culprit is the one-sided inequality and not the integral." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-l2-optimal-approximation-can-be-poor-pointwise> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "9. In the real linear space $C(0, 2\\pi)$ with inner product $(f, g) = \\int_0^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ , let $f(x) = x$ .",
        "In the subspace spanned by $u_0(x) = 1$ , $u_1(x) = \\cos x$ , $u_2(x) = \\sin x$ , find the trigonometric polynomial nearest to $f$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "9. In the real linear space $C(0, 2\\pi)$ with inner product $(f, g) = \\int_0^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ , let $f(x) = x$ .",
        "In the subspace spanned by $u_0(x) = 1$ , $u_1(x) = \\cos x$ , $u_2(x) = \\sin x$ , find the trigonometric polynomial nearest to $f$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The nearest trigonometric polynomial can be far from f at a point" ;
    ex:nameZh "最近的三角多项式在某点仍可能远离 f" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:trigonometric-polynomial-approximation> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-linear-approximation-to-sine>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:fourier-coefficients> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 9 seeks the element of the span of 1, cos x, sin x nearest to f(x) = x on (0, 2pi); the Fourier coefficients give pi - 2 sin x. At x = 0 the approximation takes the value pi while f(0) = 0, so the pointwise error is as large as pi even though no other element of the subspace has smaller integrated square error. Best approximation in the norm of a Euclidean space controls an average, and says nothing about the error at any single point." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-law-of-cosines-needs-nonzero-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "7. If $x$ and $y$ are nonzero elements making an angle $\\theta$ with each other, then",
        "\\| x - y \\| ^ {2} = \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} - 2 \\| x \\| \\| y \\| \\cos \\theta ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "7. If $x$ and $y$ are nonzero elements making an angle $\\theta$ with each other, then" ;
    ex:nameEn "The law of cosines is stated only for nonzero elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "余弦定律只对非零元素陈述" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:angle-defined-only-in-real-euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:angle-requires-nonzero-elements> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 7 derives ||x - y||^2 = ||x||^2 + ||y||^2 - 2||x|| ||y|| cos(theta), but its hypothesis names x and y as nonzero elements making an angle theta. If either element is O the angle is undefined, since its defining quotient has a zero denominator; the identity itself then survives only in the degenerate reading in which the last term is simply absent." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-legendre-family-changes-with-the-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "4. In the linear space of all real polynomials, with inner product $(x, y) = \\int_0^1 x(t)y(t)dt$ , let $x_n(t) = t^n$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ .",
        "form an orthonormal set spanning the same subspace as $\\{x_0, x_1, x_2\\}$ .",
        "y _ {0} (t) = 1, \\quad y _ {1} (t) = \\sqrt {3} (2 t - 1), \\quad y _ {2} (t) = \\sqrt {5} (6 t ^ {2} - 6 t + 1)" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "4. In the linear space of all real polynomials, with inner product $(x, y) = \\int_0^1 x(t)y(t)dt$ , let $x_n(t) = t^n$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonalizing the same powers on (0, 1) does not give the Legendre polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "在 (0, 1) 上正交化同一组幂函数得不到 Legendre 多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomials> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthogonality-depends-on-the-interval> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 4 orthonormalizes the powers t^n on (0, 1) and obtains 1, sqrt(3)(2t - 1), sqrt(5)(6t^2 - 6t + 1). The input sequence is the same one that produces the Legendre polynomials in 15.13, but there the interval is (-1, 1); changing the interval changes the whole orthogonal family. The Legendre polynomials are therefore not intrinsic to the powers, only to the powers together with an inner product." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-log-weight-vanishing-at-an-endpoint> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f, g) = \\int_ {1} ^ {e} (\\log x) f (x) g (x) d x.",
        "8. In the real linear space $C(1, e)$ , define an inner product by the equation" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(f, g) = \\int_ {1} ^ {e} (\\log x) f (x) g (x) d x." ;
    ex:nameEn "A weight that vanishes at an endpoint still yields an inner product on C(1, e)" ;
    ex:nameZh "在端点处为零的权函数仍在 C(1, e) 上给出内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:weight-function-must-be-positive> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:weighted-integral-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 8 uses (f, g) = integral over [1, e] of (log x) f(x) g(x) dx. The weight log x is positive on the open interval but vanishes at the left endpoint x = 1, so it does not meet the letter of the requirement that the weight be a positive function on the closed interval. Positivity nevertheless survives, because a continuous f with a vanishing weighted integral of f^2 must be identically zero. The boundary case shows the positivity hypothesis on the weight is sufficient, not necessary." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-metric-criteria-need-the-real-case> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "4. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + y\\|^2 = \\| x\\|^2 + \\| y\\|^2$ .",
        "Prove that each of the statements in Exercises 3 through 7 is valid for all elements $x$ and $y$ in a real Euclidean space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Prove that each of the statements in Exercises 3 through 7 is valid for all elements $x$ and $y$ in a real Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "The metric criteria for orthogonality are asserted only for real Euclidean spaces" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交性的度量判别只在实欧氏空间中被断言" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-metric-criteria-for-orthogonality> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The instruction before Exercises 3 through 7 restricts them to a real Euclidean space, and the restriction is essential rather than cosmetic. In a complex Euclidean space the norm identities only force the real part of (x, y) to vanish: in the one-dimensional complex space with x = 1 and y = i one has ||x + y||^2 = ||x||^2 + ||y||^2 while (x, y) = -i is nonzero." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-normalizing-constant-breaks-at-the-index-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "with inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t)y(t)dt$ , let $x_n(t) = \\cos nt$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ .",
        "y _ {0} (t) = \\frac {1}{\\sqrt {\\pi}}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "y _ {0} (t) = \\frac {1}{\\sqrt {\\pi}}" ;
    ex:nameEn "The normalizing constant of the constant term differs from that of the others" ;
    ex:nameZh "常数项的规范化常数与其余各项不同" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-system-norms> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-cosine-family-is-orthogonal-only-because-of-the-half-period> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "In Exercise 3 the normalized system is y_0 = 1/sqrt(pi) but y_n = sqrt(2/pi) cos nt for n >= 1, because the integral of 1 over (0, pi) is pi while the integral of cos^2(nt) is pi/2. The single index n = 0 needs its own constant, so the uniform-looking formula for the normalized system has an exception at the first term. This is the same anomaly as the norm sqrt(2 pi) of u_0 in the trigonometric system of 15.11." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthogonality-as-a-minimum-condition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "5. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + cy\\| \\geq \\| x\\|$ for all real $c$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "5. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + cy\\| \\geq \\| x\\|$ for all real $c$ .",
        "Prove that each of the statements in Exercises 3 through 7 is valid for all elements $x$ and $y$ in a real Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality characterized as a minimum over all real multiples" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交性刻画为在全体实倍数上取最小" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> ;
    ns1:equivalent <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-metric-criteria-for-orthogonality> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 5 states that (x, y) = 0 if and only if ||x + cy|| >= ||x|| for every real c. This turns orthogonality from an algebraic identity into a minimality statement: y is orthogonal to x exactly when no correction along y shortens x. It is the one-dimensional germ of the Approximation Theorem, where the residual of the best approximation is what is orthogonal to the subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthonormal-basis-is-not-unique> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "1. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{3}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "2. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{4}$ spanned by the given vectors." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "1. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{3}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "2. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{4}$ spanned by the given vectors." ;
    ex:nameEn "The exercises ask for an orthonormal basis, not the orthonormal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "习题要求的是一个正交规范基,而非那个正交规范基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-basis> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "Both Exercises 1 and 2 say to find an orthonormal basis for the given subspace. The indefinite article is exact: replacing any basis element by its negative, or feeding the same spanning vectors to Gram-Schmidt in a different order, gives a different orthonormal basis for the same subspace. Uniqueness up to scalar factors holds only after the order of the input sequence is fixed." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-pairing-can-converge-off-the-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) Compute $(x,y)$ if $x_{n} = 2^{n}$ and $y_{n} = 1 / n!$ for $n\\geq 1$",
        "13. Let $V$ consist of all infinite sequences $\\{x_{n}\\}$ of real numbers for which the series $\\sum x_{n}^{2}$ converges." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(d) Compute $(x,y)$ if $x_{n} = 2^{n}$ and $y_{n} = 1 / n!$ for $n\\geq 1$" ;
    ex:nameEn "The series can converge for a sequence that does not belong to the space" ;
    ex:nameZh "级数对不属于该空间的序列也可能收敛" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-convergence-must-be-proved-before-the-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-l2-sequence-space-is-an-infinite-dimensional-euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-inequality-statement> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 13(d) asks for the value of the pairing when x_n = 2^n and y_n = 1/n!, even though the series of squares of 2^n diverges, so this x is not an element of V. The sum of x_n y_n nonetheless converges, to e^2 - 1. Square-summability of both factors is sufficient for convergence but not necessary, so membership in the space and finiteness of the pairing are two separate questions." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-parallelogram-law-is-forced-by-an-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $\\| x + y\\|^2 + \\| x - y\\|^2 = 2\\| x\\|^2 + 2\\| y\\|^2.$",
        "16. Prove that the following identities are valid in every Euclidean space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) $\\| x + y\\|^2 + \\| x - y\\|^2 = 2\\| x\\|^2 + 2\\| y\\|^2.$",
        "16. Prove that the following identities are valid in every Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "The parallelogram law holds in every Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "平行四边形法则在每个欧几里得空间中成立" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-cross-terms-do-not-collapse-to-twice-the-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 16(c) states that ||x + y||^2 + ||x - y||^2 = 2||x||^2 + 2||y||^2 in every Euclidean space, with the cross terms cancelling rather than being estimated. This is a sharper constraint than the triangle inequality: it is an identity, not a bound, and it is exactly what a length function must satisfy to come from an inner product at all. A norm that violates it cannot be written as the square root of any inner product." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-product-of-coordinate-sums-is-degenerate> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $(x, y) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \\sum_{j=1}^{n} y_j$ .",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) $(x, y) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \\sum_{j=1}^{n} y_j$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Degenerate bilinear form: the product of the coordinate sums" ;
    ex:nameZh "退化双线性形式:两个坐标和之积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The formula (x, y) = (sum x_i)(sum y_j) on V_n is symmetric, additive and homogeneous, so it satisfies the first three axioms, but for n >= 2 it fails positivity: x = (1, -1, 0, ..., 0) is nonzero with (x, x) = 0. It is a rank-one degenerate form, and only in the single case n = 1 does it reduce to an honest inner product." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-root-of-sum-of-squared-products-breaks-linearity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\left(\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} ^ {2} y _ {i} ^ {2}\\right) ^ {1 / 2}.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\left(\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} ^ {2} y _ {i} ^ {2}\\right) ^ {1 / 2}." ;
    ex:nameEn "Counterexample: the square root of sum x_i^2 y_i^2 is not an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "反例:(sum x_i^2 y_i^2)^{1/2} 不是内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The formula (x, y) = (sum x_i^2 y_i^2)^{1/2} on V_n is symmetric and satisfies (x, x) > 0 for x nonzero, yet it is not an inner product: it is not additive in either argument and it is only absolutely homogeneous, since (cx, y) = |c|(x, y) instead of c(x, y). It is a case where positivity and symmetry hold while additivity and homogeneity fail." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-scalar-product-that-kills-the-second-coordinate> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (x_{1} + y_{1},x_{2} + y_{2}),\\qquad a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (ax_{1},0).$",
        "(b) Prove that Axiom 10 cannot be deduced from the other axioms if Axiom 6 is replaced by Axiom 6': For every x in V there is an element y in V such that $x + y = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "A scalar product that kills the second coordinate fails Axioms 6 and 10" ;
    ex:nameZh "抹掉第二分量的数乘使公理 6 与 10 失效" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness x = (0, 1), the checks on Axioms 1-5, 7-9, and the identification of Exercise 31(a) as a model for Exercise 30(b) are all the model's; Apostol only poses the two exercises." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-is-derivable-from-apostols-form-of-axiom-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-names-the-negative-as-minus-one-times-x> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "On the set of ordered pairs of real numbers with the usual addition but with a(x_1, x_2) = (a x_1, 0), Axiom 10 fails at x = (0, 1), because 1x = (0, 0) and not (0, 1). Axiom 6 fails at the same element in the form Apostol states it, since x + (-1)x = (0, 1) + (0, 0) = (0, 1) and not the zero element (0, 0); Axioms 1 through 5, 7, 8 and 9 all hold. Because the element y = (0, -1) does satisfy x + y = (0, 0), this structure satisfies the weakened Axiom 6' of Exercise 30(b) while still failing Axiom 10, and so it is a concrete model for the independence asserted there." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-span-does-not-commute-with-intersection> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f) If $S$ and $T$ are subsets of $V$ , then $L(S \\cap T) \\subseteq L(S) \\cap L(T)$ .",
        "(g) Give an example in which $L(S \\cap T) \\neq L(S) \\cap L(T)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "22. In this exercise, $L(S)$ denotes the subspace spanned by a subset $S$ of a linear space $V$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The span of an intersection can be strictly smaller than the intersection of the spans" ;
    ex:nameZh "交的线性包可以真包含于线性包的交" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness sets S = {(1, 0), (0, 1)} and T = {(1, 0), (1, 1)} and the resulting dimensions 1 and 2 are constructed by the model; Apostol asks for an example without supplying one." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span-notation-l-of-s> ;
    ex:section "15.09" ;
    ex:statement "The inclusion of Exercise 22(f) is genuinely one-way, and Exercise 22(g) asks for the witness: in V_2 take S = {(1, 0), (0, 1)} and T = {(1, 0), (1, 1)}. Then S and T each span all of V_2, so L(S) intersected with L(T) is V_2, while S intersected with T is {(1, 0)} and its span is only the x-axis, of dimension 1 rather than 2. The obstruction is that intersecting the sets destroys elements whose linear combinations were reachable from either side, so the operation L is monotone but not compatible with intersections." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-symmetric-interval-decouples-the-coefficients> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "8. In the real linear space $C(-1,1)$ with inner product $(f,g) = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(x)g(x) dx$ , let $f(x) = e^x$ and find the linear polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "8. In the real linear space $C(-1,1)$ with inner product $(f,g) = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(x)g(x) dx$ , let $f(x) = e^x$ and find the linear polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "On a symmetric interval the constant and the slope of the best linear approximation decouple" ;
    ex:nameZh "在对称区间上,最佳线性逼近的常数项与斜率彼此解耦" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-in-a-finite-dimensional-subspace> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-best-constant-is-the-mean-value>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthogonality-depends-on-the-interval> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 8 asks for the linear polynomial nearest to e^x on (-1, 1). Because 1 and t are already orthogonal on this symmetric interval, the two coefficients can be computed independently, giving the constant (e - 1/e)/2 and the slope 3/e with no orthogonalization step. On an asymmetric interval such as (1, 3) the same two functions are not orthogonal, and the coefficients no longer separate." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:dim-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ .",
        "the integer n is called the dimension of V. We write $n = \\dim V$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "the integer n is called the dimension of V. We write $n = \\dim V$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation dim V" ;
    ex:nameZh "记号 dim V" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The dimension n of a linear space V is written n = dim V; the notation is used in hypotheses such as that of Theorem 15.7." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-vn-c-section-12-16> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "One example is complex vector space $V_{n}(\\mathbf{C})$ discussed briefly in Section 12.16." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "One example is complex vector space $V_{n}(\\mathbf{C})$ discussed briefly in Section 12.16." ;
    ex:nameEn "The complex vector space V_n(C) of Section 12.16" ;
    ex:nameZh "12.16 节中的复向量空间 V_n(C)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.10的引用语。12.16 节原文不在手上,故不陈述该节对 V_n(C) 讲了什么;第15章自己也只说 discussed briefly。" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-euclidean-space> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "An example imported by name only. 15.10, having defined complex Euclidean space, offers V_n(C) as one instance and locates it in Section 12.16 as something discussed briefly there. Chapter 15 neither constructs V_n(C) nor exhibits an inner product on it nor verifies the complex inner-product axioms for it; the example carries no proof load in Chapter 15." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In a general linear space, we write $(x, y)$ instead of $x \\cdot y$ for inner products",
        "Note: Taking $c = 0$ in (3), we find that $(O, y) = 0$ for all $y$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In a general linear space, we write $(x, y)$ instead of $x \\cdot y$ for inner products",
        "In a general linear space, we write $(x, y)$ instead of $x \\cdot y$ for inner products, and we define the product axiomatically" ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation (x, y) for an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "内积记号 (x, y)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In a general linear space the inner product of x and y is written (x, y), in place of the dot product notation x . y used in V_n. Taking c = 0 in the homogeneity axiom gives (O, y) = 0 for all y." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-and-difference-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Notation. The negative of $x$ is denoted by $-x$ . The difference $y - x$ is defined to be the sum $y + (-x)$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Notation. The negative of $x$ is denoted by $-x$ . The difference $y - x$ is defined to be the sum $y + (-x)$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation for the negative and the difference" ;
    ex:nameZh "负元与差的记号" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "The negative of x is denoted by -x, and the difference y - x is defined to be the sum y + (-x)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-n-orthogonal-nonzero-elements-form-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If dim V = n and if S consists of n elements, Theorem 15.7(b) shows that S is a basis for V.",
        "In particular, in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If dim V = n and if S consists of n elements, Theorem 15.7(b) shows that S is a basis for V.",
        "In particular, in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V." ;
    ex:nameEn "In dimension n, n nonzero orthogonal elements form a basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "n 维空间中 n 个非零正交元素构成基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-repeat-for-each-index> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The second clause of Theorem 15.10: in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V. Apostol's proof is a two-step composition, independence from the first clause, then Theorem 15.7(b) to upgrade n independent elements in an n-dimensional space to a basis. So this clause adds no inner-product content; its whole force comes from a dimension-counting theorem proved earlier." .

<urn:studium:node:d:angle-well-defined-because-quotient-lies-in-minus-one-to-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Note: The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that the quotient on the right of (15.6) lies in the interval $[-1, 1]$ ,",
        "so there is exactly one $\\theta$ in $[0, \\pi]$ whose cosine is equal to this quotient." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Note: The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that the quotient on the right of (15.6) lies in the interval $[-1, 1]$ ,",
        "so there is exactly one $\\theta$ in $[0, \\pi]$ whose cosine is equal to this quotient." ;
    ex:nameEn "Cauchy-Schwarz is what makes the angle well defined" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 才使角有定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:angle-confined-to-the-interval-zero-to-pi>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:angle-cosine-equation>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:angle-requires-nonzero-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-in-norm-form> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's Note: the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that the quotient on the right of (15.6) lies in [-1, 1], so there is exactly one theta in [0, pi] whose cosine equals it. This is the load-bearing dependency of the whole angle definition. Formula (15.6) by itself defines nothing; it is solvable for theta only because Cauchy-Schwarz caps the quotient, and the solution is unique only because cosine is strictly decreasing on [0, pi]. Without Cauchy-Schwarz the definition would be vacuous for some pairs x, y." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axioms-replace-a-construction-with-required-properties> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Instead, we require that the operations have certain properties which we take as axioms for a linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Instead, we require that the operations have certain properties which we take as axioms for a linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms replace a construction by a list of required properties" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理取代构造:只要求运算具有若干性质" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.01" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol does not construct the two operations; he lists properties they must have and takes those as axioms. This grants a licence and imposes a restriction at once: any concrete pair of operations satisfying the ten axioms qualifies, and no argument may appeal to how the operations are computed." .

<urn:studium:node:d:basis-expansion-of-an-element-exists-by-spanning> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $x \\in V$ , we can express $x$ as a linear combination of these basis elements:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $x \\in V$ , we can express $x$ as a linear combination of these basis elements:" ;
    ex:nameEn "Existence of the expansion comes from the spanning clause" ;
    ex:nameZh "展开式的存在性来自张成条款" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "That every element of V can be written as a combination of the basis elements is the spanning half of the basis definition, nothing more. Independence plays no role in the existence of the expansion." .

<urn:studium:node:d:complex-cauchy-schwarz-reduces-to-modulus-squared> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) (y, x) = (x, y) \\overline {{(x , y)}} = | (x, y) | ^ {2}.",
        "When we apply this proof in a complex Euclidean space, we obtain the inequality $(x, y)(y, x) \\leq (x, x)(y, y)$ , which is the same as the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality since" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When we apply this proof in a complex Euclidean space, we obtain the inequality $(x, y)(y, x) \\leq (x, x)(y, y)$ , which is the same as the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality since" ;
    ex:nameEn "In a complex space (x, y)(y, x) equals the modulus squared" ;
    ex:nameZh "复空间中 (x, y)(y, x) 等于模的平方" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-proof-inherited-from-theorem-12-3>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "When the real proof is run in a complex Euclidean space it yields (x, y)(y, x) <= (x, x)(y, y). Apostol closes the gap by the identity (x, y)(y, x) = (x, y) times conjugate of (x, y) = |(x, y)|^2, which uses (1') alone. This single line is what makes one statement of Theorem 15.8 cover both the real and the complex case." .

<urn:studium:node:d:components-depend-on-the-ordering-not-only-on-the-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "are called the components of x relative to the ordered basis $(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "are called the components of x relative to the ordered basis $(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The component tuple depends on the ordering, not only on the basis set" ;
    ex:nameZh "分量组依赖于次序,而不仅依赖于基这个集合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "The permutation observation is not spelled out by Apostol; it is read off from the n-tuple notation quoted in the anchor." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:ordered-basis-adds-an-ordering-to-a-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Because the components are recorded as an n-tuple indexed in the given order, permuting the ordered basis permutes the components of every element. This is the sole reason Apostol introduces ordered bases before components." .

<urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-has-no-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(Whenever we consider this set it is understood that the zero polynomial is also included.)" ;
    ex:nameEn "The degree-exactly-n set contains no zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数恰为 n 的集合不含零元" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:zero-polynomial-is-included-by-convention> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The only candidate for O under pointwise addition is the zero polynomial, and it is excluded by the requirement of degree exactly n, so Axiom 5 has no witness and Axiom 6 loses its target. The set therefore fails not only closure, which is the reason Apostol gives, but also the additive axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-subspace-of-degree-at-most-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.4. Let S be a nonempty subset of a linear space V. Then S is a subspace if and only if S satisfies the closure axioms.",
        "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "The degree-exactly-n set is a subset but not a subspace of the degree-at-most-n space" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数恰为 n 的集合是子集但不是子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:example-7-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:subspace-satisfies-all-linear-space-axioms> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ex:section "15.03",
        "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The polynomials of degree exactly n sit inside the linear space of polynomials of degree at most n and inherit its operations, yet they are not a subspace, since a subspace must itself satisfy the closure axioms. The pair shows that being contained in a linear space and carrying its operations is not enough." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-from-one-element-being-a-scalar-multiple-of-another> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 2. If one element in S is a scalar multiple of another, then S is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 2. If one element in S is a scalar multiple of another, then S is dependent.",
        "The Pythagorean identity shows that $u_{1} + u_{2} - u_{3} = O$ , so the three functions $u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}$ are dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "A scalar multiple relation between two elements forces dependence" ;
    ex:nameZh "两元素成标量倍数关系即导致相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-coefficients-not-all-zero>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-distinctness-of-the-chosen-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Example 2: if some element of S equals a scalar times another element of S, then S is dependent. This is the cheapest sufficient condition for dependence and needs only two elements of S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-vn-is-n-via-unit-coordinate-vectors> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. The space $V_{n}$ has dimension $n$ . One basis is the set of $n$ unit coordinate vectors." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 1. The space $V_{n}$ has dimension $n$ . One basis is the set of $n$ unit coordinate vectors." ;
    ex:nameEn "The dimension of V_n is n, witnessed by the unit coordinate vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "V_n 的维数为 n,由单位坐标向量见证" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-uncited-unit-coordinate-basis-of-vn>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Example 1 computes a dimension the only way available: exhibit one basis and count it. The n unit coordinate vectors serve as the witness." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dimension-of-the-zero-space-is-zero-by-convention> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $V = \\{O\\}$ , we say V has dimension 0." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $V = \\{O\\}$ , we say V has dimension 0." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero space is assigned dimension 0 by convention" ;
    ex:nameZh "零空间按约定赋维数 0" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:zero-space-declared-finite-dimensional-by-fiat> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Since the zero space has no basis of positive size, its dimension is fixed by a separate stipulation. Under the convention that the empty set is independent and spans the zero space, this stipulation is consistent with the general definition." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dimension-theory-uses-no-metric-notions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$",
        "it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "What the chain from 15.5 to 15.7 does not use" ;
    ex:nameZh "从15.5到15.7这条链没有用到什么" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "The scoping claim over all three theorems is inferred; the anchor is Apostol's own statement of it for the 15.5 ingredient only." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-generality-transfer-uses-only-the-linear-space-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-conclusion-by-antisymmetry> ;
    ex:section "15.07",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.5, Theorem 15.6 and Theorem 15.7 use only the linear space axioms, the finiteness of the counts and the ordering of the integers. No inner product, norm, orthogonality, distance, coordinates or completeness appears, which is why all of 15.10 onward can be built on top of this layer rather than beside it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:distance-is-the-norm-of-the-difference> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm $\\|x - y\\|$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm $\\|x - y\\|$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Distance is defined as the norm of the difference" ;
    ex:nameZh "距离定义为差的范数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:distance-between-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The distance between x and y is ||x - y||. Every minimization statement in 15.14 and 15.15 is therefore a statement about norms, which is why the Pythagorean formula can settle it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dot-product-satisfies-the-inner-product-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. In $V_{n}$ let $(x, y) = x \\cdot y$ , the usual dot product of $x$ and $y$ .",
        "The reader should verify that each of the following satisfies all the axioms for an inner product." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 1. In $V_{n}$ let $(x, y) = x \\cdot y$ , the usual dot product of $x$ and $y$ .",
        "The reader should verify that each of the following satisfies all the axioms for an inner product." ;
    ex:nameEn "The dot product on V_n is an inner product (Example 1)" ;
    ex:nameZh "V_n 上的点积是一个内积(例 1)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dot-product-component-sum-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Example 1: taking (x, y) = x . y in V_n satisfies all four axioms, so V_n with the dot product is a real Euclidean space. This is the verification that the axiom system is non-vacuous and that the generalization really contains its model." .

<urn:studium:node:d:euclidean-space-unqualified-covers-real-and-complex> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "en we use the term Euclidean space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex.",
        "theorems of this chapter are valid for complex Euclidean spaces as well." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "en we use the term Euclidean space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex.",
        "theorems of this chapter are valid for complex Euclidean spaces as well." ;
    ex:nameEn "Unqualified 'Euclidean space' means real or complex" ;
    ex:nameZh "未加限定的「欧几里得空间」兼指实与复两种情形" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "A reading convention with mathematical force: whenever Apostol writes 'Euclidean space' without further designation, the space may be real or complex, and every theorem of the chapter is asserted for both. This is why Theorem 15.8, 15.9, 15.10, 15.11 and 15.12 must be read as complex statements too, and why conjugates appear in the proof of the triangle inequality and in Parseval's formula." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-1-verification-is-the-field-axioms-of-r> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. Let $V = \\mathbf{R}$ , the set of all real numbers, and let $x + y$ and $ax$ be ordinary addition and multiplication of real numbers.",
        "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 1: no axiom needs work, all ten are field facts" ;
    ex:nameZh "例1:十条公理全部化为 R 的域公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-1-the-real-numbers-form-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For V = R every one of the ten axioms is a familiar arithmetic law: Axioms 1 and 2 are closure of R under its own operations, Axioms 3, 4, 7, 8 and 9 are commutativity, associativity and distributivity of real arithmetic, and Axioms 5, 6 and 10 hold with O = 0 and 1x = x. No verification here is non-trivial, which is exactly why the example carries no information about the axioms' independence." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-10-functions-integrable-on-an-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 10. The set of all functions integrable on a given interval." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 10. The set of all functions integrable on a given interval." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 10: the functions integrable on a given interval" ;
    ex:nameZh "例10:在给定区间上可积的全体函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The functions integrable on one fixed interval form a function space, since sums and constant multiples of integrable functions are integrable. As in Examples 8 and 9 the interval or point must be fixed in advance, or the pointwise sum would be defined on a shrinking domain." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-12-nonhomogeneous-solutions-break-closure> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Here again 0 is essential. The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Here again 0 is essential. The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms.",
        "The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "The solutions of a nonhomogeneous equation break the closure axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "非齐次方程的解集破坏封闭公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:example-11-a-nonzero-prescribed-value-breaks-closure> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-12-solutions-of-a-homogeneous-second-order-equation> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol notes that here again 0 is essential and that the solution set of a nonhomogeneous equation fails the closure axioms; if Ly = r with r not identically zero, then two solutions give L(y1 + y2) = 2r and the scalar zero gives L(0) = 0, neither equal to r. The zero on the right-hand side of the equation plays the same role as the zero in Example 11." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-2-shows-the-scalars-decide-real-or-complex> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Even though the elements of V are complex numbers, this is a real linear space because the scalars are real." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Even though the elements of V are complex numbers, this is a real linear space because the scalars are real." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 2: the scalars, not the elements, decide real versus complex" ;
    ex:nameZh "例2:决定实/复的是标量而非元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-linear-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-2-the-complex-numbers-with-real-scalars> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axioms-2-7-8-9-are-the-scalar-dependent-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:real-in-real-linear-space-refers-to-the-scalars> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Because the multipliers are restricted to real numbers, C with these operations is a real linear space rather than a complex one, despite every element being a complex number. The example forbids the reading of real linear space as a claim about the elements." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-3-verification-reduces-to-arithmetic-in-each-component> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 3: every axiom reduces to one real-number identity per component" ;
    ex:nameZh "例3:每条公理逐分量归结为一条实数恒等式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-3-v-n-with-componentwise-operations> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-3-are-componentwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Since both operations act separately in each coordinate, each of the ten axioms for V_n follows from the corresponding law of real arithmetic applied n times, with O the n-tuple of zeros. No axiom requires work, so V_n cannot distinguish an axiom that is needed from one that is redundant." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-4-is-a-line-or-a-plane-through-the-origin> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If n = 2, this linear space is a line through O with N as a normal vector. If n = 3, it is a plane through O with N as normal vector." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If n = 2, this linear space is a line through O with N as a normal vector. If n = 3, it is a plane through O with N as normal vector." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 4 in low dimensions is a line or a plane through O" ;
    ex:nameZh "例4在低维时是过 O 的直线或平面" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-4-vectors-orthogonal-to-a-fixed-nonzero-vector> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For n = 2 the set of vectors orthogonal to N is a line through O with N as normal, and for n = 3 it is a plane through O with N as normal. Both pass through O necessarily, which is the geometric face of the fact that a linear space must contain a zero element." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-4-nontrivial-check-is-closure-by-linearity-of-the-dot-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y, denoted by $x + y$ .",
        "Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 4: the non-trivial check is closure, supplied by linearity of the dot product" ;
    ex:nameZh "例4的非平凡验证:由点积线性性给出的封闭性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-4-vectors-orthogonal-to-a-fixed-nonzero-vector> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Axioms 3, 4 and 7 to 10 are identities that already hold throughout V_n, hence on any subset; Axioms 5 and 6 need only that O and (-1)x again lie in V, which closure gives. So the only content is Axioms 1 and 2, and they hold because x.N = 0 and y.N = 0 give (x+y).N = 0 and (ax).N = a(x.N) = 0. The verification therefore consumes the linearity of the dot product, a fact from outside the axiom list." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-5-all-functions-on-a-given-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 5. The set of all functions defined on a given interval." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 5. The set of all functions defined on a given interval.",
        "Multiplication of a function f by a real scalar a is defined as follows: af is that function whose value at each x in the domain of f is $af(x)$ .",
        "The elements of V are real-valued functions, with addition of two functions f and g defined in the usual way:" ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 5: all functions defined on a given interval" ;
    ex:nameZh "例5:定义在给定区间上的全体函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-lives-on-the-intersection-of-domains> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all real-valued functions on one fixed interval is a function space under the pointwise operations. Fixing the interval in advance is what makes it work: the domains all coincide, so the intersection-of-domains clause never shrinks anything and closure is immediate with no condition on the functions themselves." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-6-all-polynomials-as-a-function-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 6. The set of all polynomials.",
        "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 6. The set of all polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 6: all polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "例6:全体多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all polynomials, of any degree whatever, is a function space: a sum of polynomials is a polynomial and a real multiple of a polynomial is a polynomial, with no bound to violate. Together with Example 7 it shows that an unbounded degree is harmless while a fixed exact degree is fatal." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-9-functions-differentiable-at-a-given-point> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 9. The set of all functions differentiable at a given point." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 9. The set of all functions differentiable at a given point." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 9: the functions differentiable at a given point" ;
    ex:nameZh "例9:在给定点可微的全体函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The functions differentiable at one fixed point form a function space under the pointwise operations, because the derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives and the derivative of af is a f'. Closure is again the only item, and again it is bought with a theorem of calculus rather than with the axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-delete-the-killed-term-and-recurse> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Deleting the $M$ th term from (15.2) and applying the induction hypothesis, we find that each of the remaining $n - 1$ coefficients $c_k$ is zero." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Deleting the $M$ th term from (15.2) and applying the induction hypothesis, we find that each of the remaining $n - 1$ coefficients $c_k$ is zero.",
        "we find that $c_M = 0$ . Deleting the $M$ th term from (15.2) and applying the induction hypothesis, we find that each of the remaining $n - 1$ coefficients $c_k$ is zero." ;
    ex:nameEn "Deleting the killed term and invoking the induction hypothesis" ;
    ex:nameZh "删去已被消去的项并调用归纳假设" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-base-case-n-equals-one>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-limit-argument-isolates-one-coefficient> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "With c_M shown to be zero, the M-th term drops out and the remaining relation involves n-1 exponentials with distinct exponents, so the induction hypothesis kills the remaining coefficients. This is the step that closes the recursion." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-uses-no-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:nameEn "What the exponential proof does not use" ;
    ex:nameZh "指数函数独立性证明没有用到什么" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "A negative claim about the proof, obtained by inspecting every step of Example 7 in 15.07. Not stated by Apostol." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-limit-argument-isolates-one-coefficient>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-law-used-in-the-shift> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The argument uses ordering of the reals, the exponential addition law, nonvanishing of the exponential and one limit. It uses no inner product, no norm, no orthogonality, no differentiation and no dimension count, which is why it works in the space of all real-valued functions on the line." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-two-clauses-are-logically-independent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$\\{i,j\\}$ , $\\{i,j,i+j\\}$ , $\\{O,i,-i,j,-j,i+j\\}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$\\{i,j\\}$ , $\\{i,j,i+j\\}$ , $\\{O,i,-i,j,-j,i+j\\}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The independence clause and the spanning clause are logically independent" ;
    ex:nameZh "独立性条款与张成条款彼此逻辑独立" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "The independence of the two clauses is not stated by Apostol; it is inferred from the spanning examples of 15.06 quoted in the anchor." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-independence-clause>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:spanning-set-need-not-be-independent> ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Neither clause implies the other. The set containing O, i, -i, j, -j and i+j spans V_2 without being independent, and a single nonzero vector of V_2 is independent without spanning V_2. So a basis is genuinely a conjunction of two separate requirements." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-dimensional-defined-by-existence-of-a-finite-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of O alone." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of O alone." ;
    ex:nameEn "Finite dimensionality means a finite basis exists" ;
    ex:nameZh "有限维即存在有限基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-finiteness-clause> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "V is finite dimensional if some finite basis for V exists. The definition is existential over bases, which is exactly why Theorem 15.6 is needed before a number can be attached to V." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-dimensionality-supplies-a-finite-basis-to-orthogonalize> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Every finite-dimensional linear space has a finite basis. If the space is Euclidean, we can always construct an orthogonal basis." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Every finite-dimensional linear space has a finite basis." ;
    ex:nameEn "Where 15.14 uses finite-dimensionality: it supplies a finite basis as input" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.14 用到有限维之处:提供可正交化的有限基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-works-in-infinite-dimensional-spaces> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The only role of the finite-dimensionality hypothesis in Theorem 15.14 is to guarantee a finite basis to feed into the construction. The construction itself is dimension-free, so this is the single point at which the hypothesis is consumed." .

<urn:studium:node:d:for-function-spaces-closure-is-the-only-real-content> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "For a set of functions only the closure axioms carry content" ;
    ex:nameZh "对函数集合而言只有封闭公理有实质内容" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:examples-are-asserted-to-satisfy-the-axioms-and-left-to-the-reader> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-axioms-reduce-pointwise-to-facts-about-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Since the pointwise laws hold automatically, deciding whether a given set of functions is a linear space amounts to asking whether it is closed under pointwise addition and pointwise scaling. Every success and every failure among Examples 5 to 12 is decided by that single question, and Apostol names the closure axioms in exactly the three places where a set fails." .

<urn:studium:node:d:fourier-coefficients-in-cosine-and-sine-form> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Using the formulas in (15.20), we can rewrite (15.21) in the form",
        "a _ {k} = \\frac {1}{\\pi} \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} f (x) \\cos k x d x, \\quad b _ {k} = \\frac {1}{\\pi} \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} f (x) \\sin k x d x" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Using the formulas in (15.20), we can rewrite (15.21) in the form" ;
    ex:nameEn "The same coefficients in classical a_k, b_k form" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一组系数的经典 a_k、b_k 形式" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:fourier-coefficients> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Substituting the explicit trigonometric functions (15.20) rewrites the whole projection formula (15.21) into the classical form (15.22); the classical a_k, b_k differ from the inner products (f, phi_k) by the normalizing factors of phi_k (for instance (f, phi_{2k-1}) = sqrt(pi) a_k, and the k = 0 term contributes a_0/2). No new mathematics, only a change of normalization convention." .

<urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-turns-a-basis-into-an-orthogonal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In particular, if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) The subspace spanned by $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is the same as that spanned by $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ :",
        "then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Applied to a basis, the process returns an orthogonal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "对基施行该过程即得正交基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-b-spans-agree> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "If x_1,...,x_k is a basis of a finite-dimensional Euclidean space then y_1,...,y_k is an orthogonal basis of the same space: conclusion (b) gives the spanning property and the nonzero criterion plus Theorem 15.10 gives independence." .

<urn:studium:node:d:infinite-dimensional-operational-criterion-arbitrarily-large-independent-sets> a ex:Node ;
    ex:nameEn "Operational criterion: for every n there exist n independent elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "可操作判据:对任意 n 都存在 n 个独立元" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "This positive criterion is not stated in 15.08; Apostol gives only the negative spanning form. Derived here from Theorem 15.5." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:infinite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:infinite-dimensional-operational-criterion-no-finite-spanning-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Equivalently, V is infinite dimensional when for each n one can produce n independent elements of V. If V had a basis of k elements, Theorem 15.5 would make every k+1 elements dependent, contradicting the criterion at n = k+1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-analogy-with-component-sum> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The function values $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ play the role of the components $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ , and integration takes the place of summation.",
        "This formula is analogous to Equation (15.5) which defines the dot product of two vectors in $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "This formula is analogous to Equation (15.5) which defines the dot product of two vectors in $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Function values play the role of components, integration the role of summation" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数值充当分量,积分充当求和" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dot-product-component-sum-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol states the structural analogy explicitly: the integral formula is analogous to (15.5), with f(t) and g(t) in the role of the components x_i and y_i, and integration in the role of summation. This is the bridge that justifies calling the axiomatic notion a generalization of the dot product rather than an unrelated abstraction, and it is the reason positive definiteness holds (the integral of f^2 is positive for a continuous f that is not identically zero)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:knowledge-of-one-example-guides-work-in-the-others> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Sometimes special knowledge of one particular example helps to anticipate or interpret results valid for other examples and reveals relationships which might otherwise escape notice." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "By unifying diverse examples in this way we gain a deeper insight into each.",
        "Sometimes special knowledge of one particular example helps to anticipate or interpret results valid for other examples and reveals relationships which might otherwise escape notice." ;
    ex:nameEn "Knowledge of one example guides expectations in the others" ;
    ex:nameZh "对某一例子的了解可指引其他例子" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:one-proof-from-the-axioms-serves-every-example> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol adds that familiarity with one particular example can help anticipate or interpret results valid for the rest and can reveal relationships that would otherwise escape notice. This is the traffic in the opposite direction from the one-proof-serves-all argument: the concrete case informs the abstract, rather than the abstract covering the concrete." .

<urn:studium:node:d:legendre-normalizing-constant-from-y-n-to-p-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "P _ {n} (t) = \\frac {(2 n) !}{2 ^ {n} (n !) ^ {2}} y _ {n} (t) = \\frac {1}{2 ^ {n} n !} \\frac {d ^ {n}}{d t ^ {n}} (t ^ {2} - 1) ^ {n}",
        "are known as the Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "are known as the Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "The constant that rescales y_n into the standard P_n" ;
    ex:nameZh "把 y_n 缩放成标准 P_n 的常数因子" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomials> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-volume-2-proof-of-the-legendre-closed-form>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:rescaling-preserves-orthogonality> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The polynomials usually called Legendre polynomials are P_n = ((2n)!/(2^n (n!)^2)) y_n, which also equals the Rodrigues expression. Since rescaling preserves orthogonality, P_n is orthogonal exactly when y_n is; the constant is a normalization convention, not part of the construction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-combination-coefficients-are-arbitrary-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$c_{1},\\ldots,c_{k}$ are scalars, is called a finite linear combination of elements of S" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$c_{1},\\ldots,c_{k}$ are scalars, is called a finite linear combination of elements of S" ;
    ex:nameEn "The coefficients range over all scalars, with no constraint" ;
    ex:nameZh "系数取遍全部标量,无任何限制" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "In the definition of a linear combination the c_i are unrestricted scalars; in particular they may all be zero. The contrast with the dependence definition, which forbids all-zero coefficients, is exactly one clause wide." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-combination-summands-drawn-from-s> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "where $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are all in S and $c_{1},\\ldots,c_{k}$ are scalars, is called a finite linear combination of elements of S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "where $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are all in S and $c_{1},\\ldots,c_{k}$ are scalars, is called a finite linear combination of elements of S." ;
    ex:nameEn "The elements combined must lie in S" ;
    ex:nameZh "被组合的元素必须取自 S" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The x_i are required to be elements of S, while the combination x itself lives in the ambient V. This asymmetry is what makes the span a construction on S rather than a property of S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-space-concept-permeates-algebra-geometry-analysis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "These examples and many others illustrate how the linear space concept permeates algebra, geometry, and analysis." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "These examples and many others illustrate how the linear space concept permeates algebra, geometry, and analysis." ;
    ex:nameEn "The examples run across algebra, geometry and analysis" ;
    ex:nameZh "这些例子横跨代数、几何与分析" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The twelve examples are drawn from arithmetic, from the geometry of lines and planes through the origin, and from calculus, which is Apostol's evidence that the axioms capture something common to all three. The spread of the examples, not their number, is what justifies the abstraction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-space-is-a-set-together-with-two-operations> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Briefly, a linear space is a set of elements of any kind on which certain operations (called addition and multiplication by numbers) can be performed.",
        "If we specify the set V and tell how to add its elements and how to multiply them by numbers, we get a concrete example of a linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Briefly, a linear space is a set of elements of any kind on which certain operations (called addition and multiplication by numbers) can be performed." ;
    ex:nameEn "A linear space is a set together with two operations" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间是一个集合连同两种运算" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.01",
        "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space is not a set by itself but a set V paired with a rule for adding two elements and a rule for multiplying an element by a number. Specifying V without the two operations specifies nothing, and putting different operations on the same underlying set produces a different linear space." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-space-requires-a-nonempty-underlying-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let V denote a nonempty set of objects, called elements. The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let V denote a nonempty set of objects, called elements. The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:nameEn "The underlying set is required to be nonempty" ;
    ex:nameZh "底集必须非空" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "V is declared nonempty before any axiom is stated. The clause is not idle: of the ten axioms only Axiom 5, being existential, would fail for the empty set, so without the nonemptiness declaration the exclusion of the empty set would rest entirely on that one axiom." .

<urn:studium:node:d:metric-properties-motivate-the-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In our study of $V_{n}$ , we defined lengths and angles in terms of the dot product",
        "We shall introduce first a generalization of the dot product, which we call an inner product, and then define length and angle in terms of the inner product.",
        "those properties that rely on the possibility of measuring lengths of line segments and angles between lines are called metric properti" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In our study of $V_{n}$ , we defined lengths and angles in terms of the dot product",
        "We shall introduce first a generalization of the dot product, which we call an inner product, and then define length and angle in terms of the inner product." ;
    ex:nameEn "Metric properties are the target; the inner product is the means" ;
    ex:nameZh "度量性质是目标,内积是手段" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dot-product-component-sum-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The organizing motive of 15.10: properties that rely on measuring lengths of segments and angles between lines are called metric properties; in V_n both were defined via the dot product; so to obtain them in a general linear space one first generalizes the dot product to an inner product and only then defines length and angle in terms of it. This fixes the dependency order inner product -> norm -> angle that the rest of the section follows." .

<urn:studium:node:d:nearest-point-by-dropping-a-perpendicular> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $x$ is not in $S$ , then the nearest point $s$ is obtained by dropping a perpendicular from $x$ to the plane.",
        "This simple example suggests an approach to the general approximation problem and motivates the discussion that follows." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $x$ is not in $S$ , then the nearest point $s$ is obtained by dropping a perpendicular from $x$ to the plane." ;
    ex:nameEn "Otherwise the nearest point comes from dropping a perpendicular" ;
    ex:nameZh "否则最近点由作垂线得到" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-problem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "In the V_3 picture, when x is not in the plane S the nearest point is obtained by dropping a perpendicular from x to S. This geometric case is the motivation for defining S perp and for the whole decomposition argument." .

<urn:studium:node:d:norm-properties-abc-follow-at-once-from-the-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Properties (a), (b), (c) follow at once from the axioms; only (d) needs proof" ;
    ex:nameZh "性质 (a)(b)(c) 由公理直接得出,只有 (d) 需要证明" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-statement> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's proof partitions Theorem 15.9 sharply: (a), (b), (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product, and the entire proof text is devoted to (d). This asymmetry is the informative content. (a)-(c) are one-step translations of single axioms into norm language, whereas (d) is the only property that requires an auxiliary theorem, namely Cauchy-Schwarz." .

<urn:studium:node:d:norm-vanishes-at-the-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that",
        "THEOREM 15.9. In a Euclidean space, every norm has the following properties for all elements $x$ and $y$ and all scalars $c$ :" ;
    ex:nameEn "Property (a): ||x|| = 0 if x is the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "性质 (a):x 为零元时 ||x|| = 0" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-has-zero-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.9(a): ||x|| = 0 if x = O. Note the direction: Apostol states only the implication from x = O, not the converse; the converse is the contrapositive of (b). The proof is one substitution into (O, y) = 0 with y = O, so this property depends on the homogeneity axiom (via c = 0) and not on positivity at all." .

<urn:studium:node:d:normalized-legendre-polynomials-are-y-n-over-its-norm> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The polynomials in the corresponding orthonormal sequence $\\varphi_{0}$ , $\\varphi_{1}$ , $\\varphi_{2}$ , $\\ldots$ ,",
        "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "The normalized Legendre polynomials are phi_n = y_n / ||y_n||" ;
    ex:nameZh "规范化 Legendre 多项式即 phi_n = y_n / ||y_n||" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalized-legendre-polynomials> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:normalized-element-has-norm-one> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The orthonormal sequence attached to the Legendre polynomials is obtained by the same normalization step as in the general theory, phi_n = y_n/||y_n||. Apostol writes the normalization on y_n; since P_n is a positive multiple of y_n, normalizing P_n would give the same phi_n." .

<urn:studium:node:d:normalized-trigonometric-system> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\varphi_ {0} (x) = \\frac {1}{\\sqrt {2 \\pi}}, \\quad \\varphi_ {2 n - 1} (x) = \\frac {\\cos n x}{\\sqrt {\\pi}}, \\quad \\varphi_ {2 n} (x) = \\frac {\\sin n x}{\\sqrt {\\pi}}, \\quad \\text {for} \\quad n \\geq 1." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Dividing each $u_{n}$ by its norm, we obtain an orthonormal set $\\{\\varphi_0,\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2,\\ldots \\}$ where $\\varphi_{n} = u_{n} / \\| u_{n}\\|$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The normalized trigonometric system" ;
    ex:nameZh "标准化后的三角函数系" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-orthogonal-system> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:normalizing-an-orthogonal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-system-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The explicit result of normalizing: phi_0(x) = 1/sqrt(2pi), phi_(2n-1)(x) = cos(nx)/sqrt(pi), phi_(2n)(x) = sin(nx)/sqrt(pi) for n >= 1. It is an orthonormal set in C(0, 2pi), and it is the standard system in which Parseval's formula (15.11) acquires its classical Fourier meaning." .

<urn:studium:node:d:notation-c-a-b-for-continuous-functions-on-a-b> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If the interval is $[a, b]$ , we denote this space by $C(a, b)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If the interval is $[a, b]$ , we denote this space by $C(a, b)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation C(a, b) for the continuous functions on [a, b]" ;
    ex:nameZh "记号 C(a, b):区间 [a, b] 上的连续函数空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-8-continuous-functions-on-an-interval> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "When the interval is [a, b] the space of functions continuous on it is written C(a, b). The symbol names a linear space, not a single function, and the letters a and b record the fixed interval that makes the pointwise operations well defined." .

<urn:studium:node:d:objects-already-met-that-can-be-added-and-scaled> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "First of all, the real numbers themselves are such objects. Other examples are real-valued functions, the complex numbers, infinite series, vectors in n-space, and vector-valued functions.",
        "we discuss a general mathematical concept, called a linear space, which includes all these examples and many others as special cases." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "we discuss a general mathematical concept, called a linear space, which includes all these examples and many others as special cases." ;
    ex:nameEn "Objects already met that can be added and scaled" ;
    ex:nameZh "抽象之前早已遇到的可加可数乘对象" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.01" ;
    ex:statement "Before any axiom appears, the chapter lists objects encountered earlier that can be added and multiplied by real numbers: the real numbers themselves, real-valued functions, the complex numbers, infinite series, vectors in n-space, and vector-valued functions. The abstraction is introduced as an after-the-fact common denominator of known cases, not as an invention." .

<urn:studium:node:d:only-axiom-1-states-that-the-result-is-unique> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y, denoted by $x + y$ .",
        "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Closure axioms",
        "there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y",
        "there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." ;
    ex:nameEn "Only Axiom 1 states that the resulting element is unique" ;
    ex:nameZh "只有公理1写明了运算结果唯一" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom 1 says that to each pair there corresponds a unique element of V, whereas Axiom 2 says only that to each scalar and element there corresponds an element of V, with no uniqueness clause. Both operations are single-valued in every example, but the requirement is written into the text only for addition." .

<urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-1-are-ordinary-arithmetic> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "let $x + y$ and $ax$ be ordinary addition and multiplication of real numbers." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "let $x + y$ and $ax$ be ordinary addition and multiplication of real numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "Operations of Example 1: ordinary addition and multiplication" ;
    ex:nameZh "例1的运算:普通加法与普通乘法" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-1-the-real-numbers-form-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:an-example-is-a-set-plus-two-explicit-operations> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "In Example 1 the sum x + y is the ordinary sum of two real numbers and the product ax is the ordinary product, so scalar multiplication coincides with multiplication inside the set. This is the only example in the section where the two operations are not distinguishable from each other by type." .

<urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-2-complex-addition-and-real-scaling> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "define $x + y$ to be ordinary addition of complex numbers, and define ax to be multiplication of the complex number x by the real number a." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "define $x + y$ to be ordinary addition of complex numbers, and define ax to be multiplication of the complex number x by the real number a." ;
    ex:nameEn "Operations of Example 2: complex addition, multiplication by a real number" ;
    ex:nameZh "例2的运算:复数加法与实数数乘" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-2-the-complex-numbers-with-real-scalars> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Addition in Example 2 is ordinary addition of complex numbers, and ax means the complex number x multiplied by the real number a. Multiplication by a non-real complex number is deliberately not part of the structure, even though C supports it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-4-are-inherited-from-v-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:nameEn "Operations of Example 4 are those of V_n, unchanged" ;
    ex:nameZh "例4的运算原样继承 V_n" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:subspace-inherits-the-operations-of-the-ambient-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-4-vectors-orthogonal-to-a-fixed-nonzero-vector> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-3-are-componentwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Example 4 defines no new operations: addition and scalar multiplication are the componentwise ones of V_n, restricted to the selected vectors. This is what makes the example a test of the set rather than of the operations, and it is the pattern every later subspace example follows." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-to-a-set-equals-orthogonality-to-its-span> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "An element in V is said to be orthogonal to S if it is orthogonal to every element of S.",
        "Therefore, it is orthogonal to every element in the subspace" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Therefore, it is orthogonal to every element in the subspace" ;
    ex:nameEn "Being orthogonal to S is the same as being orthogonal to L(S)" ;
    ex:nameZh "与 S 正交等价于与 L(S) 正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-to-generators-extends-to-their-span> ;
    ex:section "15.13",
        "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Since orthogonality to a family of elements extends to every linear combination of them, S perp and L(S) perp are the same set. This is the structural reason the subspace-ness of S is irrelevant: passing to the span changes nothing." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-to-the-basis-extends-to-all-of-s> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$ , which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$ , which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$ .",
        "Therefore, it is orthogonal to every element in the subspace",
        "so $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to $e_j$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality to the basis upgrades to membership in S perp" ;
    ex:nameZh "对基正交即升级为属于 S 垂直" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-to-generators-extends-to-their-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:s-perp-is-orthogonal-to-each-basis-element> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "From orthogonality to each e_j the proof concludes orthogonality to every element of S, hence s perp is in S perp. This is the same span-extension bridge used inside the Gram-Schmidt proof, applied to a basis instead of a partial list." .

<urn:studium:node:d:parseval-conjugate-comes-from-hermitian-symmetry> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\overline {{(y , x)}},\\tag{1'}",
        "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) (\\overline {{y , e _ {i}}}) \\quad \\text {(Parseval's formula)}.\\tag{15.11}" ;
    ex:nameEn "The conjugate in (15.11) comes from Hermitian symmetry, not from homogeneity" ;
    ex:nameZh "(15.11) 中的共轭来自 Hermite 对称,而非齐次性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol compresses the proof to 'using the linearity property'; the placement of the conjugate is not analyzed there. The anchors give the formula and the axiom; the attribution is supplied by this node." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:conjugate-homogeneity-in-second-argument> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:parseval-proof-pair-expansion-with-y> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Tracing the conjugate precisely: pairing (15.10) with y in the first slot gives (x, y) = sum of (x, e_i)(e_i, y), where each c_i = (x, e_i) is extracted by homogeneity in the FIRST slot and no conjugate arises. The conjugate appears only at the last step, when (e_i, y) is rewritten as the conjugate of (y, e_i) by axiom (1'), so that the formula is expressed in the components of y rather than in reversed products. So (15.11)'s conjugate is a bookkeeping consequence of Hermitian symmetry, not of conjugate-homogeneity." .

<urn:studium:node:d:parseval-norm-form> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "When x = y, Equation (15.11) reduces to (15.12).",
        "\\| x \\| ^ {2} = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} | (x, e _ {i}) | ^ {2}.\\tag{15.12}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When x = y, Equation (15.11) reduces to (15.12).",
        "\\| x \\| ^ {2} = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} | (x, e _ {i}) | ^ {2}.\\tag{15.12}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Norm form (15.12): ||x||^2 = sum of |(x, e_i)|^2" ;
    ex:nameZh "范数形式 (15.12):||x||^2 = sum |(x, e_i)|^2" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:parseval-formula-statement> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Setting x = y in (15.11) gives ||x||^2 = sum over i of |(x, e_i)|^2. Two collapses happen at once: (x, e_i) times its own conjugate becomes the squared modulus, and (x, x) becomes ||x||^2 by the definition of the norm. This is the abstract Pythagorean theorem, and specialized to the normalized trigonometric system it is the classical Parseval identity for Fourier coefficients." .

<urn:studium:node:d:parseval-requires-an-orthonormal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.11. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dimension $n$ , and assume that $S = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$ .",
        "THEOREM 15.12. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space of dimension $n$ , and assume that $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.12. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space of dimension $n$ , and assume that $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Parseval's formula needs an ORTHONORMAL basis, not merely an orthogonal one" ;
    ex:nameZh "Parseval 公式需要正交规范基,而非仅正交基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:15-11-hypotheses-finite-dimension-and-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-expansion-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.12 assumes {e_1, ..., e_n} is an ORTHONORMAL basis, a strictly stronger hypothesis than Theorem 15.11's orthogonal basis. The reason is traceable: the proof pairs Equation (15.10) with y, and (15.10) itself exists only under orthonormality. With a merely orthogonal basis the analogous formula would carry factors 1/(e_i, e_i), so the clean bilinear form of (15.11) is exactly what the unit norms purchase." .

<urn:studium:node:d:projection-along-an-element-needs-a-nonzero-direction> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element",
        "is called the projection of x along y." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O",
        "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element" ;
    ex:nameEn "The projection along y is defined only for y != O" ;
    ex:nameZh "沿 y 的投影只对 y != O 有定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-along-an-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The projection of x along y, ((x, y)/(y, y)) y, presupposes y != O so that (y, y) != 0. The hypothesis y != O in the definition is exactly the hypothesis that makes the Gram-Schmidt coefficient formula legal." .

<urn:studium:node:d:projection-does-not-depend-on-the-chosen-orthonormal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique." ;
    ex:nameEn "The projection does not depend on which orthonormal basis is used" ;
    ex:nameZh "投影不依赖所选的正交规范基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-uniqueness> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Different orthonormal bases of S give the same element s, because the uniqueness half of Theorem 15.15 pins down the S-component of x. Apostol does not remark on it, but without this the definition would be basis-dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:d:projection-on-a-subspace-is-defined-through-an-orthonormal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "is called the projection of $x$ on the subspace $S$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "is called the projection of $x$ on the subspace $S$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The projection on S is defined through an orthonormal basis of S" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间上的投影是借 S 的正交规范基定义的" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "For a finite-dimensional S with orthonormal basis e_1,...,e_n, the projection of x on S is s = sum (x, e_i) e_i. The definition is stated in terms of a chosen basis, not intrinsically." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-a-cancel-by-adding-the-negative-of-z> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Now add -z to both members to get z = O." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Now add -z to both members to get z = O." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (a), step 2: add -z to both members" ;
    ex:nameZh "(a)证明第二步:两边加 -z" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-4-associative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "From z + z = z, adding -z to both members gives z = O. The step is where the existence of a negative (Axiom 6) and the neutrality of O (Axiom 5) are spent, together with Axiom 4 to regroup; the text performs it in one line without naming these axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-b-mirrors-proof-a-with-axiom-8> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (b). Let $z = aO$ , add $z$ to itself, and use Axiom 8." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof of (b). Let $z = aO$ , add $z$ to itself, and use Axiom 8." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (b) is the proof of (a) with Axiom 8 in place of Axiom 9" ;
    ex:nameZh "(b)的证明是(a)的镜像,把公理9换成公理8" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:proof-a-set-z-equal-to-0x-and-double-it> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3b-every-scalar-annihilates-the-zero-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol gives only a one-line recipe: let z = aO, add z to itself, and use Axiom 8. The doubling then reads aO + aO = a(O + O) = aO, and the same cancellation finishes; the two proofs differ only in which distributive axiom is invoked." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-c-conclusion-rests-on-uniqueness-of-the-negative> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "so z is the negative of ax, $z = -(ax)$ . Similarly, if we add $a(-x)$ to ax and use Axiom 8 and property (b), we find that $a(-x) = -(ax)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "so z is the negative of ax, $z = -(ax)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (c) concludes through the uniqueness of the negative" ;
    ex:nameZh "(c)的收尾借助负元唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3c-negation-passes-through-scalar-multiplication> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Having shown z + ax = O, the text concludes z = -(ax). The inference from being a negative to being the negative is exactly Theorem 15.2, so this identification rests on the uniqueness result proved just above rather than on any further axiom; the second half of (c) does invoke Axiom 8 and property (b)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-c-first-half-collapses-z-plus-ax-by-axiom-9> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (c). Let $z = (-a)x$ . Adding $z$ to $ax$ and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + a x = (- a) x + a x = (- a + a) x = 0 x = O," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Adding $z$ to $ax$ and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + a x = (- a) x + a x = (- a + a) x = 0 x = O," ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (c), first half: z + ax collapses to O by Axiom 9" ;
    ex:nameZh "(c)证明前半:用公理9 把 z + ax 压成 O" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3c-negation-passes-through-scalar-multiplication> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "With z = (-a)x, Axiom 9 rewrites z + ax as (-a + a)x = 0x, which is O by part (a). So z is a negative of ax." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-c-second-half-uses-axiom-8-and-property-b> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "so z is the negative of ax, $z = -(ax)$ . Similarly, if we add $a(-x)$ to ax and use Axiom 8 and property (b), we find that $a(-x) = -(ax)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Similarly, if we add $a(-x)$ to ax and use Axiom 8 and property (b), we find that $a(-x) = -(ax)$ .",
        "use Axiom 8 and property (b), we find that $a(-x) = -(ax)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (c), second half: a(-x) = -(ax) by Axiom 8 and property (b)" ;
    ex:nameZh "(c)证明后半:用公理8 与性质(b) 得 a(-x) = -(ax)" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3c-negation-passes-through-scalar-multiplication> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3b-every-scalar-annihilates-the-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Adding a(-x) to ax gives a(-x) + ax = a((-x) + x) by Axiom 8, which is aO, and aO = O by property (b). Hence a(-x) is also the negative of ax, which closes the second equality of (c); this half needs the already proved part (b) as an input." .

<urn:studium:node:d:pythagorean-cross-terms-vanish-by-orthogonality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\| x \\| ^ {2} = \\| s \\| ^ {2} + \\| s ^ {\\perp} \\| ^ {2}.\\tag{15.17}",
        "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\| x \\| ^ {2} = (x, x) = (s + s ^ {\\perp}, s + s ^ {\\perp}) = (s, s) + (s ^ {\\perp}, s ^ {\\perp}),",
        "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal." ;
    ex:nameEn "The cross terms vanish because s and s perp are orthogonal" ;
    ex:nameZh "交叉项因 s 与 s 垂直正交而消失" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:pythagorean-expansion-of-the-squared-norm> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The two mixed terms (s, s perp) and (s perp, s) are zero precisely because s lies in S and s perp lies in S perp. Dropping them leaves ||x||^2 = ||s||^2 + ||s perp||^2." .

<urn:studium:node:d:s-lies-in-s-because-it-is-a-linear-combination-of-the-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since s is a linear combination of the basis elements, s lies in S.",
        "The definition of $s^{\\perp}$ shows that Equation (15.16) holds." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since s is a linear combination of the basis elements, s lies in S.",
        "s = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}, \\quad s ^ {\\perp} = x - s.\\tag{15.18}" ;
    ex:nameEn "s lies in S because it is a linear combination of basis elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "s 属于 S,因为它是基元的线性组合" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:s-defined-as-the-sum-of-projections-along-the-basis-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "That s belongs to S is immediate: it is a linear combination of e_1,...,e_n, which span S. The equation x = s + s perp then holds by the definition of s perp." .

<urn:studium:node:d:s-perp-closure-follows-from-linearity-in-the-first-argument> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(1) $(x,y) = (y,x)$ (commutativity, or symmetry).",
        "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one.",
        "whether or not S itself is one." ;
    ex:nameEn "S perp is closed under sums and scalar multiples by linearity of the inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "S 垂直的封闭性来自内积对第一变元的线性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:s-perp-is-defined-for-an-arbitrary-subset> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "If x and x' are both orthogonal to every element of S then so are x + x' and cx, because additivity in the second argument (axiom 2) transfers to the first through symmetry (axiom 1), while homogeneity is stated on the first argument outright (axiom 3); the subspace criterion then applies. This is the exercise Apostol leaves to the reader, and it uses nothing about S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:span-inclusion-x-inside-y> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Equation (15.13) shows that $x_{r+1}$ is the sum of two elements in $L(y_1, \\ldots, y_{r+1})$ so a similar argument gives the inclusion in the other direction:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "so a similar argument gives the inclusion in the other direction:",
        "y _ {r + 1} = x _ {r + 1} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {r} a _ {i} y _ {i},\\tag{15.13}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Second inclusion: L(x_1,...,x_{r+1}) is contained in L(y_1,...,y_{r+1})" ;
    ex:nameZh "包含关系之二:L(x_1,...,x_{r+1}) 含于 L(y_1,...,y_{r+1})" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-b-spans-agree> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-inductive-step-defines-y-r-plus-one> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Reading the defining equation the other way round exhibits x_{r+1} as a sum of two elements of L(y_1,...,y_{r+1}), which gives the reverse inclusion and completes (b). The symmetry of the argument is exactly why the same displayed equation serves both directions." .

<urn:studium:node:d:span-inclusion-y-inside-x> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The new element $y_{r + 1}$ given by (15.13) is a difference of two elements in $L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r + 1})$ so it, too, is in $L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r + 1})$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The new element $y_{r + 1}$ given by (15.13) is a difference of two elements in $L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r + 1})$ so it, too, is in $L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r + 1})$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "First inclusion: L(y_1,...,y_{r+1}) is contained in L(x_1,...,x_{r+1})" ;
    ex:nameZh "包含关系之一:L(y_1,...,y_{r+1}) 含于 L(x_1,...,x_{r+1})" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-b-spans-agree> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The first r elements y_1,...,y_r lie in L(x_1,...,x_r) by the inductive hypothesis, hence in the larger space L(x_1,...,x_{r+1}); and y_{r+1} is a difference of two elements of that space, so it lies there too. Closure of a subspace under differences is the only tool used." .

<urn:studium:node:d:span-is-a-subspace-via-the-closure-test> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V. We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V.",
        "satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V. We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S" ;
    ex:nameEn "The span is a subspace because it passes the closure test" ;
    ex:nameZh "张成集通过封闭性判别故为子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "That L(S) is a subspace is not asserted directly: the text checks the closure axioms and then invokes Theorem 15.4. So the very first application of Theorem 15.4 in the book is to the span." .

<urn:studium:node:d:span-of-the-empty-set-is-the-zero-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is empty, we define $L(S)$ to be $\\{O\\}$ , the set consisting of the zero element alone." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If S is empty, we define $L(S)$ to be $\\{O\\}$ , the set consisting of the zero element alone." ;
    ex:nameEn "Convention: the span of the empty set is the zero subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "约定:空集的张成集为零子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:zero-element-destroys-independence> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "For S empty there are no elements to combine, so L(S) is fixed by a separate stipulation to be the one-element set containing only O. Without this convention L(S) would be empty and would fail to be a subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:d:spanning-set-may-be-infinite> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The space of all polynomials is spanned by the infinite set of polynomials $\\{1, t, t^{2}, \\ldots\\}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Although the infinite set $\\{1, t, t^{2}, \\ldots\\}$ spans this space, no finite set of polynomials spans the space.",
        "The space of all polynomials is spanned by the infinite set of polynomials $\\{1, t, t^{2}, \\ldots\\}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "A spanning set may be infinite" ;
    ex:nameZh "张成集可以是无限集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:infinite-dimensional-operational-criterion-no-finite-spanning-set> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:linear-combination-finiteness-of-the-index-set> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The space of all polynomials is spanned by the infinite set of powers of t. Nothing in the definition of span forces S to be finite, even though each individual combination uses only finitely many terms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:strict-minimality-rests-on-positivity-of-the-norm> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:nameEn "Strictness comes from positivity of the norm" ;
    ex:nameZh "严格性来自范数的正定性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:equality-holds-exactly-when-t-equals-s> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The step from ||s - t||^2 = 0 to s = t is exactly Theorem 15.9(b): the norm of a nonzero element is positive. Without positivity the theorem would only give a non-strict minimum." .

<urn:studium:node:d:subspace-nonempty-requirement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Given a linear space V, let S be a nonempty subset of V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Given a linear space V, let S be a nonempty subset of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Nonemptiness requirement in the definition of a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间定义中的非空性要求" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "A subspace is defined only for a nonempty subset S of V. Nonemptiness is a separate condition from closure: it is what later supplies a witness element x from which the zero element and negatives are manufactured." .

<urn:studium:node:d:theorem-15-9-holds-for-every-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.9. In a Euclidean space, every norm has the following properties for all elements $x$ and $y$ and all scalars $c$ :" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since it may be possible to define an inner product in many different ways, the norm of an element will depend on the choice of inner product.",
        "THEOREM 15.9. In a Euclidean space, every norm has the following properties for all elements $x$ and $y$ and all scalars $c$ :" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.9 quantifies over all elements, all scalars, and every norm" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.9 对一切元素、一切标量、每个范数成立" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-not-unique-on-a-given-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The framing clause of Theorem 15.9: in a Euclidean space, EVERY norm has properties (a)-(d) for ALL elements x and y and ALL scalars c. Combined with the fact that a space may carry several inner products, this is the exact content of the theorem's significance: (a)-(d) are the properties that survive any choice of inner product, and they are therefore the right axioms for a norm in the abstract." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-1-two-candidate-zeros-swap-roles-in-axiom-5> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that",
        "Taking $x = O_1$ and $O = O_2$ in Axiom 5, we obtain $O_1 + O_2 = O_1$ . Similarly, taking $x = O_2$ and $O = O_1$ , we find $O_2 + O_1 = O_2$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Axiom 5 tells us that there is at least one zero element.",
        "But $O_1 + O_2 = O_2 + O_1$ because of the commutative law, so $O_1 = O_2$ .",
        "Taking $x = O_1$ and $O = O_2$ in Axiom 5, we obtain $O_1 + O_2 = O_1$ . Similarly, taking $x = O_2$ and $O = O_1$ , we find $O_2 + O_1 = O_2$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Each candidate zero is fed into Axiom 5 in both roles" ;
    ex:nameZh "两个候选零元各自轮流充当 Axiom 5 的 x 与 O" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-zero-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "The step that closes the proof of Theorem 15.1 is a double substitution into Axiom 5 with the roles exchanged: taking x = O_1 and O = O_2 gives O_1 + O_2 = O_1, and taking x = O_2 and O = O_1 gives O_2 + O_1 = O_2. The move is available because a zero element is itself an element of V, so each candidate can occupy the element slot of the axiom as well as the zero slot." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-2-left-neutrality-needs-the-commutative-law> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Adding $y_{2}$ to both members of the first equation and using Axioms 5, 4, and 3, we find that",
        "x + O = x \\quad f o r a l l x i n V.",
        "y _ {2} + (x + y _ {1}) = (y _ {2} + x) + y _ {1} = O + y _ {1} = y _ {1} + O = y _ {1}." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Adding $y_{2}$ to both members of the first equation and using Axioms 5, 4, and 3, we find that",
        "x + O = x \\quad f o r a l l x i n V." ;
    ex:nameEn "The second evaluation needs O + y = y, which Axiom 5 does not state" ;
    ex:nameZh "第二次求值用到 O + y = y,而 Axiom 5 并未直接给出这一形式" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-2-same-sum-evaluated-in-two-groupings> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-states-the-zero-only-as-a-right-neutral-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "In the regrouped evaluation the term O + y_1 appears, but Axiom 5 asserts neutrality only in the form x + O = x, with the zero on the right. Apostol therefore writes the extra link O + y_1 = y_1 + O before reducing to y_1, and this is why the proof cites Axiom 3 alongside Axioms 5 and 4." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3-only-a-b-c-are-proved-in-the-text> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We shall prove (a), (b), and (c) and leave the proofs of the other properties as exercises." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "We shall prove (a), (b), and (c) and leave the proofs of the other properties as exercises." ;
    ex:nameEn "Only parts (a), (b), (c) are proved in the text" ;
    ex:nameZh "书中只证明了 (a)(b)(c) 三条" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3d-a-vanishing-product-forces-a-vanishing-factor>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3e-a-nonzero-scalar-may-be-cancelled>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3f-a-nonzero-element-may-be-cancelled>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3g-the-negative-of-a-sum>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3h-repeated-addition-is-an-integer-multiple> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol proves (a), (b) and (c) and leaves (d) through (h) as exercises. The axiom attributions the text itself certifies are therefore only those appearing in the proofs of (a), (b) and (c)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3-quantifiers-arbitrary-elements-and-arbitrary-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.3. In a given linear space, let $x$ and $y$ denote arbitrary elements and let $a$ and $b$ denote arbitrary scalars." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.3. In a given linear space, let $x$ and $y$ denote arbitrary elements and let $a$ and $b$ denote arbitrary scalars." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3 quantifies over arbitrary elements and arbitrary scalars" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3 的量词:任意元素与任意标量" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "The eight properties are asserted for arbitrary elements x, y of a given linear space and arbitrary scalars a, b, with no finiteness, dimension or metric hypothesis. Consequently every property holds verbatim in each of the twelve examples of Section 15.3 and in the complex case as well." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-identities-inherited-from-the-ambient-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "laws for addition (Axioms 3 and 4) and the axioms for multiplication by scalars (Axioms 7 through 10) are automatically satisfied in S because they hold for all elements of V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "the axioms for multiplication by scalars (Axioms 7 through 10) are automatically satisfied in S because they hold for all elements of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms 3, 4 and 7 through 10 are inherited because they are identities holding for all of V" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理3、4与7至10因对 V 的全部元素成立而自动继承" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-sufficiency-direction> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The commutative and associative laws and the four scalar multiplication axioms are universally quantified identities. Any subset automatically satisfies them, so no property of S is used here beyond S being a subset of V." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-negative-obtained-by-taking-the-scalar-minus-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Taking $a = -1$ , we see that $(-1)x$ is in $S$ . But $x + (-1)x = O$ since both $x$ and $(-1)x$ are in $V$ , so Axiom 6 is satisfied in $S$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But $x + (-1)x = O$ since both $x$ and $(-1)x$ are in $V$ , so Axiom 6 is satisfied in $S$ .",
        "Taking $a = -1$ , we see that $(-1)x$ is in $S$ .",
        "Taking $a = -1$ , we see that $(-1)x$ is in $S$ . But $x + (-1)x = O$ since both $x$ and $(-1)x$ are in $V$ , so Axiom 6 is satisfied in $S$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Taking a = -1 puts the negative of x into S" ;
    ex:nameZh "取 a = -1 把 x 的负元放进 S" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-remaining-obligations-zero-and-negative> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-closure-under-scalar-multiplication-is-the-active-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-witness-element-supplied-by-nonemptiness> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Closure with a = -1 gives (-1)x in S, and the identity x + (-1)x = O, which holds already in V, shows (-1)x is a negative of x. Hence Axiom 6 holds in S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-conclusion-any-k-plus-one-elements-of-the-span-are-dependent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "and let $L(S)$ be the subspace spanned by S. Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "and let $L(S)$ be the subspace spanned by S. Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.5 conclusion: every set of k+1 elements of L(S) is dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.5的结论:L(S) 中任意 k+1 个元素相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The conclusion is universally quantified over all sets of k+1 elements drawn from the span, not from V. Note that the k+1 elements need not lie in S; they need only be expressible as finite combinations of members of S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-hypothesis-independent-set-of-k-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let S be an independent set consisting of k elements in a linear space V" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let S be an independent set consisting of k elements in a linear space V" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.5 hypothesis: S is independent with exactly k elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.5的前提:S 独立且恰有 k 个元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The hypothesis fixes a count k and requires S to be independent. Independence is what makes k the sharp threshold: it is the reason the theorem speaks of k+1 rather than of some smaller number of elements." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-hypothesis-finite-dimensional-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.6 hypothesis: V is finite dimensional" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.6的前提:V 是有限维的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The equal-cardinality theorem is stated only for finite-dimensional V and compares only finite bases. It says nothing about infinite bases, which Apostol never introduces in this chapter." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7-proofs-delegated-to-theorem-12-10> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The proof of (a) is identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10. The proof of (b) is identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The proof of (a) is identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10. The proof of (b) is identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Both parts of Theorem 15.7 are proved by delegation to Theorem 12.10" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.7两部分的证明都委派给定理12.10" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7a-independent-sets-extend-to-bases>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-n-independent-elements-form-a-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol reproduces no argument: part (a) is said to be identical to part (b) of Theorem 12.10 and part (b) identical to part (c). So within Chapter 15 the proof of 15.7 is empty, and the only chapter-internal ingredient available is Theorem 15.5." .

<urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-equality-sufficient-conditions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The equality sign holds in (d) if $x = O$ , if $y = O$ , or if $y = cx$ for some $c > 0$ .",
        "\\| x + y \\| = \\| x + c x \\| = (1 + c) \\| x \\| = \\| x \\| + \\| c x \\| = \\| x \\| + \\| y \\|." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The equality sign holds in (d) if $x = O$ , if $y = O$ , or if $y = cx$ for some $c > 0$ .",
        "\\| x + y \\| = \\| x + c x \\| = (1 + c) \\| x \\| = \\| x \\| + \\| c x \\| = \\| x \\| + \\| y \\|." ;
    ex:nameEn "Sufficient conditions for equality in the triangle inequality" ;
    ex:nameZh "三角不等式取等号的充分条件" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-equality-condition> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-homogeneity-property> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol states that equality holds in (d) if x = O, if y = O, or if y = cx for some c > 0, and verifies the last case by the computation ||x + y|| = ||x + cx|| = (1 + c)||x|| = ||x|| + ||cx|| = ||x|| + ||y||. Read the quantifier carefully: these are given as SUFFICIENT conditions only. Unlike the equality clause of Theorem 15.8, this is not an if-and-only-if in the text, and the positivity requirement c > 0 (not merely c nonzero) is what distinguishes it from the dependence condition in Cauchy-Schwarz." .

<urn:studium:node:d:trivial-case-when-x-already-lies-in-s> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Given x in V, the problem is to find, in the plane S, that point s nearest to x.",
        "If $x \\in S$ , then clearly $s = x$ is the solution." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $x \\in S$ , then clearly $s = x$ is the solution." ;
    ex:nameEn "If x is already in S the solution is x itself" ;
    ex:nameZh "若 x 已在 S 中则解就是 x 本身" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-problem> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "When x belongs to S the nearest element is s = x, giving distance zero. Apostol notes this case first in the V_3 illustration." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-concludes-by-self-orthogonality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself, we must have $s - t = O$ .",
        "This shows that the decomposition is unique." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$s - t$ is both orthogonal to $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ and equal to $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ .",
        "Since the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself, we must have $s - t = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The conclusion s - t = O rests on self-orthogonality" ;
    ex:nameZh "由自正交推出 s - t = O" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-uniqueness> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:the-difference-lies-in-both-s-and-s-perp>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-is-the-only-element-orthogonal-to-itself> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Because the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself, the element found above must be O, giving s = t and then s perp = t perp. This one clause is where the whole uniqueness argument is discharged." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-of-components-conclusion-tuple-determined-by-x> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The coefficients in this equation determine an n-tuple of numbers $(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{n})$ that is uniquely determined by x." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The coefficients in this equation determine an n-tuple of numbers $(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{n})$ that is uniquely determined by x." ;
    ex:nameEn "Conclusion: the coefficient tuple is uniquely determined by x" ;
    ex:nameZh "结论:系数组由 x 唯一确定" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:uniqueness-of-components> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-of-components-apply-independence> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The two tuples being equal, the expansion coefficients form an n-tuple determined by x alone once the ordered basis is fixed. This is what licenses treating components as a function of x." .

<urn:studium:node:d:unqualified-linear-space-may-be-real-or-complex> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Although we shall deal primarily with examples of real linear spaces, all the theorems are valid for complex linear spaces as well.",
        "When we use the term linear space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When we use the term linear space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex." ;
    ex:nameEn "An unqualified linear space may be real or complex" ;
    ex:nameZh "未加限定的「线性空间」可实可复" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "When the text writes linear space with no qualifier, either the real or the complex case is meant. Every theorem of the chapter is asserted for both, so no proof may silently assume that the scalars are real." .

<urn:studium:node:d:vanishing-projection-terms-in-the-legendre-computation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\left(y _ {0}, y _ {0}\\right) = \\int_ {- 1} ^ {1} d t = 2 \\quad \\text { and } \\quad \\left(x _ {1}, y _ {0}\\right) = \\int_ {- 1} ^ {1} t d t = 0,",
        "y _ {1} (t) = x _ {1} (t) - \\frac {(x _ {1} , y _ {0})}{(y _ {0} , y _ {0})} y _ {0} (t) = x _ {1} (t) = t." ;
    ex:nameEn "Odd integrands make several projection terms vanish" ;
    ex:nameZh "奇次被积函数使若干投影项为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomials> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "In the computation of y_1 the projection coefficient is (x_1, y_0)/(y_0, y_0) with (x_1, y_0) = integral of t over [-1, 1] = 0, so nothing is subtracted and y_1(t) = t. Integration of an odd power over the symmetric interval is what kills these terms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:vector-space-and-linear-vector-space-are-aliases> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Sometimes a linear space is referred to as a linear vector space or simply a vector space; the numbers used as multipliers are also called scalars." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Sometimes a linear space is referred to as a linear vector space or simply a vector space; the numbers used as multipliers are also called scalars." ;
    ex:nameEn "Vector space and linear vector space are alternative names" ;
    ex:nameZh "vector space 与 linear vector space 是同名异称" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The same object is called a linear space, a linear vector space, or simply a vector space, and the numbers used as multipliers are called scalars. No mathematical distinction is intended by the choice of name." .

<urn:studium:node:d:z-r-is-orthogonal-to-itself> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "By property (a), both $y_{r+1}'$ and $c_{r+1}y_{r+1}$ are orthogonal to $z_{r}$ . Therefore, their difference, $z_{r}$ , is orthogonal to $z_{r}$ .",
        "In other words, $z_{r}$ is orthogonal to itself, so $z_{r} = O$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "By property (a), both $y_{r+1}'$ and $c_{r+1}y_{r+1}$ are orthogonal to $z_{r}$ .",
        "In other words, $z_{r}$ is orthogonal to itself, so $z_{r} = O$ .",
        "where $z_{r} \\in L(y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{r})$ . We wish to prove that $z_{r} = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "z_r comes out orthogonal to itself" ;
    ex:nameZh "z_r 被证明与自身正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-a-orthogonal-to-earlier-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-splits-the-competitor-into-span-plus-multiple>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-is-the-only-element-orthogonal-to-itself> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Property (a), applied to both y'_{r+1} and to c_{r+1} y_{r+1}, makes each of them orthogonal to z_r; their difference is z_r itself, so z_r is orthogonal to z_r. This is the step that hands the argument over to the self-orthogonality fact." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:bases-for-matrix-and-function-spaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Matrix spaces The vector space M contains all 2 by 2 matrices. Its dimension is 4.",
        "That solution space for $y ^ { \\prime \\prime } = - y$ has two basis functions: sin x and cos x.",
        "The words \"independence\" and \"basis\" and \"dimension\" are not at all restricted to column vectors." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The words \"independence\" and \"basis\" and \"dimension\" are not at all restricted to column vectors.",
        "you haven't got the ideas of basis and dimension straight until you can apply them to \"vectors\" other than column vectors." ;
    ex:nameEn "Bases for matrix spaces and function spaces" ;
    ex:nameZh "矩阵空间与函数空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Independence, basis and dimension are not restricted to column vectors. The space of all 2 by 2 matrices has dimension 4 and the n by n matrix space has dimension n^2; the solution space of y'' = -y has the two basis functions sin x and cos x, so its dimension is 2." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:basis-by-elimination> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Eliminate to find the pivot columns (of A not R). Those pivot columns are a basis for the column space.",
        "First answer Make them the rows of A, and eliminate to find the nonzero rows of R.",
        "Question Given five vectors in ${ \\bf R } ^ { 7 }$ , how do you find a basis for the space they span?" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Eliminate to find the pivot columns (of A not R). Those pivot columns are a basis for the column space.",
        "First answer Make them the rows of A, and eliminate to find the nonzero rows of R.",
        "Question Given five vectors in ${ \\bf R } ^ { 7 }$ , how do you find a basis for the space they span?",
        "The pivot rows of A are a basis for its row space. So are the pivot rows of its echelon form R." ;
    ex:nameEn "Finding a basis by elimination" ;
    ex:nameZh "用消元法求基" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:pivot-columns-basis-for-column-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:pivot-rows-basis-for-row-space> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "To find a basis for the space spanned by given vectors: either make them the rows of A and eliminate, keeping the nonzero rows of R, or put them in the columns of A and eliminate, keeping the pivot columns of A (not of R)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:column-space-projection-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection matrix $\\overset { \\cdot } { P } = \\overset { \\cdot } { A } ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A ) ^ { - 1 } A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ gives $p = P b$",
        "The projection matrix is $P = A ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A ) ^ { - 1 } A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Find the vector ${ \\widehat x } ,$ find the projection $p = A { \\widehat { x } } ,$ find the projection matrix $P \\cdot$",
        "The new formulas contain $( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A ) ^ { - 1 }$ instead of $\\boldsymbol { \\mathrm { 1 / } a ^ { \\mathrm { T } } a }$",
        "The next formula picks out the projection matrix that is multiplying bin (6):",
        "This matrix satisfies $P ^ { 2 } = P$",
        "has $P ^ { \\mathrm { T } } = P$ and $P ^ { 2 } = P$ and $P b = p$" ;
    ex:nameEn "projection matrix P = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T" ;
    ex:nameZh "列空间投影矩阵 P = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:rank-one-projection-matrix> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-matrix-symmetric-idempotent> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-matrix>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-subspace-formula> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection matrix onto the column space C(A) is P = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T; it satisfies p = Pb." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:error-in-left-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "That nullspace of $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ contains the error vector $e = \\pmb { b } - A \\widehat { \\pmb { x } }$ .",
        "The error vector $\\pmb { b } - A \\widehat { \\pmb { x } }$ is perpendicular to that column space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The n equations are exactly $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } ( \\pmb { b } - A \\pmb { \\widehat { x } } ) = \\mathbf { 0 }$",
        "contains the error vector $e = \\pmb { b } - A \\widehat { \\pmb { x } }$" ;
    ex:nameEn "error is orthogonal to the subspace / in N(A^T)" ;
    ex:nameZh "误差与子空间正交 / 在 A^T 的零空间中" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:error-perpendicular-to-a> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:normal-equation> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:error-vector> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The error vector b - A x-hat is perpendicular to the column space of A, hence lies in the nullspace of A^T (the left nullspace)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:full-column-rank-criterion> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Full column rank The columns of A are independent exactly when the rank is $r = n .$ There are n pivots and no free variables.",
        "The rank is only $r = 2$ . Independent columns produce full column rank $r = n = 3$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Full column rank The columns of A are independent exactly when the rank is $r = n .$ There are n pivots and no free variables.",
        "The columns might be dependent or might be independent if $n \\leq m$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Independent columns exactly when r = n" ;
    ex:nameZh "列无关当且仅当秩等于 n" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:short-wide-matrix-has-dependent-columns> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:independent-columns> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The columns of A are independent exactly when the rank is r = n: there are n pivots and no free variables, so only x = 0 is in the nullspace. Independent columns produce full column rank." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:fundamental-theorem-of-linear-algebra-part-1> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra, Part 1",
        "The column space and row space both have dimension r.",
        "The nullspaces have dimensions n - r and m - r." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Part 1 of the Fundamental Theorem finds the dimensions of the four subspaces. One fact stands out: The row space and column space have the same dimension r.",
        "The column space and row space both have dimension r.",
        "The counting rule for A was $r + ( n - r ) = n$",
        "The dimension of a subspace is the number of vectors in a basis.",
        "The nullspaces have dimensions n - r and m - r." ;
    ex:nameEn "Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra, Part 1" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性代数基本定理第一部分" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:rank-theorem> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:counting-theorem>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:four-fundamental-subspaces>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:left-nullspace> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "For an m by n matrix of rank r: the column space and row space both have dimension r, the nullspace has dimension n - r and the left nullspace has dimension m - r. The rank of A therefore fixes the dimensions of all four fundamental subspaces." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:particular-solution> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Notice how we choose the free variables (as zero) and solve for the pivot variables.",
        "This particular solution is my favorite: free variables = zero, pivot variables from d. The method always works." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Figure 3.3: Complete solution = one particular solution + all nullspace solutions." ;
    ex:nameEn "Particular solution (free variables zero)" ;
    ex:nameZh "特解(自由变量取零)" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:complete-solution> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:reduce-augmented-to-rd> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "To find one particular solution x_p to Ax = b, set all free variables to zero and solve for the pivot variables, which then come directly from d." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:rank-one-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\"The column rank equals the row rank which is l.\"",
        "Every rank one matrix is one column times one row $A = u v ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$",
        "On the left is a matrix with three rows. But its row space only has dimension $= ~ 1$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\"The column rank equals the row rank which is l.\"",
        "On the left is a matrix with three rows. But its row space only has dimension $= ~ 1$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Rank one matrix A = uv^T" ;
    ex:nameZh "秩一矩阵" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:strang:rank-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "Every rank one matrix is one column times one row, A = uv^T: every row is a multiple of v^T, which is a basis for the one-dimensional row space, and every column is a multiple of u, which is a basis for the column space." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:row-combination-consistency> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If a combination of the rows ( on the left side) gives the zero row, then the same combination must give zero on the right side.",
        "If the equations are consistent, this must be true on the right side of the equations also!" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If a combination of the rows ( on the left side) gives the zero row, then the same combination must give zero on the right side." ;
    ex:nameEn "Row-combination consistency for solvability" ;
    ex:nameZh "行组合一致性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:solvability-condition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:reduce-augmented-to-rd> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "If a combination of the rows of A gives the zero row, then the same combination of the entries of b must give zero; otherwise the equations are inconsistent and b is not in the column space." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:row-space-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Instead of the rows of A, look at the columns of $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ . Same numbers, but now in the column space",
        "The row space of A is $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ . It is the column space of" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The row space of A is $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ . It is the column space of" ;
    ex:nameEn "C(A^T) for the row space" ;
    ex:nameZh "行空间记号 C(A^T)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "3.4",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The row space of A is written C(A^T), the column space of A transpose: transposing turns the rows of A into columns, so the same numbers now span a subspace of R^n. The left nullspace is written N(A^T)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:row-space-to-column-space-invertible> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Every vector b in the column space comes from one and only one vector",
        "From the row space to the column space, $A$ is invertible." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Every vector b in the column space comes from one and only one vector",
        "This diference must be the zero vector, because the nullspace and row space are perpendicular." ;
    ex:nameEn "A is invertible from row space to column space" ;
    ex:nameZh "A 在行空间到列空间上可逆" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:vector-splitting-row-null>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:zero-vector-in-two-orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Every vector b in the column space comes from one and only one vector x_r in the row space. From the row space to the column space, A acts as an invertible (r by r) map." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:square-invertible-system> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Question Suppose A is a square invertible matrix,",
        "The particular solution is the one and only solution" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Cases 1 and 2 have full row rank",
        "Cases 1 and 3 have full column rank" ;
    ex:nameEn "Square invertible case r = m = n" ;
    ex:nameZh "方阵可逆情形 r = m = n" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:strang:full-column-rank>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:full-row-rank>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:four-possibilities-by-rank> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "When A is square and invertible (r = m = n), R = I has no zero rows, the nullspace is only the zero vector, and x_p = A^{-1}b is the one and only solution." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:standard-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The columns of the 3 by 3 identity matrix are the standard basis $i , j ,$ , k.",
        "The columns of the n by n identity matrix give the \"standard basis\" for $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The columns of the n by n identity matrix give the \"standard basis\" for $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Standard basis for R^n" ;
    ex:nameZh "R^n 的标准基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The columns of the n by n identity matrix give the standard basis for R^n: i and j in R^2, and i, j, k in R^3. They are independent and they span the space." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:unique-combination-in-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A basis consists of linearly independent vectors that span the space. Every vector in the space is a unique combination of the basis vectors.",
        "Every vector v in the space is a combination of the basis vectors, because they span the space.",
        "There is one and only one way to write v as a combination of the basis vectors." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Every vector v in the space is a combination of the basis vectors, because they span the space.",
        "the combination that produces v is unique, because the basis vectors" ;
    ex:nameEn "Unique representation in a basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "基表示的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:unique-coordinates-in-a-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Every vector v in the space is a combination of the basis vectors because they span the space, and there is one and only one such combination because the basis vectors are independent." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:best-linear-approximation-to-sine> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since $(f, \\varphi_2) = 0$ , this is also the nearest quadratic approximation.",
        "Therefore the linear polynomial $f_{1}(t)$ which is nearest to $\\sin \\pi t$ on $[-1, 1]$ is",
        "f _ {1} (t) = \\sqrt {\\frac {3}{2}} \\frac {2}{\\pi} \\varphi_ {1} (t) = \\frac {3}{\\pi} t." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(f, \\varphi_ {k}) = \\int_ {- 1} ^ {1} \\sin \\pi t \\varphi_ {k} (t) d t.",
        "For example, when $f(x) = \\sin \\pi x$ , the coefficients $(f,\\varphi_k)$ are given by",
        "This is the polynomial of degree $\\leq n$ for which the norm $\\| f - f_n\\|$ is smallest." ;
    ex:nameEn "Nearest linear polynomial to sin(pi t) on [-1, 1]" ;
    ex:nameZh "[-1, 1] 上距 sin(pi t) 最近的线性多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomial-approximation> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Worked numerical application instance inside Example 2: for f(x) = sin(pi x) the Legendre coefficients satisfy (f, phi_0) = 0 and (f, phi_1) = sqrt(3/2) * 2/pi, so the nearest linear polynomial is f_1(t) = (3/pi) t; since (f, phi_2) = 0 this is also the nearest quadratic approximation." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-and-product-of-integrals-on-polynomials> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $(f,g) = \\left|\\int_0^1 f(t)g(t)dt\\right|.$",
        "\\text {(d)} (f, g) = \\left(\\int_ {0} ^ {1} f (t) d t\\right) \\left(\\int_ {0} ^ {1} g (t) d t\\right)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "12. In the linear space of all real polynomials, determine whether or not $(f,g)$ is an inner product if $(f,g)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:nameEn "Two more failures on polynomials: an absolute value and a product of integrals" ;
    ex:nameZh "多项式空间上的另两个失败例:绝对值与两积分之积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-of-the-dot-product-breaks-homogeneity>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 12 offers two further failures on the space of all real polynomials. Taking the absolute value of the integral of f g preserves symmetry and positivity but destroys homogeneity for negative scalars and additivity. Taking the product of the separate integrals of f and of g is symmetric and bilinear but degenerate, since f(t) = t - 1/2 is nonzero with vanishing integral. The two failures are complementary: the first keeps the sign but loses linearity, the second keeps linearity but loses the sign." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-in-second-slot-breaks-symmetry> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} | y _ {i} |.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} | y _ {i} |." ;
    ex:nameEn "Counterexample: sum x_i |y_i| is not an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "反例:sum x_i |y_i| 不是内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The formula (x, y) = sum x_i |y_i| on V_n is not an inner product. Putting the absolute value on only the second factor destroys symmetry, since (x, y) and (y, x) differ already for n = 1 with x = -1, y = 1. It also destroys positivity: for x = (-1, 0, ..., 0) one gets (x, x) = -1 < 0, so axiom 4 fails too." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-in-the-scalar-product-destroys-the-identity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (|x_{1} + x_{2}|,|y_{1} + y_{2}|),\\qquad a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (|ax_{1}|,|ax_{2}|).$",
        "If the set is not a linear space, indicate which axioms are violated." ;
    ex:nameEn "An absolute value in the scalar product destroys the identity scalar" ;
    ex:nameZh "数乘取绝对值后单位标量失效" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses x = (-1, 0), x = (1, 0) with a = b = 1 and the computed values (1, 0), (2, 0), (1, 1) are the model's." ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "On the set of ordered pairs of real numbers with a(x_1, x_2) = (|a x_1|, |a x_2|), Axiom 10 fails at x = (-1, 0): 1x = (1, 0), not (-1, 0). Axiom 9 fails at a = b = 1 and x = (1, 0) under the addition of Exercise 31(d), where (a + b)x = (2, 0) but ax + bx = (1, 0) + (1, 0) = (1, 1). No scalar multiple ever has a negative coordinate, so no element outside the closed first quadrant is a scalar multiple of anything, and the failure is independent of which addition it is paired with." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-addition-that-discards-the-second-coordinate-has-no-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $(x_{1},x_{2}) + (y_{1},y_{2}) = (x_{1} + y_{1},0),$ $a(x_{1},x_{2}) = (ax_{1},ax_{2}).$",
        "If the set is not a linear space, indicate which axioms are violated." ;
    ex:nameEn "An addition that discards the second coordinate leaves the set with no zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "丢掉第二分量的加法使零元不存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness x = (0, 1), the scalars a = b = 1, the value (0, 2) and the survey of the surviving axioms are the model's." ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "On the set of ordered pairs of real numbers with (x_1, x_2) + (y_1, y_2) = (x_1 + y_1, 0) and the usual scaling, Axiom 5 fails: every sum has second coordinate 0, so no candidate O can satisfy x + O = x for x = (0, 1). Axiom 9 fails at a = b = 1 and x = (0, 1), where (a + b)x = (0, 2) but ax + bx = (0, 1) + (0, 1) = (0, 0). Axioms 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8 and 10 all hold, so the entire defect sits in the addition." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-best-constant-is-the-mean-value> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "let $f(x) = 1 / x$ and show that the constant polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ is $g = \\frac{1}{2} \\log 3$ . Compute $\\| g - f \\|^2$ for this $g$ .",
        "let $f(x) = e^x$ and show that the constant polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ is $g = \\frac{1}{2}(e^2 - 1)$ . Compute $\\| g - f \\|^2$ for this $g$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "let $f(x) = 1 / x$ and show that the constant polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ is $g = \\frac{1}{2} \\log 3$ . Compute $\\| g - f \\|^2$ for this $g$ .",
        "let $f(x) = e^x$ and show that the constant polynomial $g$ nearest to $f$ is $g = \\frac{1}{2}(e^2 - 1)$ . Compute $\\| g - f \\|^2$ for this $g$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The nearest constant is the mean value, not the midrange" ;
    ex:nameZh "最近的常数是均值,而不是最大最小值的中点" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-in-a-finite-dimensional-subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "In Exercises 6 and 7 the approximating subspace is the one-dimensional space of constants, and the answers are (log 3)/2 for 1/x on (1, 3) and (e^2 - 1)/2 for e^x on (0, 2). Both are the average value of f over the interval, which is what the projection formula yields when the orthonormal basis of the subspace is the single normalized constant. Minimizing the integrated square error therefore gives the mean, not the midpoint between the maximum and minimum values of f." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-cosine-family-is-orthogonal-only-because-of-the-half-period> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "form an orthonormal set spanning the same subspace as $x_0, x_1, x_2, \\ldots$ .",
        "with inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t)y(t)dt$ , let $x_n(t) = \\cos nt$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "form an orthonormal set spanning the same subspace as $x_0, x_1, x_2, \\ldots$ .",
        "with inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t)y(t)dt$ , let $x_n(t) = \\cos nt$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "On C(0, pi) the cosines are orthogonal but sine is not orthogonal to the constant" ;
    ex:nameZh "在 C(0, pi) 上余弦族正交,但正弦与常数不正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-orthogonal-system> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthogonality-depends-on-the-interval>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 3 orthonormalizes cos nt for n = 0, 1, 2, ... on the interval (0, pi), where the family is already orthogonal. The full trigonometric system of 15.11 needs the whole period (0, 2pi): on (0, pi) the integral of sin t against the constant 1 equals 2, so sin t is not orthogonal to the constant there. Which trigonometric functions form an orthogonal system is decided by the interval of integration." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-cross-terms-do-not-collapse-to-twice-the-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) $\\| x + y\\|^2 = \\| x\\|^2 + \\| y\\|^2 + (x, y) + (y, x)$ .",
        "16. Prove that the following identities are valid in every Euclidean space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) $\\| x + y\\|^2 = \\| x\\|^2 + \\| y\\|^2 + (x, y) + (y, x)$ .",
        "16. Prove that the following identities are valid in every Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "In a Euclidean space the expansion keeps (x, y) and (y, x) as separate terms" ;
    ex:nameZh "欧氏空间中展开式必须保留 (x, y) 与 (y, x) 两项" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:cross-term-sum-is-real>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-expand-norm-squared> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 16 asserts that ||x + y||^2 = ||x||^2 + ||y||^2 + (x, y) + (y, x) in every Euclidean space, and deliberately does not write the cross terms as 2(x, y). In the complex case the two terms are conjugates, so their sum is twice the real part of (x, y) and generally not 2(x, y); only Hermitian symmetry, not homogeneity, licenses the pairing of the two terms." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-increasing-functions-fail-under-negative-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "6. All step functions defined on [0, 1]. 11. All increasing functions.",
        "In Exercises 7 through 12, each domain contains all real numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "The increasing functions fail the scalar axioms at the scalar -1" ;
    ex:nameZh "递增函数集在标量 -1 处失去封闭性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witness f(x) = x and the diagnosis of Axioms 2 and 6 are supplied by the model." ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "The set of increasing real-valued functions on the real line, with the usual operations, is closed under addition but not under multiplication by scalars, so Axiom 2 fails: f(x) = x is increasing while (-1)f, that is -x, is decreasing and therefore outside the set. Axiom 6 fails on the same element, because the element (-1)f that Axiom 6 names does not exist in the set at all. The set is closed under multiplication by nonnegative scalars only, which is exactly the defect that separates a convex cone from a linear space." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-isotropic-element-in-a-mixed-sign-plane> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In the space spanned by $\\{x, y\\}$ , find an element $z \\neq O$ with $(z, z) = 0$ .]" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In the space spanned by $\\{x, y\\}$ , find an element $z \\neq O$ with $(z, z) = 0$ .]" ;
    ex:nameEn "A nonzero self-orthogonal element in a plane of mixed sign" ;
    ex:nameZh "混合符号平面中的非零自正交元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:positivity-axiom-forbids-nonzero-self-orthogonal-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The hint to Exercise 2 constructs the obstruction: if (x, x) > 0 and (y, y) < 0 for nonzero x, y, then the two-dimensional space spanned by {x, y} contains a nonzero z with (z, z) = 0. Such an isotropic element is exactly what axiom 4 outlaws, so a genuine inner product can never take values of both signs on the diagonal." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-l2-sequence-space-is-an-infinite-dimensional-euclidean-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) Prove that $V$ is a linear space with $(x, y)$ as an inner product.",
        "(x, y) = \\sum_ {n = 1} ^ {\\infty} x _ {n} y _ {n}.",
        "13. Let $V$ consist of all infinite sequences $\\{x_{n}\\}$ of real numbers for which the series $\\sum x_{n}^{2}$ converges." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) Prove that $V$ is a linear space with $(x, y)$ as an inner product.",
        "(x, y) = \\sum_ {n = 1} ^ {\\infty} x _ {n} y _ {n}.",
        "[Hint: Use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to estimate the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{M}|x_n y_n|.$ ]" ;
    ex:nameEn "The space of square-summable sequences as an infinite-dimensional Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "平方可和序列空间作为无限维欧几里得空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:infinite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-convergence-must-be-proved-before-the-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.11",
        "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 13 takes V to be the set of real sequences {x_n} for which the series of squares converges, with (x, y) the sum of x_n y_n. It is a Euclidean space of infinite dimension, so the inner-product and norm machinery of 15.10 (Cauchy-Schwarz, the norm, the triangle inequality, the angle) and Theorem 15.10 of 15.11 apply with no finite-dimension hypothesis, while Theorems 15.11 and 15.12 of that section do assume finite dimension. The square-summability restriction is what makes the pairing finite; on the space of all real sequences the same formula diverges, for instance when every x_n = y_n = 1." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthonormalizing-a-dependent-list-yields-a-shorter-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "1. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{3}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "x _ {1} = (1, 1, 0, 1), \\quad x _ {2} = (1, 0, 2, 1), \\quad x _ {3} = (1, 2, - 2, 1).",
        "x _ {1} = (1, 1, 1), \\quad x _ {2} = (1, 0, 1), \\quad x _ {3} = (3, 2, 3)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "1. In each case, find an orthonormal basis for the subspace of $V_{3}$ spanned by the given vectors.",
        "x _ {1} = (1, 1, 0, 1), \\quad x _ {2} = (1, 0, 2, 1), \\quad x _ {3} = (1, 2, - 2, 1).",
        "x _ {1} = (1, 1, 1), \\quad x _ {2} = (1, 0, 1), \\quad x _ {3} = (3, 2, 3)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Every list in Exercises 1 and 2 is dependent, so the orthonormal basis is shorter than the list" ;
    ex:nameZh "习题1、2中每个向量组都是相依的,故正交规范基比原组更短" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:vanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> ;
    ex:section "15.16" ;
    ex:statement "The instruction is to orthonormalize the subspace spanned by the given vectors, and in each part the given list is dependent: in 1(a) x_3 = 2x_1 + x_2, in 1(b) x_3 lies in the plane of x_1 and x_2, in 2(a) x_1 - x_2 + x_3 - x_4 = O, and in 2(b) x_3 = 2x_1 - x_2. The Gram-Schmidt process returns O at the dependent step, so the resulting orthonormal basis has fewer elements than the input list. The exercises are a systematic drill in that detection, not in the arithmetic of independent input." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-polarization-formula-is-twice-the-dot-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x _ {i} + y _ {i}) ^ {2} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} ^ {2} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} y _ {i} ^ {2}.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x _ {i} + y _ {i}) ^ {2} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} ^ {2} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} y _ {i} ^ {2}." ;
    ex:nameEn "An unfamiliar-looking formula that is in fact an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "看起来陌生但确实是内积的公式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-of-the-dot-product-breaks-homogeneity> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The candidate (x, y) = sum (x_i + y_i)^2 - sum x_i^2 - sum y_i^2 looks like none of the standard formulas, yet expanding the square collapses it to 2 sum x_i y_i, twice the dot product, and a positive multiple of an inner product is again an inner product. It is the counterintuitive member of Exercise 1: the answer is yes, and the axioms must be checked after simplification, not before." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-sampling-inner-product-on-p-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) Prove that $(f,g)$ is an inner product for $P_{n}$ .",
        "(f, g) = \\sum_ {k = 0} ^ {n} f \\left(\\frac {k}{n}\\right) g \\left(\\frac {k}{n}\\right).",
        "10. In the linear space $P_{n}$ of all real polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , define" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) Prove that $(f,g)$ is an inner product for $P_{n}$ .",
        "10. In the linear space $P_{n}$ of all real polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , define" ;
    ex:nameEn "A finite sum of point evaluations is an inner product on P_n but not on all polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "有限个点值之和在 P_n 上是内积,在全体多项式空间上则不是" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n-is-n-plus-one> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "Exercise 10 defines (f, g) as the sum over k = 0, ..., n of f(k/n) g(k/n) on the space P_n of polynomials of degree at most n. Positivity holds precisely because a nonzero polynomial of degree at most n cannot vanish at all n + 1 sample points. On the space of all real polynomials the same formula is degenerate: the product of the n + 1 factors (t - k/n) is nonzero yet has vanishing self-pairing. Whether the formula is an inner product depends on the dimension of the space it is placed on." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-single-point-evaluation-is-degenerate> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "12. In the linear space of all real polynomials, determine whether or not $(f,g)$ is an inner product if $(f,g)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "12. In the linear space of all real polynomials, determine whether or not $(f,g)$ is an inner product if $(f,g)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:nameEn "Degenerate pairing: evaluation at one point" ;
    ex:nameZh "退化配对:只在一点取值" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-sampling-inner-product-on-p-n> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "On the space of all real polynomials, (f, g) = f(1) g(1) satisfies symmetry, additivity and homogeneity but fails positivity: f(t) = t - 1 is nonzero with (f, f) = 0. It is the extreme case of the sampling formula of Exercise 10 with a single node, and it shows that one evaluation point can never separate a space of dimension greater than one." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-union-of-two-coordinate-planes-fails-additive-closure> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "23. All vectors $(x, y, z)$ in $V_{3}$ with $x = 0$ or $y = 0$ .",
        "For those that are not, tell which axioms fail to hold." ;
    ex:nameEn "A union of two coordinate planes fails only the additive closure axiom" ;
    ex:nameZh "两个坐标平面的并只在加法封闭性上失效" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:originNote "The witnesses (0, 1, 0), (1, 0, 0) and their sum (1, 1, 0) are supplied by the model." ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.05" ;
    ex:statement "The set of vectors (x, y, z) in V_3 with x = 0 or y = 0 is a union of two planes through the origin and fails Axiom 1: (0, 1, 0) and (1, 0, 0) both belong to the set, but their sum (1, 1, 0) has neither coordinate equal to 0. Axioms 2, 5 and 6 all hold, since the set contains the origin and is closed under every scalar multiple, so this is the case in which closure under addition is the single failing axiom. The inclusive or is what does the damage: replacing it by and gives the z-axis, which is a subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:dependence-inherited-by-supersets> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. If a subset T of a set S is dependent, then S itself is dependent.",
        "This is logically equivalent to the statement that every subset of an independent set is independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 1. If a subset T of a set S is dependent, then S itself is dependent.",
        "If a subset T of a set S is dependent, then S itself is dependent. This is logically equivalent to the statement that every subset of an independent set is independent.",
        "every subset of an independent set is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "A set containing a dependent subset is dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "含相依子集的集合是相依的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:independence-universal-quantifier-over-choices> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "EXAMPLE 1: if a subset T of a set S is dependent, then S itself is dependent. Apostol notes this is logically equivalent to the statement that every subset of an independent set is independent." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-uncited-dimension-of-a-second-order-de-solution-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. The space of solutions of the differential equation $y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0$ has dimension 2.",
        "One basis consists of the two functions $u_1(x) = e^{-x}$ , $u_2(x) = e^{3x}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The uncited fact that a second-order homogeneous linear equation has a two-dimensional solution space" ;
    ex:nameZh "未标注出处的事实:二阶齐次线性方程的解空间是二维的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "锚点是第15章的断言处,不是引用语 —— 第15章在此处没有引用语,它没有指向任何外部出处。把该事实判为外部的是我们的推断(第15章内确实没有证明它),不是第15章的说法。因此本节点标 origin: model,并且刻意不指名任何第12章或第8章的定理编号:我们无原文可查,指名即为编造归属。" ;
    ex:section "15.03",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A borrowing with no citation attached. 15.08 Example 3 asserts that the solution space of y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0 has dimension 2, with basis e^{-x} and e^{3x}, and that every solution is a combination of these two. Chapter 15 proves none of this: it verifies neither that the two functions span the solution set nor that no third independent solution exists, and it names no chapter, section or theorem as the source. The independence of the two exponentials is available inside Chapter 15 (15.07 Example 7), but the spanning half is not." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-uncited-unit-coordinate-basis-of-vn> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. The space $V_{n}$ has dimension $n$ . One basis is the set of $n$ unit coordinate vectors." ;
    ex:nameEn "The uncited fact that the n unit coordinate vectors form a basis of V_n" ;
    ex:nameZh "未标注出处的事实:n 个单位坐标向量构成 V_n 的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "锚点是第15章的断言处,不是引用语 —— 第15章在此处未给任何出处。判其为外部借用是我们的推断(第15章内无证明),并且我们不指名第12章的任何定理编号:无原文可查,指名即为编造归属。" ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A borrowing with no citation attached. 15.08 Example 1 asserts that V_n has dimension n and that one basis is the set of n unit coordinate vectors. Chapter 15 verifies neither the independence nor the spanning of that set, and names no source. This is the only place where Chapter 15's abstract notion of dimension is tied to a concrete number, and the tie rests on credit taken from outside the chapter." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:norm-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "When the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is expressed in terms of norms, it becomes" ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation ||x|| for the norm" ;
    ex:nameZh "范数记号 ||x||" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The double-bar symbol ||x|| denotes the norm of the element x, defined by ||x|| = (x, x)^(1/2). Inequalities such as |(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y|| are stated in this notation." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-exactly-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "For example, the sum of two polynomials of degree n need not have degree n.",
        "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "Set of polynomials of degree equal to n" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数恰为 n 的多项式集合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space, because the closure axioms are not satisfied: the sum of two polynomials of degree n need not have degree n." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:trigonometric-polynomial> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The $2n + 1$ elements $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\ldots, \\varphi_{2n}$ span a subspace $S$ of dimension $2n + 1$ . The elements of $S$ are called trigonometric polynomials." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In Section 15.11 we exhibited an orthonormal set of trigonometric functions $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\varphi_2, \\ldots$ , where",
        "Let $V = C(0, 2\\pi)$ , the linear space of all real functions continuous on the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$",
        "span a subspace $S$ of dimension $2n + 1$ . The elements of $S$ are called trigonometric polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "Trigonometric polynomial" ;
    ex:nameZh "三角多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "An element of the (2n + 1)-dimensional subspace S of C(0, 2pi) spanned by the orthonormal trigonometric functions phi_0, phi_1, ..., phi_{2n} of (15.20); concretely a finite combination (1/2)a_0 + sum_{k=1}^{n} (a_k cos kx + b_k sin kx)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:trigonometric-polynomial-approximation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. Approximation of continuous functions on $[0, 2\\pi]$ by trigonometric polynomials.",
        "The approximation theorem tells us that the trigonometric polynomial in (15.22) approximates f better than any other trigonometric polynomial in S",
        "define an inner product by the equation $(f, g) = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In Section 15.11 we exhibited an orthonormal set of trigonometric functions $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\varphi_2, \\ldots$ , where",
        "Let $V = C(0, 2\\pi)$ , the linear space of all real functions continuous on the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$",
        "The $2n + 1$ elements $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\ldots, \\varphi_{2n}$ span a subspace $S$ of dimension $2n + 1$ . The elements of $S$ are called trigonometric polynomials.",
        "The approximation theorem tells us that the trigonometric polynomial in (15.22) approximates f better than any other trigonometric polynomial in S",
        "The numbers $(f, \\varphi_k)$ are called Fourier coefficients of $f$ .",
        "define an inner product by the equation $(f, g) = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ .",
        "span a subspace $S$ of dimension $2n + 1$ . The elements of $S$ are called trigonometric polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "Approximation of continuous functions on [0, 2pi] by trigonometric polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "用三角多项式逼近 [0, 2pi] 上的连续函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomial-approximation> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:fourier-coefficients>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:trigonometric-polynomial> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Application instance (Example 1) of the Approximation Theorem: in V = C(0, 2pi) with (f, g) = integral_0^{2pi} f g, the projection f_n = sum_{k=0}^{2n} (f, phi_k) phi_k on the span of the orthonormal trigonometric set is the trigonometric polynomial of order n making ||f - f_n|| as small as possible. Rewritten with (15.20) it is the truncated Fourier series (15.22)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-divide-by-nonzero-self-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "But $(x_{1}, x_{1}) \\neq 0$ since $x_{1} \\neq O$ so $c_{1} = 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But $(x_{1}, x_{1}) \\neq 0$ since $x_{1} \\neq O$ so $c_{1} = 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 3: divide by (x_1, x_1), which is nonzero because x_1 is nonzero" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第三步:除以 (x_1, x_1),它非零是因为 x_1 非零" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-nonzero-hypothesis-is-essential>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-pair-with-a-single-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "From c_1 (x_1, x_1) = 0 Apostol concludes c_1 = 0 because (x_1, x_1) is not 0, and (x_1, x_1) is not 0 precisely because x_1 is not O. This is the unique point in the proof where the nonzero hypothesis is spent, and it is spent by invoking the positivity axiom. On a positive semi-definite form this step fails and the theorem fails with it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-pair-with-a-single-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Taking the dot product of each member with $x_{1}$ and using the fact that $(x_{1}, x_{i}) = 0$ if $i \\neq 1$ , we find that $c_{1}(x_{1}, x_{1}) = 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Taking the dot product of each member with $x_{1}$ and using the fact that $(x_{1}, x_{i}) = 0$ if $i \\neq 1$ , we find that $c_{1}(x_{1}, x_{1}) = 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 2: pair the vanishing combination with x_1 and kill all but one term" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第二步:与 x_1 作内积,只留下一项" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-setup-vanishing-finite-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:additivity-in-first-argument-derived>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-set-condition-on-distinct-pairs-only>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-has-zero-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Pairing each member with x_1 and using (x_1, x_i) = 0 for i not 1 leaves c_1 (x_1, x_1) = 0. Three separate facts are consumed in this one line: additivity, to split the sum inside the inner product; homogeneity, to extract each c_i; and the right-hand side (O, x_1) = 0, itself the c = 0 corollary of homogeneity. Note also that whichever slot x_1 is placed in, one of the two needed rules is NOT an axiom but a consequence of an axiom plus symmetry, since Apostol postulates additivity only in the second slot and homogeneity only in the first." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-repeat-for-each-index> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Repeating the argument with $x_{1}$ replaced by $x_{j}$ , we find that each $c_{j} = 0$ . This proves that S is independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Repeating the argument with $x_{1}$ replaced by $x_{j}$ , we find that each $c_{j} = 0$ . This proves that S is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 4: repeat with x_j in place of x_1, so every coefficient vanishes" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第四步:把 x_1 换成 x_j 重复,得每个系数为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-divide-by-nonzero-self-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Repeating the argument with x_1 replaced by x_j gives each c_j = 0, which proves S independent. The repetition is legitimate because the previous two steps used nothing specific about the index 1, only that x_j belongs to the orthogonal set and is nonzero. This symmetry-in-the-index observation is what turns a single-coefficient conclusion into independence." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-proof-setup-vanishing-finite-combination> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof. Let S be an orthogonal set of nonzero elements in V, and suppose some finite linear combination of elements of S is zero, say",
        "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Let S be an orthogonal set of nonzero elements in V, and suppose some finite linear combination of elements of S is zero, say" ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 1: suppose some finite linear combination is zero" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第一步:设某个有限线性组合为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The proof opens by taking S orthogonal with nonzero elements and supposing some FINITE linear combination of elements of S is zero, sum over i from 1 to k of c_i x_i = O. Finiteness is essential to the argument's shape rather than a restriction on S: S itself may be infinite, as the trigonometric system is, and independence of an infinite set is by definition a statement about its finite subsets." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-11-denominator-nonzero-by-positivity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O",
        "since $(e_i, e_j) = 0$ if $i \\neq j$ . This implies (15.8), and when $(e_j, e_j) = 1$ , we obtain (15.9)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dividing by (e_j, e_j) is legitimate because basis elements are nonzero" ;
    ex:nameZh "能除以 (e_j, e_j) 是因为基元素非零" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol writes only 'This implies (15.8)'; the nonvanishing of the denominator is left implicit. The anchors give his sentence and the positivity axiom it silently invokes." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element-forces-dependence>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Passing from c_j (e_j, e_j) = (x, e_j) to formula (15.8) requires (e_j, e_j) nonzero. Apostol does not remark on it, but the justification is a two-link chain: a basis element cannot be O, since a set containing O is dependent; and a nonzero element has (e_j, e_j) > 0 by the positivity axiom. So (15.8) rests on strict positive definiteness even though no inequality appears in its statement." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-11-proof-pair-with-basis-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, e _ {j}) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} c _ {i} (e _ {i}, e _ {j}) = c _ {j} (e _ {j}, e _ {j})",
        "Proof. Taking the inner product of each member of (15.7) with $e_{j}$ , we obtain" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Taking the inner product of each member of (15.7) with $e_{j}$ , we obtain",
        "since $(e_i, e_j) = 0$ if $i \\neq j$ . This implies (15.8), and when $(e_j, e_j) = 1$ , we obtain (15.9)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step: pair (15.7) with e_j and collapse the sum" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明步骤:与 e_j 作内积,把和式压缩为一项" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:15-11-hypotheses-finite-dimension-and-orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-set-condition-on-distinct-pairs-only> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Taking the inner product of each member of (15.7) with e_j gives (x, e_j) = sum over i of c_i (e_i, e_j) = c_j (e_j, e_j), since (e_i, e_j) = 0 for i not j. This is structurally the same move as step 2 of the proof of Theorem 15.10, applied to a representation of x rather than to a vanishing combination, and it likewise consumes additivity to split the sum and homogeneity to extract the c_i." .

<urn:studium:node:d:adjoining-an-element-outside-the-span-preserves-independence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:nameEn "Lemma: adjoining an element outside the span keeps a set independent" ;
    ex:nameZh "引理:添加张成集之外的元素保持独立性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Not stated in 15.06 through 15.08. Recorded as model-origin because it is the missing link that the delegation to Theorem 12.10 conceals." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-argument-adjoining-an-outside-element-breaks-the-count> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:independence-vanishing-combination-forces-vanishing-coefficients> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "If a set is independent and y is not in its span, then the set enlarged by y is still independent, because in any vanishing relation the coefficient of y must be zero, else y would be a combination of the others. This lemma is the engine of both the extension in part (a) and the contradiction in part (b)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:angle-confined-to-the-interval-zero-to-pi> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "in the interval $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ which satisfies the equation" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "in the interval $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ which satisfies the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "The angle is confined to [0, pi] by stipulation, not derived" ;
    ex:nameZh "角被规定落在 [0, pi] 内,而非推导所得" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:angle-cosine-equation> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The definition does not merely solve cos(theta) = (x, y) / (||x|| ||y||); it requires theta to be the solution lying in 0 <= theta <= pi. The interval is part of the stipulation, and it is doing work: over all of R, or even over [0, 2pi], the equation has more than one solution (theta and 2pi - theta share a cosine), so without the restriction the definition would not name a single number. That the restricted equation has exactly one solution is a separate fact, carried by d:angle-well-defined-because-quotient-lies-in-minus-one-to-one." .

<urn:studium:node:d:approximation-proof-starts-from-the-orthogonal-decomposition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "By Theorem 15.15 we can write $x = s + s^{\\perp}$ , where $s \\in S$ and $s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$ .",
        "Let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space V, and let x be any element of V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "By Theorem 15.15 we can write $x = s + s^{\\perp}$ , where $s \\in S$ and $s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$ .",
        "That is, if s is the projection of x on S, we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "The proof starts by invoking Theorem 15.15" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明以引用定理15.15 开始" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:approximation-inequality-with-equality-only-at-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-dimensionality-supplies-a-finite-orthonormal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:projection-is-the-s-component-of-the-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The first move is to write x = s + s perp with s in S and s perp in S perp, citing Theorem 15.15. This citation is one channel through which the finite-dimensionality hypothesis reaches Theorem 15.16; the other is the statement's own phrase 'the projection of x on S', since that notion is defined in 15.14 only for a subspace with a finite orthonormal basis." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-is-derivable-from-apostols-form-of-axiom-6> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x.",
        "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 10 is derivable from Apostol's form of Axiom 6" ;
    ex:nameZh "在 Apostol 版公理6 之下公理10 可推出" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol does not remark on this; the derivation below is not in the text. Because Axiom 6 names (-1)x as a negative of x, Axiom 10 is not independent of the rest: Axiom 9 gives 1x + (-1)x = (1 + (-1))x = 0x, which is O by Theorem 15.3(a), while Axiom 6 gives x + (-1)x = O, so adding a negative of (-1)x to both relations yields 1x = x using only Axioms 3, 4, 5, 6 and 9. Under the more common existential form of the inverse axiom this derivation is unavailable and Axiom 10 is genuinely independent." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-names-the-negative-as-minus-one-times-x> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property",
        "Therefore $y_{1} = y_{2}$ , so $x$ has exactly one negative, the element $(-1)x$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property",
        "Therefore $y_{1} = y_{2}$ , so $x$ has exactly one negative, the element $(-1)x$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 6 names the negative instead of merely asserting one exists" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理6指名 (-1)x 而非仅断言负元存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol does not postulate that some y with x + y = O exists; he asserts that the specific element (-1)x has that property. The negative is therefore tied to the scalar -1 from the outset, so no space can have an additive inverse unrelated to scalar multiplication, and Theorem 15.2 can end by identifying the unique negative as (-1)x." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axioms-2-7-8-9-are-the-scalar-dependent-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8, and 9, the resulting structure is called a complex linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8, and 9, the resulting structure is called a complex linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms 2, 7, 8, 9 are the four axioms that mention the scalars" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理2、7、8、9 是提到标量的那四条" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-7-associative-law-for-multiplication-by-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "Exactly four of the ten axioms mention real numbers, namely Axioms 2, 7, 8 and 9, and replacing real number by complex number in those four converts the definition into that of a complex linear space. The remaining six axioms name no scalar other than the fixed numbers -1 and 1 (in Axioms 6 and 10), which lie in both fields, and are therefore untouched by the change of scalar field." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-equality-condition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Moreover, the equality sign holds if and only if x and y are dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Moreover, the equality sign holds if and only if x and y are dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Equality in Cauchy-Schwarz holds if and only if x and y are dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 取等号当且仅当 x 与 y 相依" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The second clause of Theorem 15.8, and the only biconditional in the section: |(x, y)|^2 = (x, x)(y, y) exactly when the set {x, y} is dependent. For nonzero x and y dependence means y = cx, and then the angle between them is 0 if c > 0 and pi if c < 0, so the cosine attains an endpoint of [-1, 1]; if x = O or y = O the set is dependent and equality holds, but the pair has no angle at all under the definition of 15.10. It is here, and only here, that STRICT positivity is required: semi-definiteness would still give the inequality, but with (z, z) = 0 possible for z not O one could have equality without x - ty = O, so dependence could not be concluded." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-for-integrals> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE. Applying Theorem 15.8 to the space $C(a, b)$ with the inner product $(f, g) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(t) g(t) dt$ , we find that the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality becomes",
        "\\left(\\int_ {a} ^ {b} f (t) g (t) d t\\right) ^ {2} \\leq \\left(\\int_ {a} ^ {b} f ^ {2} (t) d t\\right) \\left(\\int_ {a} ^ {b} g ^ {2} (t) d t\\right)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE. Applying Theorem 15.8 to the space $C(a, b)$ with the inner product $(f, g) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(t) g(t) dt$ , we find that the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality becomes" ;
    ex:nameEn "Cauchy-Schwarz applied to C(a, b) gives the integral inequality" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 用于 C(a, b) 得到积分不等式" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-inequality-statement>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Instantiating Theorem 15.8 in C(a, b) with the integral inner product yields (integral of fg)^2 <= (integral of f^2)(integral of g^2). Apostol offers it as the pay-off of the axiomatic route: a nontrivial analytic inequality obtained with no new work, purely by checking that the integral form satisfies the axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-proof-inherited-from-theorem-12-3> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore, the very same proof is valid in any real Euclidean space.",
        "the proof was a consequence of the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2 and did not depend on the particular definition used to deduce these properties" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Therefore, the very same proof is valid in any real Euclidean space.",
        "the proof was a consequence of the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2 and did not depend on the particular definition used to deduce these properties" ;
    ex:nameEn "The proof is inherited: it used only the properties in Theorem 12.2" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明是继承而来的:它只用到定理 12.2 所列性质" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-3>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-axiomatic-not-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's proof strategy for 15.8 is a re-use argument, not a new computation. In proving the V_n case (Theorem 12.3) he was careful that the proof drew only on the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2, never on the component formula. Because those listed properties are precisely the inner-product axioms, the identical proof runs in any real Euclidean space. This is the paradigm of the whole section: axiomatize the properties actually used, and every theorem transfers for free." .

<urn:studium:node:d:coefficient-that-cancels-the-projection-component> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $y_{j}\\neq O$ , we can make $y_{r + 1}$ orthogonal to $y_{j}$ by taking",
        "a _ {j} = \\frac {(x _ {r + 1} , y _ {j})}{(y _ {j} , y _ {j})}.\\tag{15.14}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O",
        "(y _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) = (x _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {r} a _ {i} (y _ {i}, y _ {j}) = (x _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) - a _ {j} (y _ {j}, y _ {j}),",
        "If $y_{j} = O$ , then $y_{r+1}$ is orthogonal to $y_{j}$ for any choice of $a_{j}$ , and in this case we choose $a_{j} = 0$ .",
        "If $y_{j}\\neq O$ , we can make $y_{r + 1}$ orthogonal to $y_{j}$ by taking" ;
    ex:nameEn "The coefficient a_j = (x_{r+1}, y_j)/(y_j, y_j)" ;
    ex:nameZh "使新元与 y_j 正交的系数 a_j = (x_{r+1}, y_j)/(y_j, y_j)" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:zero-earlier-element-makes-the-coefficient-irrelevant> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-inductive-step-defines-y-r-plus-one> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-of-the-new-element-with-an-earlier-one> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "When y_j != O the denominator (y_j, y_j) is nonzero, so setting a_j = (x_{r+1}, y_j)/(y_j, y_j) makes the collapsed expression vanish and hence makes y_{r+1} orthogonal to y_j. Solving that one linear equation is the whole content of the step." .

<urn:studium:node:d:complex-homogeneity-allows-complex-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number." ;
    ex:nameEn "In the complex case the homogeneity scalar may be any complex number" ;
    ex:nameZh "复情形下齐次性公理中的标量可取任意复数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The second modification Apostol makes: in axiom (3) the multiplier c is allowed to be any complex number, not merely a real one. This widening is what turns the replacement of (1) by (1') into a chain reaction, because it is the combination 'complex c in the first slot' plus 'conjugation on swapping slots' that produces conjugate-homogeneity in the second slot." .

<urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-fails-axiom-1-with-an-explicit-witness> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "For example, the sum of two polynomials of degree n need not have degree n." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "For example, the sum of two polynomials of degree n need not have degree n." ;
    ex:nameEn "Failure of Axiom 1: a sum of two degree-n polynomials can drop degree" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理1失效:两个 n 次多项式之和次数可以下降" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's reason is that the sum of two polynomials of degree n need not have degree n; a witness is p(t) = t^n and q(t) = -t^n + t, both of degree n, whose sum is t, of degree 1. Since the sum leaves the set, Axiom 1 fails, and it fails even though the set is perfectly closed under multiplication by nonzero scalars." .

<urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-fails-axiom-2-at-the-scalar-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "Failure of Axiom 2: the scalar zero sends every member out of the set" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理2失效:标量 0 把任何成员送出集合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-fails-axiom-1-with-an-explicit-witness> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For p of degree exactly n, the product 0p is the zero polynomial, which has no degree n and so lies outside the set; hence Axiom 2 fails for the single scalar a = 0. This failure is more radical than the additive one because it has no witness-dependent escape: it occurs for every member of the set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-definition-not-restricted-to-finite-sets> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets." ;
    ex:nameEn "The definition applies to infinite sets as well" ;
    ex:nameZh "定义不限于有限集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-finiteness-of-the-witnessing-family> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "For finite S the definition agrees with the earlier one for V_n, but here S itself may be infinite while each witnessing relation stays finite. This separation of two finiteness questions is what lets Example 6 speak of an infinite independent set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-distinctness-of-the-chosen-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "if there is a finite set of distinct elements in S, say $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "if there is a finite set of distinct elements in S, say $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$" ;
    ex:nameEn "The chosen elements must be distinct" ;
    ex:nameZh "所选元素必须互不相同" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The witnessing elements x_1 through x_k are required to be distinct. Drop this and every nonempty set becomes dependent: take x_1 = x_2 = x with c_1 = 1, c_2 = -1, giving a nonzero coefficient tuple summing to O." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-target-is-the-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O." ;
    ex:nameEn "The linear combination must equal the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性组合必须等于零元" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The witnessing combination is required to equal O, the zero element of V. Replace O by an arbitrary target y and the condition stops describing dependence and instead describes membership of y in the span of S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-witness-must-be-exhibited-not-merely-counted> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The Pythagorean identity shows that $u_{1} + u_{2} - u_{3} = O$ , so the three functions $u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}$ are dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The Pythagorean identity shows that $u_{1} + u_{2} - u_{3} = O$ , so the three functions $u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}$ are dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dependence is witnessed by one explicit relation" ;
    ex:nameZh "相关性由一条显式关系见证" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-of-a-function-space-is-a-pointwise-identity> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Example 5 shows the practical shape of a dependence proof: exhibit a single identity with nonzero coefficients. The Pythagorean identity gives cos squared plus sin squared minus 1 equal to O, so those three functions are dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-a-second-order-de-solution-space-is-two> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "One basis consists of the two functions $u_1(x) = e^{-x}$ , $u_2(x) = e^{3x}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "One basis consists of the two functions $u_1(x) = e^{-x}$ , $u_2(x) = e^{3x}$ .",
        "The space of solutions of the differential equation $y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0$ has dimension 2." ;
    ex:nameEn "The solution space of y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0 has dimension 2 (Example 3)" ;
    ex:nameZh "例3:方程 y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0 的解空间维数为 2" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-uncited-dimension-of-a-second-order-de-solution-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:homogeneous-de-solution-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-hypothesis-distinct-exponents> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Example 3 gives dimension 2 for the solution space of the single equation y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, one basis being u_1(x) = e^{-x}, u_2(x) = e^{3x}. Apostol proves nothing here about second-order homogeneous equations in general. Verifying that this pair is independent is exactly Example 7 of 15.07 with the two distinct exponents -1 and 3." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dimension-defined-as-the-element-count-of-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dimension is defined as the number of elements in a basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "维数定义为某个基的元素个数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The definition attaches to V the integer n whenever V has a basis of n elements. As written, the integer is read off from one chosen basis, so the definition is a priori a property of the pair (space, basis)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dimension-well-definedness-requires-all-bases-to-be-equinumerous> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements.",
        "Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:nameEn "Well-definedness of dimension rests on all bases having the same size" ;
    ex:nameZh "维数的良定义性依赖任两个基等势" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dimension-defined-as-the-element-count-of-a-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Reading n off from a chosen basis defines a function of V only because Theorem 15.6 makes n independent of the choice. Without Theorem 15.6 the phrase the dimension of V would not denote anything, so dimension does not hang on its definition alone but on the definition together with Theorem 15.6." .

<urn:studium:node:d:equality-holds-exactly-when-t-equals-s> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "But $\\| s - t\\|^2 \\geq 0$ , so we have $\\| x - t\\|^2 \\geq \\| x - s\\|^2$ , with equality holding if and only if $s = t$ .",
        "for all $t$ in $S$ ; the equality sign holds if and only if $t = s$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But $\\| s - t\\|^2 \\geq 0$ , so we have $\\| x - t\\|^2 \\geq \\| x - s\\|^2$ ,",
        "with equality holding if and only if $s = t$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Equality holds exactly when t = s" ;
    ex:nameZh "等号成立恰当 t = s" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:approximation-inequality-with-equality-only-at-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:pythagorean-split-of-the-approximation-error> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Since ||s - t||^2 >= 0, the identity yields ||x - t||^2 >= ||x - s||^2, and equality forces ||s - t||^2 = 0, that is s = t. The projection is therefore not just a minimizer but the only one." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-11-a-nonzero-prescribed-value-breaks-closure> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The number 0 is essential in this example. If we replace 0 by a nonzero number c, we violate the closure axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If we replace 0 by a nonzero number c, we violate the closure axioms.",
        "The number 0 is essential in this example. If we replace 0 by a nonzero number c, we violate the closure axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "Replacing 0 by a nonzero c in Example 11 breaks the closure axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "例11中把 0 换成非零 c 即破坏封闭公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-11-functions-vanishing-at-a-point> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol notes that the number 0 is essential and that prescribing a nonzero value c violates the closure axioms; concretely, f(1) = g(1) = c gives (f+g)(1) = 2c, which differs from c when c is nonzero, and 0f has value 0 rather than c. The zero on the right-hand side of the constraint is what makes the condition survive addition and scaling." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-12-solutions-of-a-homogeneous-second-order-equation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 12. The set of all solutions of a homogeneous linear differential equation $y'' + ay' + by = 0$ , where a and b are given constants." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 12. The set of all solutions of a homogeneous linear differential equation $y'' + ay' + by = 0$ , where a and b are given constants." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 12: the solutions of y'' + ay' + by = 0" ;
    ex:nameZh "例12:齐次方程 y'' + ay' + by = 0 的解集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-a-second-order-de-solution-space-is-two> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:homogeneous-de-solution-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For fixed constants a and b the set of all solutions of the homogeneous equation y'' + ay' + by = 0 is a function space. Closure holds because the left-hand side is a linear operator applied to y, so it sends a sum of solutions and a multiple of a solution again to 0." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-8-continuous-functions-on-an-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 8. The set of all functions continuous on a given interval." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 8. The set of all functions continuous on a given interval." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 8: the functions continuous on a given interval" ;
    ex:nameZh "例8:在给定区间上连续的全体函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The continuous functions on one interval form a function space; the closure axioms hold because a sum of continuous functions and a scalar multiple of a continuous function are continuous. Here closure is genuine mathematics rather than bookkeeping, since it rests on the limit theorems for sums and constant multiples." .

<urn:studium:node:d:existence-of-an-orthogonal-basis-deferred> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In Section 15.13 we shall prove that every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthogonal basis." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In Section 15.13 we shall prove that every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthogonal basis." ;
    ex:nameEn "Existence of an orthogonal basis is deferred to Section 15.13" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交基的存在性推迟到 15.13 节" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol flags that the existence of an orthogonal basis in every finite-dimensional Euclidean space is proved only later, in Section 15.13. Theorem 15.11 is therefore conditional in practice: it computes components RELATIVE TO an orthogonal basis and assumes one is given. Recording this keeps the dependency honest, since 15.11 is otherwise easy to read as if orthogonal bases were already known to exist." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-base-case-n-equals-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The result holds trivially when $n = 1$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The result holds trivially when $n = 1$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Base case: a single exponential function is independent" ;
    ex:nameZh "归纳起点:单个指数函数是独立的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "For n = 1 the claim is that c times e to the a x vanishing identically forces c = 0, which holds because the exponential function is never zero. Apostol calls this trivial and does not expand it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-negativity-of-the-shifted-exponents> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $k \\neq M$ , the number $a_k - a_M$ is negative." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $k \\neq M$ , the number $a_k - a_M$ is negative." ;
    ex:nameEn "The shifted exponents are strictly negative off the maximal index" ;
    ex:nameZh "除最大指标外平移后的指数严格为负" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-hypothesis-distinct-exponents>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-select-the-largest-exponent> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "For every k different from M the number a_k minus a_M is negative. This is where the distinctness hypothesis is consumed: without distinctness a tie would give a shifted exponent equal to zero and the corresponding term would not decay." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-law-used-in-the-shift> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\sum_ {k = 1} ^ {n} c _ {k} e ^ {(a _ {k} - a _ {M}) x} = 0.\\tag{15.3}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\sum_ {k = 1} ^ {n} c _ {k} e ^ {(a _ {k} - a _ {M}) x} = 0.\\tag{15.3}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Non-algebraic input: the exponential addition law" ;
    ex:nameZh "非代数输入:指数加法律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-multiply-by-a-normalizing-exponential> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Rewriting e to the a_k x times e to the minus a_M x as e to the (a_k minus a_M) x is a property of the exponential function, not of the linear space. Without it the multiplication would not simplify the relation at all." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-finiteness-clause> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V" ;
    ex:nameEn "Finiteness is part of the phrase finite basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "有限性是「有限基」一词的组成部分" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The definition speaks of a finite set S. Finiteness is not derived from independence or spanning; it is stipulated, and it is what makes the element count in Theorem 15.6 a well-defined integer." .

<urn:studium:node:d:function-space-axioms-reduce-pointwise-to-facts-about-real-numbers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Multiplication of a function f by a real scalar a is defined as follows: af is that function whose value at each x in the domain of f is $af(x)$ .",
        "The elements of V are real-valued functions, with addition of two functions f and g defined in the usual way:",
        "The zero element is the function whose values are everywhere zero." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following sets is a function space." ;
    ex:nameEn "All ten axioms in a function space reduce pointwise to real arithmetic" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数空间的十条公理按点归结为实数运算律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-zero-is-the-everywhere-zero-function> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Because both operations are defined value by value, each of Axioms 3, 4, 7, 8, 9 and 10 for functions is the corresponding law of real arithmetic asserted at every point of the domain, and Axioms 5 and 6 are met by the zero function and by (-1)f, both of which Axiom 2 puts back into the set. Hence no function-space example needs a separate argument for these eight axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-base-step-y1-equals-x1> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We construct the elements $y_1, y_2, \\ldots$ , by induction. To start the process, we take $y_1 = x_1$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "To start the process, we take $y_1 = x_1$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Base step of the recursion: y_1 = x_1" ;
    ex:nameZh "递推起点:y_1 = x_1" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The induction that proves Theorem 15.13 starts by setting y_1 = x_1, so no subtraction is performed at the first step. Condition (a) holds vacuously for k = 1 because there are no earlier elements for y_1 to be orthogonal to; condition (b) is a substantive equality L(y_1) = L(x_1) that holds precisely because y_1 = x_1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-works-in-infinite-dimensional-spaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "consider the infinite sequence $x_0, x_1, x_2, \\ldots$ , where $x_n(t) = t^n$ .",
        "whose proof shows how to construct orthogonal sets in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "whose proof shows how to construct orthogonal sets in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional" ;
    ex:nameEn "The construction needs no finiteness hypothesis" ;
    ex:nameZh "构造本身不需要有限维假设" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The orthogonalization theorem is stated for a finite or infinite sequence and its proof is by induction on one step at a time, so it applies in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional. The Legendre example is an actual infinite-dimensional instance." .

<urn:studium:node:d:independence-of-an-infinite-set-reduces-to-its-finite-subsets> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set $S = \\{u_{0}, u_{1}, u_{2}, \\ldots\\}$ is independent. To prove this, it suffices to show that for each $n$ the $n + 1$ polynomials" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets.",
        "The set $S = \\{u_{0}, u_{1}, u_{2}, \\ldots\\}$ is independent. To prove this, it suffices to show that for each $n$ the $n + 1$ polynomials" ;
    ex:nameEn "Independence of an infinite set reduces to independence of its finite subsets" ;
    ex:nameZh "无限集的独立性归约为其有限子集的独立性" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-definition-not-restricted-to-finite-sets>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-finiteness-of-the-witnessing-family> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "To prove the infinite set of powers independent, Apostol proves that for each n the first n+1 powers are independent. This reduction works because every witnessing relation is finite, so no relation can involve more than finitely many members." .

<urn:studium:node:d:independence-of-the-empty-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 4. The empty set is independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 4. The empty set is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "The empty set is independent" ;
    ex:nameZh "空集是独立的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element-forces-dependence> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:zero-element-destroys-independence> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Example 4: with no elements available there is no finite family of distinct elements to witness dependence, so the empty set is independent by vacuity. This boundary case is what makes the zero space fit the basis machinery." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-axiomatic-not-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The reader should verify that each of the following satisfies all the axioms for an inner product.",
        "we define the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "we define the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula" ;
    ex:nameEn "The inner product is defined axiomatically, not by a formula" ;
    ex:nameZh "内积是公理式定义,而非由公式定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol defines the inner product by listing properties and declaring them axioms, instead of prescribing a formula such as the component sum. This is the methodological pivot of 15.10: every later theorem (15.8, 15.9, 15.10-15.12) is then a consequence of the axioms alone and therefore holds in every model, including function spaces where no component sum exists." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-of-the-new-element-with-an-earlier-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(y _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) = (x _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {r} a _ {i} (y _ {i}, y _ {j}) = (x _ {r + 1}, y _ {j}) - a _ {j} (y _ {j}, y _ {j}),",
        "For $j\\leq r$ , the inner product of $y_{r+1}$ with $y_{j}$ is given by",
        "since $(y_{i},y_{j}) = 0$ if $i\\neq j$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "For $j\\leq r$ , the inner product of $y_{r+1}$ with $y_{j}$ is given by",
        "since $(y_{i},y_{j}) = 0$ if $i\\neq j$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Expanding (y_{r+1}, y_j) collapses to two terms" ;
    ex:nameZh "把 (y_{r+1}, y_j) 展开后只剩两项" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-inductive-step-defines-y-r-plus-one> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Taking the inner product of the defining equation with y_j for j <= r and using linearity gives (y_{r+1}, y_j) = (x_{r+1}, y_j) - a_j (y_j, y_j): every cross term with i != j drops out by the inductive orthogonality hypothesis. This single computation is what turns the choice of a_j into a one-unknown equation." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-real-valued-and-quantifiers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. A real linear space V is said to have an inner product if for each pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique real ",
        "satisfying the following axioms for all choices of x, y, z in V and all real scalars c." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. A real linear space V is said to have an inner product if for each pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique real ",
        "In a complex linear space, an inner product $(x, y)$ is a complex number satisfying the same axioms as those for a real inner produ" ;
    ex:nameEn "An inner product assigns a unique real number, for all x, y, z and all real scalars c" ;
    ex:nameZh "内积给出唯一实数,并对所有 x, y, z 与所有实标量 c 成立" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The framing clause of the definition: an inner product on a real linear space V is a map that assigns to each pair x, y in V one and only one REAL number (x, y), and the axioms are required for all x, y, z in V and all real scalars c. Two things are fixed here before any axiom is stated: the value is real, and the axioms are universally quantified rather than asserted for special elements. The reality of the value is exactly what must be relaxed in the complex case." .

<urn:studium:node:d:linear-combination-finiteness-of-the-index-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "x = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i}," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "x = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i}," ;
    ex:nameEn "Finiteness of the number of terms in a linear combination" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性组合中项数的有限性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "A linear combination is a sum over indices 1 through k for some finite k. Only finitely many terms are allowed because a bare linear space has addition of two elements and no notion of convergence, so an infinite sum is undefined." .

<urn:studium:node:d:n-plus-one-elements-in-an-n-dimensional-space-are-dependent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "In a space of dimension n every n+1 elements are dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "在 n 维空间中任意 n+1 个元素必相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol does not state this as a numbered result in Chapter 15; it is Theorem 15.5 instantiated at a basis. The anchor quotes the conclusion of Theorem 15.5 that is being instantiated." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dimension-well-definedness-requires-all-bases-to-be-equinumerous>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause> ;
    ex:section "15.07",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Applying Theorem 15.5 to a basis of V, whose span is V itself, gives that every set of n+1 elements of V is dependent. It is not part of the statement of Theorem 15.7; it is the consequence of Theorem 15.5 that the proof of 15.7(b) uses, and its whole proof is one instantiation of Theorem 15.5." .

<urn:studium:node:d:nature-of-the-elements-is-left-unspecified> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In defining a linear space, we do not specify the nature of the elements nor do we tell how the operations are to be performed on them." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In defining a linear space, we do not specify the nature of the elements nor do we tell how the operations are to be performed on them." ;
    ex:nameEn "The nature of the elements is left unspecified" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素的性质被刻意不加规定" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.01" ;
    ex:statement "The definition never says what the elements are; they may be numbers, functions, n-tuples or series. This deliberate omission is what lets one proof serve every example, and it also forbids any proof from using a property of the elements that no axiom grants." .

<urn:studium:node:d:new-element-is-well-defined-and-orthogonal-to-all-earlier-ones> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Thus, the element $y_{r+1}$ is well defined and is orthogonal to each of the earlier elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{r}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Thus, the element $y_{r+1}$ is well defined and is orthogonal to each of the earlier elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{r}$ .",
        "a _ {j} = \\frac {(x _ {r + 1} , y _ {j})}{(y _ {j} , y _ {j})}.\\tag{15.14}",
        "and in this case we choose $a_{j} = 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "y_{r+1} is well defined and orthogonal to each earlier element" ;
    ex:nameZh "y_{r+1} 良定义且与前面每个元素都正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-a-orthogonal-to-earlier-span> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:coefficient-that-cancels-the-projection-component>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-earlier-element-makes-the-coefficient-irrelevant> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Combining the two cases, every coefficient a_1,...,a_r is determined, so y_{r+1} is well defined, and by construction it is orthogonal to each of y_1,...,y_r simultaneously. This is the independently checkable assertion that the subtraction of projections buys orthogonality to all earlier elements at once, not merely to the last one." .

<urn:studium:node:d:norm-homogeneity-property> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) $\\| cx\\| = |c|\\| x\\|$ (homogeneity)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) $\\| cx\\| = |c|\\| x\\|$ (homogeneity).",
        "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Property (c): ||cx|| = |c| ||x||" ;
    ex:nameZh "性质 (c):||cx|| = |c| ||x||" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:conjugate-homogeneity-in-second-argument>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.9(c): ||cx|| = |c| ||x|| for every scalar c. Its proof uses homogeneity in the first slot to pull c out, then symmetry (or (1') in the complex case) plus homogeneity again to pull the second copy out, giving (cx, cx) = c times conjugate(c) times (x, x) = |c|^2 (x, x). The absolute value, and in the complex case the modulus, is produced by this second extraction, which is why the identity reads |c| and not c." .

<urn:studium:node:d:normalized-element-has-norm-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "(c) $\\| cx\\| = |c|\\| x\\|$ (homogeneity).",
        "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) $\\| cx\\| = |c|\\| x\\|$ (homogeneity).",
        "In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "Dividing by the norm produces an element of norm 1" ;
    ex:nameZh "除以范数后范数为 1" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:normalizing-converts-an-orthogonal-basis-into-an-orthonormal-one> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "For y != O the element y/||y|| has norm 1, because the norm is absolutely homogeneous: ||cy|| = |c| ||y||. Positivity of the norm is what makes the division legal." .

<urn:studium:node:d:normalizing-an-orthogonal-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Dividing each $u_{n}$ by its norm, we obtain an orthonormal set $\\{\\varphi_0,\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2,\\ldots \\}$ where $\\varphi_{n} = u_{n} / \\| u_{n}\\|$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Dividing each $u_{n}$ by its norm, we obtain an orthonormal set $\\{\\varphi_0,\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2,\\ldots \\}$ where $\\varphi_{n} = u_{n} / \\| u_{n}\\|$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Normalization: divide each element by its norm" ;
    ex:nameZh "标准化:每个元素除以自身范数" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:norm-positivity-property> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The procedure by which an orthonormal set is manufactured from an orthogonal one: dividing each u_n by its norm gives an orthonormal set with phi_n = u_n / ||u_n||. Two prerequisites are silent. The division requires ||u_n|| nonzero, hence u_n nonzero and hence the positivity axiom; and orthogonality survives the division because scalars pull out of the inner product by homogeneity, so (phi_m, phi_n) is a scalar multiple of (u_m, u_n) and stays zero." .

<urn:studium:node:d:one-proof-from-the-axioms-serves-every-example> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "These examples and many others illustrate how the linear space concept permeates algebra, geometry, and analysis.",
        "When a theorem is deduced from the axioms of a linear space, we obtain, in one stroke, a result valid for each concrete example." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When a theorem is deduced from the axioms of a linear space, we obtain, in one stroke, a result valid for each concrete example." ;
    ex:nameEn "One proof from the axioms serves every example at once" ;
    ex:nameZh "一次从公理出发的证明同时服务于所有例子" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:d:examples-are-asserted-to-satisfy-the-axioms-and-left-to-the-reader> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Because the theorems of the chapter are deduced from the axioms alone, each of them holds simultaneously in every set that satisfies the axioms. This is the payoff that repays the axiomatic detour, and it fixes the price: an argument that uses a feature special to one example proves nothing about the others." .

<urn:studium:node:d:ordered-basis-adds-an-ordering-to-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "consider a basis whose elements $e_1, \\ldots, e_n$ are taken in a given order. We denote such an ordered basis as an $n$ -tuple $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "consider a basis whose elements $e_1, \\ldots, e_n$ are taken in a given order. We denote such an ordered basis as an $n$ -tuple $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "An ordered basis adds an ordering that a basis as a set does not carry" ;
    ex:nameZh "有序基在基之上附加了集合本身不具备的次序" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:ordered-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A basis is a set and therefore unordered; an ordered basis is the basis together with a chosen enumeration, recorded as an n-tuple. The ordering is extra data, and permuting it gives a different ordered basis for the same basis." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-basis-component-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "c _ {j} = \\frac {(x , e _ {j})}{(e _ {j} , e _ {j})} \\quad f o r \\quad j = 1, 2, \\dots , n.\\tag{15.8}",
        "then its components relative to the ordered basis $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ are given by the formulas" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "c _ {j} = \\frac {(x , e _ {j})}{(e _ {j} , e _ {j})} \\quad f o r \\quad j = 1, 2, \\dots , n.\\tag{15.8}",
        "then its components relative to the ordered basis $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ are given by the formulas" ;
    ex:nameEn "Formula (15.8): c_j = (x, e_j) / (e_j, e_j)" ;
    ex:nameZh "公式 (15.8):c_j = (x, e_j) / (e_j, e_j)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:15-11-denominator-nonzero-by-positivity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:15-11-proof-pair-with-basis-element> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The conclusion of Theorem 15.11: the components of x relative to the ordered basis (e_1, ..., e_n) are c_j = (x, e_j)/(e_j, e_j) for j = 1, ..., n. The significance is computational independence: each component is obtained by a single inner product against a single basis element, with no linear system to solve and no reference to the other components. That decoupling is exactly what orthogonality buys." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-complement-is-a-name-reserved-for-subspaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S.",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one." ;
    ex:nameEn "The name orthogonal complement is reserved for the case where S is a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "只有 S 是子空间时才叫正交补" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "S perp always exists and is always a subspace, but Apostol calls it the orthogonal complement of S only when S itself is a subspace. The restriction is terminological, not mathematical." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-is-vanishing-of-the-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality is defined as the vanishing of the inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交定义为内积为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:angle-defined-only-in-real-euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-real-valued-and-quantifiers> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Definition: in a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero. No angle is invoked. This matters because the angle is defined only in a real Euclidean space, whereas orthogonality as defined here is available in a complex space too, where (x, y) = 0 is equivalent to (y, x) = 0 by Hermitian symmetry, so the relation is still symmetric." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-basis-component-is-the-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In particular, if $S$ is an orthonormal basis, each $c_{j}$ is given by",
        "c _ {j} = (x, e _ {j}).\\tag{15.9}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In particular, if $S$ is an orthonormal basis, each $c_{j}$ is given by",
        "since $(e_i, e_j) = 0$ if $i \\neq j$ . This implies (15.8), and when $(e_j, e_j) = 1$ , we obtain (15.9)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Formula (15.9): for an orthonormal basis, c_j = (x, e_j)" ;
    ex:nameZh "公式 (15.9):正交规范基下 c_j = (x, e_j)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-basis-component-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-set-adds-unit-norm> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Specialization of (15.8): if S is an orthonormal basis then c_j = (x, e_j). The mechanism is stated by Apostol in one clause, when (e_j, e_j) = 1 we obtain (15.9); the unit-norm condition simply erases the denominator. This is where normalization pays for itself, and it is what makes Parseval's formula expressible purely in inner products." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-set-adds-unit-norm> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1." ;
    ex:nameEn "An orthonormal set is an orthogonal set whose elements all have norm 1" ;
    ex:nameZh "标准正交集是每个元素范数为 1 的正交集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-set-condition-on-distinct-pairs-only> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Definition: an orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1. Two consequences follow immediately from the definitions already in place. First, an orthonormal set cannot contain O, since ||O|| = 0, so orthonormal sets are automatically independent by Theorem 15.10. Second, (e_j, e_j) = 1, which is exactly what collapses formula (15.8) to (15.9)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:parseval-formula-statement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) (\\overline {{y , e _ {i}}}) \\quad \\text {(Parseval's formula)}.\\tag{15.11}",
        "The next theorem shows that in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with an orthonormal basis the inner product of two elements can be computed in terms of their components." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) (\\overline {{y , e _ {i}}}) \\quad \\text {(Parseval's formula)}.\\tag{15.11}",
        "Proof. Taking the inner product of both members of Equation (15.10) with y and using the linearity property of the inner product, we obtain (15.11)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Parseval's formula (15.11)" ;
    ex:nameZh "Parseval 公式 (15.11)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:parseval-proof-pair-expansion-with-y> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Equation (15.11): (x, y) = sum over i from 1 to n of (x, e_i) times the conjugate of (y, e_i). It reduces an abstract inner product to a finite sum of scalar products of components, i.e. it says that in an orthonormal basis every Euclidean space computes like V_n with the dot product. Note where the conjugate sits: on the y-factor, i.e. on the component of the SECOND argument." .

<urn:studium:node:d:positivity-axiom-forbids-nonzero-self-orthogonal-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:nameEn "Positivity of the inner product is what forbids self-orthogonality" ;
    ex:nameZh "正定性公理正是禁止非零元自正交的那条依据" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:zero-is-the-only-element-orthogonal-to-itself> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "The inner-product axiom (x, x) > 0 for x != O immediately yields the implication used above: (x, x) = 0 forces x = O. The self-orthogonality fact therefore carries no content beyond positivity, which is why it can be invoked in one clause. Note the implication is strictly weaker than the axiom, not equivalent to it: it alone does not give strict positivity." .

<urn:studium:node:d:powers-independence-by-differentiating-and-evaluating-at-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "When $t = 0$ , this gives $c_0 = 0$ . Differentiating (15.1) and setting $t = 0$ , we find that $c_1 = 0$ . Repeating the process, we find that each coefficient $c_k$ is zero." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A relation of the form $\\sum c_{k} u_{k} = O$ means that",
        "To prove this, it suffices to show that for each $n$ the $n + 1$ polynomials $u_{0}, u_{1}, \\ldots, u_{n}$ are independent.",
        "When $t = 0$ , this gives $c_0 = 0$ . Differentiating (15.1) and setting $t = 0$ , we find that $c_1 = 0$ . Repeating the process, we find that each coefficient $c_k$ is zero." ;
    ex:nameEn "Independence of the powers of t by repeated differentiation at zero" ;
    ex:nameZh "用反复求导并在零点取值证明 t 的幂的独立性" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:independence-of-an-infinite-set-reduces-to-its-finite-subsets>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:independence-vanishing-combination-forces-vanishing-coefficients>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-of-a-function-space-is-a-pointwise-identity> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Example 6 extracts the coefficients one at a time: setting t = 0 gives c_0, differentiating once and setting t = 0 gives c_1, and repeating gives every c_k. The technique is analytic, not algebraic: it needs differentiability of polynomials and the fact that an identity in t may be differentiated." .

<urn:studium:node:d:projection-is-the-s-component-of-the-orthogonal-decomposition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We prove next that the projection of $x$ on $S$ is the solution to the approximation problem stated at the beginning of this section.",
        "is called the projection of $x$ on the subspace $S$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "That is, if s is the projection of x on S, we have",
        "We prove next that the projection of $x$ on $S$ is the solution to the approximation problem stated at the beginning of this section.",
        "s = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}" ;
    ex:nameEn "The projection is exactly the S-component of the orthogonal decomposition" ;
    ex:nameZh "投影恰是正交分解中的 S 分量" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:s-defined-as-the-sum-of-projections-along-the-basis-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The formula defining the projection is the same formula that produced s in the existence proof of Theorem 15.15, so the projection of x on S is the S-component of x in the decomposition x = s + s perp. This identification is what lets Theorem 15.16 speak about the projection. Apostol never states this identification explicitly; it follows from the definition of the projection being verbatim formula (15.18) of the existence proof." .

<urn:studium:node:d:proof-a-set-z-equal-to-0x-and-double-it> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (a). Let $z = 0x$ . We wish to prove that $z = O$ . Adding $z$ to itself and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + z = 0 x + 0 x = (0 + 0) x = 0 x = z." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $z = 0x$ . We wish to prove that $z = O$ . Adding $z$ to itself and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + z = 0 x + 0 x = (0 + 0) x = 0 x = z." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof of (a), step 1: set z = 0x and add z to itself" ;
    ex:nameZh "(a)证明第一步:令 z = 0x 并把 z 与自身相加" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Put z = 0x and form z + z. Axiom 9 turns 0x + 0x into (0 + 0)x, which is again 0x, so z + z = z; this is the step that consumes the distributive Axiom 9; the cancellation that follows spends Axioms 4, 5 and 6." .

<urn:studium:node:d:pythagorean-expansion-of-the-squared-norm> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Finally, we prove that the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula. We have",
        "\\| x \\| ^ {2} = (x, x) = (s + s ^ {\\perp}, s + s ^ {\\perp}) = (s, s) + (s ^ {\\perp}, s ^ {\\perp})," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "Finally, we prove that the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula. We have",
        "In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "Expanding ||x||^2 by linearity" ;
    ex:nameZh "用线性展开 ||x||^2" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Writing ||x||^2 = (x, x) = (s + s perp, s + s perp) and expanding by linearity produces the two square terms plus two cross terms. The expansion is pure axiom application. Apostol collapses this expansion into a single displayed line, so the intermediate cross-term form is a reconstruction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:pythagorean-split-of-the-approximation-error> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\| x - t \\| ^ {2} = \\| x - s \\| ^ {2} + \\| s - t \\| ^ {2}.",
        "so its norm is given by the Pythagorean formula" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\| x - t \\| ^ {2} = \\| x - s \\| ^ {2} + \\| s - t \\| ^ {2}.",
        "so its norm is given by the Pythagorean formula",
        "this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$ , so its norm is given by the Pythagorean formula" ;
    ex:nameEn "The error identity ||x - t||^2 = ||x - s||^2 + ||s - t||^2" ;
    ex:nameZh "误差恒等式 ||x - t||^2 = ||x - s||^2 + ||s - t||^2" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:approximation-inequality-with-equality-only-at-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:x-minus-t-splits-orthogonally> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Applying the Pythagorean formula to the decomposition of x - t gives ||x - t||^2 = ||x - s||^2 + ||s - t||^2. This identity, not an inequality, is the substantive content of the approximation theorem: the error of any competitor exceeds the error at s by exactly ||s - t||^2." .

<urn:studium:node:d:real-in-real-linear-space-refers-to-the-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Even though the elements of V are complex numbers, this is a real linear space because the scalars are real.",
        "Linear spaces, as defined above, are sometimes called real linear spaces to emphasize the fact that we are multiplying the elements of V by real numbers." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A real linear space has real numbers as scalars; a complex linear space has complex numbers as scalars.",
        "Linear spaces, as defined above, are sometimes called real linear spaces to emphasize the fact that we are multiplying the elements of V by real numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "The word real in real linear space refers to the scalars" ;
    ex:nameZh "「实线性空间」中的「实」指标量而非元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The qualifier real records which numbers are used as multipliers, not what the elements are. Example 2 is the test case: its elements are complex numbers, yet it is a real linear space because only real multipliers are admitted." .

<urn:studium:node:d:rescaling-preserves-orthogonality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, c y) = \\overline {{(c y , x)}} = \\bar {c} (\\overline {{y , x}}) = \\bar {c} (x, y).",
        "Dividing each $u_{n}$ by its norm, we obtain an orthonormal set $\\{\\varphi_0,\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2,\\ldots \\}$ where $\\varphi_{n} = u_{n} / \\| u_{n}\\|$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number." ;
    ex:nameEn "Rescaling each element preserves orthogonality" ;
    ex:nameZh "逐元素缩放不破坏正交性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:normalizing-converts-an-orthogonal-basis-into-an-orthonormal-one> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.11",
        "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "If (y_i, y_j) = 0 for i != j then (c_i y_i, c_j y_j) = c_i conj(c_j) (y_i, y_j) = 0, by homogeneity in the first argument and conjugate homogeneity in the second, so any elementwise rescaling of an orthogonal set is again orthogonal. In a real Euclidean space the conjugate is absent and the factor is simply c_i c_j. Apostol uses this without comment whenever he normalizes, both in 15.11 and in 15.13." .

<urn:studium:node:d:s-perp-is-defined-for-an-arbitrary-subset> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. Let S be a subset of a Euclidean space V.",
        "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. Let S be a subset of a Euclidean space V.",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one. In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S.",
        "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" ;
    ex:nameEn "S perp is defined for an arbitrary subset, not just a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "S 垂直对任意子集都有定义,不要求 S 是子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-complement-is-a-name-reserved-for-subspaces> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:s-perp-notation> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol introduces S only as a subset of V, so the collection of all elements orthogonal to S is defined with no structural hypothesis on S. This weak hypothesis is what the next observation exploits." .

<urn:studium:node:d:s-perp-is-orthogonal-to-each-basis-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(s ^ {\\perp}, e _ {j}) = (x - s, e _ {j}) = (x, e _ {j}) - (s, e _ {j}).",
        "But from (15.18), we find that $(s, e_j) = (x, e_j)$ , so $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to $e_j$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "But from (15.18), we find that $(s, e_j) = (x, e_j)$ ,",
        "Since S is finite-dimensional, it has a finite orthonormal basis, say $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$ .",
        "To prove that $s^{\\perp}$ lies in $S^{\\perp}$ , we consider the inner product of $s^{\\perp}$ and any basis element $e_{j}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "s perp is orthogonal to each basis element e_j" ;
    ex:nameZh "s 垂直与每个基元 e_j 正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:s-defined-as-the-sum-of-projections-along-the-basis-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Expanding (s perp, e_j) = (x, e_j) - (s, e_j) and reading (s, e_j) = (x, e_j) off the definition of s gives (s perp, e_j) = 0 for every j. Here the orthonormality of the basis is what makes (s, e_j) collapse to a single coefficient." .

<urn:studium:node:d:spanning-set-is-not-unique> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Different sets may span the same subspace. For example, the space $V_{2}$ is spanned by each of the following sets of vectors:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Different sets may span the same subspace. For example, the space $V_{2}$ is spanned by each of the following sets of vectors:" ;
    ex:nameEn "A subspace does not determine its spanning set" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间并不决定其张成集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Distinct sets can have the same span; the text exhibits three different sets all spanning V_2, and three different sets all spanning the polynomials of degree at most n. So spanning is a many-to-one relation from sets to subspaces." .

<urn:studium:node:d:spanning-set-need-not-be-independent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$\\{i,j\\}$ , $\\{i,j,i+j\\}$ , $\\{O,i,-i,j,-j,i+j\\}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$\\{i,j\\}$ , $\\{i,j,i+j\\}$ , $\\{O,i,-i,j,-j,i+j\\}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "A spanning set may be redundant" ;
    ex:nameZh "张成集可以是冗余的" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:spanning-set-is-not-unique> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The set containing O, i, -i, j, -j and i+j spans V_2 while containing the zero element and repeated directions. Spanning therefore imposes no independence requirement, which is precisely why a basis needs a second clause." .

<urn:studium:node:d:subspace-inherits-the-operations-of-the-ambient-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is also a linear space, with the same operations of addition and multiplication by scalars, then S is called a subspace of V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If S is also a linear space, with the same operations of addition and multiplication by scalars, then S is called a subspace of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "A subspace carries the same addition and scalar multiplication as V" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间沿用母空间的加法与数乘" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "S must be a linear space under the operations it inherits from V, not under some freshly invented pair of operations. This clause is what makes a subspace a structural part of V rather than an unrelated space that happens to sit inside it as a set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:subspace-satisfies-all-linear-space-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is a subspace, it satisfies all the axioms for a linear space" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If S is a subspace, it satisfies all the axioms for a linear space",
        "If S is a subspace, it satisfies all the axioms for a linear space, and hence, in particular, it satisfies the closure axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "A subspace satisfies every linear space axiom" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间满足全部线性空间公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "By definition a subspace is itself a linear space, so all ten axioms hold in S. This is the burden that Theorem 15.4 later reduces to the two closure axioms alone." .

<urn:studium:node:d:the-difference-lies-in-both-s-and-s-perp> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "But $s - t \\in S$ and $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$ so $s - t$ is both orthogonal to $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ and equal to $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But $s - t \\in S$ and $t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one.",
        "so we need only prove that $s - t = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The same element lies in S and in S perp at once" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一个元素同时属于 S 和 S 垂直" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-uniqueness> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-reduces-to-a-single-vanishing-difference> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "s - t belongs to S while t perp - s perp belongs to S perp, and the two are equal; hence this single element is orthogonal to itself. Membership in both sides is forced only by S and S perp being closed under differences." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-2-same-sum-evaluated-in-two-groupings> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Suppose x has two negatives, say $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ . Then $x + y_{1} = O$ and $x + y_{2} = O$ .",
        "y _ {2} + (x + y _ {1}) = (y _ {2} + x) + y _ {1} = O + y _ {1} = y _ {1} + O = y _ {1}.",
        "y _ {2} + (x + y _ {1}) = y _ {2} + O = y _ {2}," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Adding $y_{2}$ to both members of the first equation and using Axioms 5, 4, and 3, we find that",
        "Suppose x has two negatives, say $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ . Then $x + y_{1} = O$ and $x + y_{2} = O$ .",
        "y _ {2} + (x + y _ {1}) = (y _ {2} + x) + y _ {1} = O + y _ {1} = y _ {1} + O = y _ {1}." ;
    ex:nameEn "One sum evaluated in two groupings forces the two negatives to agree" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一个和用两种结合方式各算一次,迫使两个负元相等" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-4-associative-law-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "The proof of Theorem 15.2 assumes two negatives y_1 and y_2 of the same x and then evaluates the single expression y_2 + (x + y_1) twice. Grouped as written it collapses to y_2 because x + y_1 = O; regrouped as (y_2 + x) + y_1 it collapses to y_1 because y_2 + x = O. The two values are values of one expression, so y_1 = y_2." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3e-a-nonzero-scalar-may-be-cancelled> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(e) If $ax = ay$ and $a \\neq 0$ , then $x = y$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(e) If $ax = ay$ and $a \\neq 0$ , then $x = y$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(e): a nonzero scalar may be cancelled" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(e):非零标量可约去" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-7-associative-law-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "If ax = ay and a is not 0 then x = y. The standard route multiplies by 1/a and uses only Axioms 7 and 10, so the property is exactly the statement that a nonzero scalar acts injectively on V." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3f-a-nonzero-element-may-be-cancelled> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f) If $ax = bx$ and $x \\neq O$ , then $a = b$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(f) If $ax = bx$ and $x \\neq O$ , then $a = b$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(f): a nonzero element may be cancelled" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(f):非零元素可约去" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3c-negation-passes-through-scalar-multiplication>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3d-a-vanishing-product-forces-a-vanishing-factor> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "If ax = bx and x is not O then a = b. It is the mirror image of (e) with the roles of scalar and element exchanged, and it says that a nonzero element is faithful: distinct scalars cannot act on it in the same way." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3g-the-negative-of-a-sum> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(g) $-(x + y) = (-x) + (-y) = -x - y.$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(g) $-(x + y) = (-x) + (-y) = -x - y.$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(g): the negative of a sum" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(g):和的负元" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "For all x, y we have -(x + y) = (-x) + (-y) = -x - y. Negation distributes over addition, which is what makes the difference notation of Section 15.4 behave like ordinary subtraction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3h-repeated-addition-is-an-integer-multiple> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(h) $x + x = 2x, x + x + x = 3x$ , and in general, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x = nx$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(h) $x + x = 2x, x + x + x = 3x$ , and in general, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x = nx$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(h): repeated addition is an integer scalar multiple" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(h):反复相加即整数倍数乘" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "x + x = 2x, x + x + x = 3x, and in general the sum of n copies of x equals nx. It identifies an operation internal to V, repeated addition, with scalar multiplication by a positive integer, so the integers acquire their familiar counting role inside any linear space." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-hypothesis-nonempty-subset> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let S be a nonempty subset of a linear space V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let S be a nonempty subset of a linear space V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.4 hypothesis: S is a nonempty subset of V" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.4的前提:S 是 V 的非空子集" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The closure test is asserted only for nonempty S. The hypothesis is not decoration: the empty set satisfies both closure axioms vacuously yet has no zero element, so it is not a subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-zero-element-obtained-by-taking-the-scalar-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Taking $a = 0$ , it follows that $0x$ is in $S$ . But $0x = O$ , by Theorem 15.3(a), so $O \\in S$ , and Axiom 5 is satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But $0x = O$ , by Theorem 15.3(a), so $O \\in S$ , and Axiom 5 is satisfied.",
        "By Axiom 2, $ax$ is in $S$ for every scalar $a$ . Taking $a = 0$ , it follows that $0x$ is in $S$ .",
        "Let $x$ be any element of $S$ . (S has at least one element since $S$ is not empty.) By Axiom 2, $ax$ is in $S$ for every scalar $a$ .",
        "Taking $a = 0$ , it follows that $0x$ is in $S$ . But $0x = O$ , by Theorem 15.3(a), so $O \\in S$ , and Axiom 5 is satisfied.",
        "it follows that $0x$ is in $S$ . But $0x = O$ , by Theorem 15.3(a), so $O \\in S$ , and Axiom 5 is satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "Taking a = 0 puts the zero element into S" ;
    ex:nameZh "取 a = 0 把零元放进 S" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-remaining-obligations-zero-and-negative> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-closure-under-scalar-multiplication-is-the-active-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-witness-element-supplied-by-nonemptiness> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Closure under scalar multiplication with a = 0 gives 0x in S, and Theorem 15.3(a) identifies 0x with the zero element O. Hence Axiom 5 holds in S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-generality-transfer-uses-only-the-linear-space-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "we find that it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If we examine the proof of Theorem 12.8, we find that it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space",
        "it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$ .",
        "we find that it is based only on the fact that $V_{n}$ is a linear space and nc on any other special property of $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The transfer argument: the old proof used only the linear space axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "迁移论证:旧证明只用到线性空间公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-proof-delegated-to-theorem-12-8> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The justification for reusing the proof of Theorem 12.8 is an audit of that proof: it rests only on V_n being a linear space and on no other special property of V_n. This audit, not any new computation, is what makes Theorem 15.5 hold in arbitrary linear spaces." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-5-proof-delegated-to-theorem-12-8> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "When $V = V_{n}$ , Theorem 15.5 reduces to Theorem 12.8." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Therefore the proof given for Theorem 12.8 is valid for any linear space $V$ .",
        "When $V = V_{n}$ , Theorem 15.5 reduces to Theorem 12.8." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.5 is proved by delegation to Theorem 12.8" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.5的证明委派给定理12.8" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-8>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol gives no new argument in 15.07. He observes that in V_n the statement is Theorem 12.8 and then argues that the old proof transfers. So the load-bearing wall of the dimension theory has no proof inside Chapter 15." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-apply-theorem-15-5-to-the-first-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since S is independent and spans V, Theorem 15.5 tells us that every set of $k + 1$ elements in V is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since S is independent and spans V, Theorem 15.5 tells us that every set of $k + 1$ elements in V is dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Applying Theorem 15.5 to S to bound sets of size k+1" ;
    ex:nameZh "对 S 应用定理15.5以界定 k+1 元集" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-independence-clause>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Because S is independent and spans V, Theorem 15.5 applies with L(S) equal to V and yields that every set of k+1 elements of V is dependent. Both clauses of the basis definition are consumed here: independence to satisfy the hypothesis, spanning to identify L(S) with V." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-conclusion-by-antisymmetry> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The same argument with S and T interchanged shows that $k \\leq m$ . Therefore k = m." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The same argument with S and T interchanged shows that $k \\leq m$ . Therefore k = m." ;
    ex:nameEn "Conclusion by antisymmetry of the order on the integers" ;
    ex:nameZh "由整数序的反对称性得出结论" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-first-inequality-m-at-most-k>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-symmetry-step-requires-t-to-span> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "From m at most k and k at most m the equality k = m follows. The only input to this final step is the antisymmetry of the ordering of the integers; the two inequalities it consumes are each produced by Theorem 15.5, applied once in each direction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-first-inequality-m-at-most-k> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since T is an independent set, we must have $m \\leq k$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since T is an independent set, we must have $m \\leq k$ .",
        "Therefore, every set of more than k elements in V is dependent. Since T is an independent set, we must have $m \\leq k$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "First inequality: m is at most k" ;
    ex:nameZh "第一个不等式:m 不超过 k" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-upgrade-from-k-plus-one-to-more-than-k> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "T is independent, so T cannot have more than k elements, giving m at most k. Only the independence half of T being a basis is used at this point; its spanning property is not needed yet." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-symmetry-step-requires-t-to-span> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The same argument with S and T interchanged shows that $k \\leq m$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The same argument with S and T interchanged shows that $k \\leq m$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The symmetry step is where T being a spanning set is consumed" ;
    ex:nameZh "对称步骤正是用到 T 张成 V 的地方" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Interchanging S and T gives k at most m, but this reuse is legitimate only because T also spans V, so that Theorem 15.5 can be applied with T in the role of S. The phrase the same argument hides the second, and only, use of T being a spanning set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-upgrade-from-k-plus-one-to-more-than-k> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore, every set of more than k elements in V is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Therefore, every set of more than k elements in V is dependent.",
        "every set of $k + 1$ elements in V is dependent. Therefore, every set of more than k elements in V is dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Upgrading from exactly k+1 elements to more than k elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "从恰好 k+1 个元升级到多于 k 个元" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependence-inherited-by-supersets>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-6-apply-theorem-15-5-to-the-first-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.5 speaks of k+1 elements, but the proof needs every set of more than k elements. The upgrade is silent in the text and rests on the fact that a set containing a dependent subset is dependent, which is Example 1 of 15.07." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7-hypothesis-dim-v-equals-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ . Then we have the following:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ . Then we have the following:" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.7 hypothesis: V is finite dimensional of dimension n" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.7的前提:V 有限维且维数为 n" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Both parts are stated under a fixed dimension n. Part (b) is meaningless without it, since the number n in n independent elements refers to the dimension and to nothing else." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7a-independent-sets-extend-to-bases> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) Any set of independent elements in $V$ is a subset of some basis for $V$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) Any set of independent elements in $V$ is a subset of some basis for $V$ .",
        "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V.",
        "The proof of (a) is identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.7(a): every independent set is contained in a basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.7(a):每个独立集都含于某个基中" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-n-independent-elements-form-a-basis> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-b>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:adjoining-an-element-outside-the-span-preserves-independence> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Part (a) asserts an extension property: an independent set, of any size up to n, sits inside some basis for V. The assertion is existential in the basis and says nothing about which basis or how to find it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-argument-adjoining-an-outside-element-breaks-the-count> a ex:Node ;
    ex:nameEn "Why n independent elements must span: adjoining an outside element breaks the count" ;
    ex:nameZh "n 个独立元为何必然张成:添加界外元素会突破计数上界" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol delegates the proof of 15.7(b) to Theorem 12.10 and gives no argument in 15.08. This is the standard reconstruction from the chapter-internal ingredients only." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-n-independent-elements-form-a-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:n-plus-one-elements-in-an-n-dimensional-space-are-dependent> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "If n independent elements failed to span V, some element of V would lie outside their span; adjoining it would produce n+1 independent elements of V, contradicting the fact that every n+1 elements are dependent. The argument is a proof by contradiction whose contradiction point is a cardinality bound." .

<urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-bound-cross-terms-by-cauchy-schwarz> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The sum $(x,y) + (\\overline{x,y})$ is real. The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that $|(x,y)| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ and $|(\\overline{x,y})| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ , so we have",
        "\\| x + y \\| ^ {2} \\leq \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} + 2 \\| x \\| \\| y \\| = (\\| x \\| + \\| y \\|) ^ {2}." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The sum $(x,y) + (\\overline{x,y})$ is real. The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that $|(x,y)| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ and $|(\\overline{x,y})| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ , so we have",
        "\\| x + y \\| ^ {2} \\leq \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} + 2 \\| x \\| \\| y \\| = (\\| x \\| + \\| y \\|) ^ {2}." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 3: bound the cross terms with Cauchy-Schwarz and complete the square" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第三步:用 Cauchy-Schwarz 控制交叉项并配成完全平方" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-in-norm-form>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:cross-term-sum-is-real>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-expand-norm-squared> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol applies the norm form of Cauchy-Schwarz to both cross terms, |(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y|| and the same for the conjugate, obtaining ||x + y||^2 <= ||x||^2 + ||y||^2 + 2||x|| ||y|| = (||x|| + ||y||)^2, then takes square roots. This is the exact junction at which the triangle inequality depends on Cauchy-Schwarz: without the bound, the expansion of step 1 gives no inequality at all." .

<urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-statement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) $\\|x + y\\| \\leq \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$ (triangle inequality)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(d) $\\|x + y\\| \\leq \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$ (triangle inequality).",
        "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that",
        "This proves (d). When $y = cx$ , where $c > 0$ , we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "Property (d): the triangle inequality ||x + y|| <= ||x|| + ||y||" ;
    ex:nameZh "性质 (d):三角不等式 ||x + y|| <= ||x|| + ||y||" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-bound-cross-terms-by-cauchy-schwarz>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-expand-norm-squared> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.9(d): ||x + y|| <= ||x|| + ||y||. It is the only one of the four norm properties whose proof leaves the axioms and calls a theorem, and the theorem it calls is Cauchy-Schwarz. Its proof also silently needs additivity in the FIRST argument, which is not an axiom but a consequence of additivity (2) plus symmetry (1)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-of-components-apply-independence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i",
        "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i, so we have $(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{n})=(d_{1},\\ldots,d_{n})$ .",
        "we find that $\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}-d_{i})e_{i}=O$ . But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i" ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness proof step 3: independence forces the differences to vanish" ;
    ex:nameZh "唯一性证明第三步:独立性迫使差为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:uniqueness-of-components> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-independence-clause>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:independence-vanishing-combination-forces-vanishing-coefficients>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-of-components-subtract-to-get-a-vanishing-relation> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Since the basis elements are independent, the vanishing relation forces every c_i minus d_i to be zero, so the two coefficient tuples coincide. Uniqueness therefore consumes only the independence clause of the basis definition, while existence consumed only the spanning clause." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-of-components-subtract-to-get-a-vanishing-relation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "then by subtraction from (15.4), we find that $\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}-d_{i})e_{i}=O$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "then by subtraction from (15.4), we find that $\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(c_{i}-d_{i})e_{i}=O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness proof step 2: subtract to obtain a vanishing relation" ;
    ex:nameZh "唯一性证明第二步:相减得到一个为零的关系" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:uniqueness-of-components> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Subtracting the two expansions yields a combination of the basis elements with coefficients c_i minus d_i equal to O. This is the move that converts a uniqueness question into an independence question, and it uses only the linear space axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-reduces-to-a-single-vanishing-difference> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "From (15.19), we have $s - t = t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ , so we need only prove that $s - t = O$ .",
        "x = s + s ^ {\\perp} \\quad \\text { and } \\quad x = t + t ^ {\\perp},\\tag{15.19}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "From (15.19), we have $s - t = t^{\\perp} - s^{\\perp}$ , so we need only prove that $s - t = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness reduces to proving s - t = O" ;
    ex:nameZh "唯一性化归为证明 s - t = O" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-uniqueness> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Subtracting the two representations gives s - t = t perp - s perp, so both equalities follow from the single claim s - t = O. The reduction is what makes one argument suffice for both components." .

<urn:studium:node:d:uniqueness-splits-the-competitor-into-span-plus-multiple> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore, assume (c) is true for $k = r$ and consider the element $y_{r + 1}'$ . Because of (b), this element is in",
        "where $z_{r} \\in L(y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{r})$ . We wish to prove that $z_{r} = O$ .",
        "y _ {r + 1} ^ {\\prime} = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {r + 1} c _ {i} y _ {i} = z _ {r} + c _ {r + 1} y _ {r + 1}," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Because of (b), this element is in",
        "Finally we prove (c) by induction on $k$ . The case $k = 1$ is trivial." ;
    ex:nameEn "Splitting a competing y'_{r+1} as z_r + c_{r+1} y_{r+1}" ;
    ex:nameZh "把竞争者 y'_{r+1} 拆成 z_r + c_{r+1} y_{r+1}" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-b-spans-agree> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "In the induction proving (c), property (b) puts y'_{r+1} in L(y_1,...,y_{r+1}), so it can be written as z_r + c_{r+1} y_{r+1} with z_r in L(y_1,...,y_r). Uniqueness up to a scalar then reduces to the single claim z_r = O." .

<urn:studium:node:d:x-minus-t-splits-orthogonally> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since $s - t \\in S$ and $x - s = s^{-} \\in S^{\\perp}$ , this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$ , so its norm is given by the Pythagorean formula",
        "Then, for any $t$ in $S$ , we have" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "By Theorem 15.15 we can write $x = s + s^{\\perp}$ , where $s \\in S$ and $s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$ .",
        "Since $s - t \\in S$ and $x - s = s^{-} \\in S^{\\perp}$ , this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$ ,",
        "this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$ ," ;
    ex:nameEn "x - t splits as (x - s) + (s - t), an orthogonal decomposition" ;
    ex:nameZh "x - t 拆成 (x - s) + (s - t) 且这是正交分解" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:approximation-inequality-with-equality-only-at-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:approximation-proof-starts-from-the-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "For any t in S, write x - t = (x - s) + (s - t); here s - t lies in S while x - s = s perp lies in S perp, so this is again an orthogonal decomposition. Recognizing it as such is the step that licenses the Pythagorean formula." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-orthogonal-to-every-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V" ;
    ex:nameZh "零元与 V 中每个元素正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-has-zero-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's first remark after the definition: the zero element is orthogonal to every element of V. This is exactly the corollary (O, y) = 0 of the homogeneity axiom with c = 0, restated in the language of orthogonality. It is also the reason Theorem 15.10 must add a nonzero hypothesis: adjoining O to an orthogonal set leaves it orthogonal, yet makes the enlarged set dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-polynomial-is-included-by-convention> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(Whenever we consider this set it is understood that the zero polynomial is also included.)" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(Whenever we consider this set it is understood that the zero polynomial is also included.)" ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero polynomial is included in the degree-at-most-n set by convention" ;
    ex:nameZh "约定:零多项式属于次数不超过 n 的集合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-zero-element-obtained-by-taking-the-scalar-zero> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-7-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-zero-is-the-everywhere-zero-function> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol states explicitly that whenever this set is considered, the zero polynomial is understood to be a member. The stipulation is not cosmetic: the zero polynomial is the element O demanded by Axiom 5, and it is also 0p for any member p, so excluding it would break Axiom 2 and Axiom 5 at once." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-space-declared-finite-dimensional-by-fiat> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is empty, we define $L(S)$ to be $\\{O\\}$ , the set consisting of the zero element alone.",
        "or if V consists of O alone. Otherwise V is called infinite dimensional." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of O alone.",
        "or if V consists of O alone. Otherwise V is called infinite dimensional." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero space is declared finite dimensional separately" ;
    ex:nameZh "零空间被单列宣告为有限维" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:independence-of-the-empty-set> ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "15.07",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The clause or if V consists of O alone is a second disjunct, not a consequence of the first. Formally it is redundant: by Example 4 the empty set is independent, and by the convention of 15.06 it spans {O}, so the empty set already is a finite basis for the zero space. Apostol lists the case separately so that the definition need not lean on those two boundary conventions." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:ata-invertible-iff-independent-columns> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A is invertible if and only if A has linearly independent columns.",
        "When A has independent columns, $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A$ is square, symmetric, and invertible." ;
    ex:nameEn "A^T A is invertible iff A has independent columns" ;
    ex:nameZh "A^T A 可逆当且仅当 A 的列线性无关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "A^T A is invertible if and only if A has linearly independent columns; A^T A has the same nullspace as A, and when A has independent columns A^T A is square, symmetric, and invertible." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:basis-of-rn-from-invertible-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The columns of every invertible n by n matrix give a basis for $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$",
        "The only solution to $A x = \\mathbf { 0 }$ is $\\pmb { x } = \\pmb { A } ^ { - 1 } \\mathbf { 0 } = \\mathbf { 0 }$ . The columns are independent.",
        "The vectors $v _ { 1 } , \\ldots , v _ { n }$ are a basis for $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ exactly when they are the columns of an n by n invertible matrix." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The only solution to $A x = \\mathbf { 0 }$ is $\\pmb { x } = \\pmb { A } ^ { - 1 } \\mathbf { 0 } = \\mathbf { 0 }$ . The columns are independent.",
        "The vectors $v _ { 1 } , \\ldots , v _ { n }$ are a basis for $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ exactly when they are the columns of an n by n invertible matrix." ;
    ex:nameEn "Columns of an invertible matrix are a basis for R^n" ;
    ex:nameZh "可逆矩阵的列构成 R^n 的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:right-count-upgrades-one-basis-property-to-both> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:independent-columns>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:spanning-a-space> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The vectors v1,...,vn are a basis for R^n exactly when they are the columns of an n by n invertible matrix. Then Ax = 0 forces x = 0 (independence) and Ax = b is always solvable by x = A^{-1}b (spanning), so R^n has infinitely many different bases." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:counting-theorem> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "( dimension of column space) + ( dimension of nullspace) = dimension of $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$",
        "The counting rule for A was $r + ( n - r ) = n$",
        "There are $n - r$ free variables, so the dimension of the nullspace is $n - r$ . This is the Counting Theorem" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "There are $n - r$ free variables, so the dimension of the nullspace is $n - r$ . This is the Counting Theorem" ;
    ex:nameEn "Counting Theorem: r + (n - r) = n" ;
    ex:nameZh "计数定理" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:special-solutions-basis-for-nullspace> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "There are n - r free variables, so the dimension of the nullspace is n - r: (dimension of column space) + (dimension of nullspace) = dimension of R^n. The counting rule for A^T is r + (m - r) = m." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:elimination-preserves-row-space-and-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "1 A has the same row space as R. Same dimension rand same basis.",
        "3 A has the same nullspace as R Same dimension $n - r$ and same basis",
        "Reason: Every row of A is a combination of the rows of R. Also every row of R is a combination of the rows of A. Elimination changes rows, but not row spaces." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "4 Elimination often changes the column space and left nullspace (but dimensions don't change).",
        "Reason: Every row of A is a combination of the rows of R. Also every row of R is a combination of the rows of A. Elimination changes rows, but not row spaces." ;
    ex:nameEn "Elimination changes rows but not the row space or nullspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "消元不改变行空间与零空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:pivot-columns-basis-for-column-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "A has the same row space as R and the same nullspace as R, with the same dimensions and the same bases, because every row of A is a combination of the rows of R and conversely, and the elimination steps do not change the solutions of Ax = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:error-perpendicular-to-a> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The error $e = \\pmb { b } - \\pmb { p }$ is perpendicular to a: Right triangle b p e has",
        "The error e should be perpendicular to" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "It is perpendicular to a-this will determine x.",
        "The error $e = \\pmb { b } - \\pmb { p }$ is perpendicular to a: Right triangle b p e has" ;
    ex:nameEn "error is perpendicular to the line" ;
    ex:nameZh "误差与直线垂直" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-coefficient-x-hat> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:error-vector> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The error e = b - p is perpendicular to a. The right triangle b, p, e satisfies ||p||^2 + ||e||^2 = ||b||^2." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:four-possibilities-by-rank> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The four possibilities for linear equations depend on the rank r",
        "which depend on the rank. Notice how r, m, n are the critical numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "Four possibilities for Ax = b by rank" ;
    ex:nameZh "按秩分的四种可能" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:full-column-rank>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:full-row-rank>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:solvability-condition> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "The number of solutions to Ax = b depends on r, m, n: r = m = n gives 1 solution, r = m < n gives infinitely many, r = n < m gives 0 or 1, and r < m with r < n gives 0 or infinitely many." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:fundamental-theorem-part-2> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$N ( A )$ is the orthogonal complement of the row space $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$",
        "This perpendicularity of subspaces is Part 2 of the Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$N ( A )$ is the orthogonal complement of the row space $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$",
        "$N ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ is the orthogonal complement of the column space $C ( A )$",
        "The point of \"complements\" is that every x can be split into a row space component",
        "This perpendicularity of subspaces is Part 2 of the Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra." ;
    ex:nameEn "Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra, Part 2" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性代数基本定理第二部分" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:column-space-orthogonal-to-left-nullspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space-orthogonal-to-nullspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:vector-splitting-row-null> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "N(A) is the orthogonal complement of the row space C(A^T) in R^n, and N(A^T) is the orthogonal complement of the column space C(A) in R^m. This is the perpendicularity of the four subspaces." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:left-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "4. The left nullspace is $N(A^{\\mathrm{T}})$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{m}$.",
        "The vectors y go on the $l e f t$ side of A when the equation is written",
        "The y's in the left nulspace combine the rows to give the zero row." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "4. The left nullspace is $N(A^{\\mathrm{T}})$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{m}$. This is our new space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Left nullspace N(A^T)" ;
    ex:nameZh "左零空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:four-fundamental-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The left nullspace of A is the nullspace of A transpose: the solutions y of A^T y = 0, a subspace of R^m. Written y^T A = 0^T the vectors y sit on the left of A and combine the rows of A to give the zero row." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:linear-dependence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A sequence of vectors is either dependent or independent.",
        "If a combination gives 0, when the x's are not all zero, the vectors are dependent.",
        "Note Another way to describe linear dependence is this: \"One vector is a combination of the other vectors.\"" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A sequence of vectors is either dependent or independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Linear dependence" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:zero-element-destroys-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Vectors are dependent when some combination with x's not all zero gives the zero vector; equivalently one vector is a combination of the other vectors. A sequence of vectors is either dependent or independent." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "This orthogonal subspace is denoted by $V ^ { \\perp }$ (pronounced \"V perp\")." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "This orthogonal subspace is denoted by $V ^ { \\perp }$ (pronounced \"V perp\")." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal complement notation V perp" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交补记号 V perp" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The orthogonal complement of a subspace V is denoted V^perp, pronounced \"V perp\"." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:pivot-rows-basis-for-row-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "1. The r pivot rows of Rare a basis for the row spaces of Rand A (same space).",
        "So the r pivot rows are a basis for the row space.",
        "The pivot rows of A are a basis for its row space. So are the pivot rows of its echelon form R." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "1. The r pivot rows of Rare a basis for the row spaces of Rand A (same space).",
        "The pivot rows of A are a basis for its row space. So are the pivot rows of its echelon form R." ;
    ex:nameEn "The r pivot rows are a basis for the row space" ;
    ex:nameZh "r 个主元行构成行空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:elimination-preserves-row-space-and-nullspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ex:section "3.4",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The r pivot rows of R are a basis for the row space of R and of A (the same space): the nonzero rows of R are independent because in the pivot columns they contain the r by r identity matrix, and they span the row space. The dimension of the row space is r." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-matrix-symmetric-idempotent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Projecting a second time doesn't change anything, so $P ^ { 2 } = P$",
        "has $P ^ { \\mathrm { T } } = P$ and $P ^ { 2 } = P$ and $P b = p$" ;
    ex:nameEn "projection matrix is symmetric and idempotent" ;
    ex:nameZh "投影矩阵对称且幂等" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection matrix satisfies P^T = P and P^2 = P: projecting a second time does not change the result." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-of-vector-onto-line-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection of b onto the line through a is $\\pmb { p } = \\pmb { a } \\widehat { \\pmb { x } } = \\pmb { a } ( \\pmb { a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } \\pmb { b } / a ^ { \\mathrm { T } } \\pmb { a } )$",
        "The projection of b onto the line through a is the vector" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Our formula ${ \\widehat { \\pmb x } } = { \\pmb a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb b } / { \\pmb a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb a }$ gives the projection",
        "These three steps will lead to all projection matrices: find ${ \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } ,$ , then find the vector" ;
    ex:nameEn "projection formula p = (a^T b / a^T a) a" ;
    ex:nameZh "直线投影公式 p = (a^T b / a^T a) a" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-coefficient-x-hat> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection of b onto the line through a is the vector p = x-hat a = (a^T b / a^T a) a." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:rank-one-projection-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection matrix $P$ is $m$ by $m ,$ , but its rank is one.",
        "The rank one projection matrix $P = { { a } { { a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } } / { { a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } } }$ a multiplies b to produce $p .$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "These three steps will lead to all projection matrices: find ${ \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } ,$ , then find the vector $^ { p , }$ then find the matrix $P _ { - }$" ;
    ex:nameEn "rank-one projection matrix P = a a^T / a^T a" ;
    ex:nameZh "秩一投影矩阵 P = a a^T / a^T a" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-matrix>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection matrix onto the line through a is P = a a^T / a^T a: a column times a row divided by the number a^T a. P is m by m but has rank one." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:row-space-orthogonal-to-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The nulspace $N ( A )$ and the row space $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ are orthogonal subspaces of",
        "The row space is perpendicular to the nullspace. Every row of A is perpendicular to every solution of" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The nulspace $N ( A )$ and the row space $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ are orthogonal subspaces of",
        "Two pairs of orthogonal subspaces." ;
    ex:nameEn "Row space is orthogonal to the nullspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "行空间与零空间正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-vectors> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:column-space-orthogonal-to-left-nullspace> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The row space C(A^T) and the nullspace N(A) are orthogonal subspaces of R^n. Every row of A is perpendicular to every solution of Ax = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:special-solutions-basis-for-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Reason: There is a special solution for each free variable.",
        "The next section shows that the $n - r$ special solutions always give a basis for the nullspace.",
        "The nullspace has dimension $n - r$ . The special solutions form a basis" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The nullspace has dimension $n - r$ . The special solutions form a basis" ;
    ex:nameEn "The n - r special solutions are a basis for the nullspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "n-r 个特解构成零空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis> ;
    ex:section "3.4",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "There is one special solution for each free variable, so with n variables and r pivots there are n - r of them. They are independent because they contain the identity matrix in the free rows, so they form a basis for the nullspace, which has dimension n - r." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:zero-vector-in-two-orthogonal-subspaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If a vector v is orthogonal to itself then v is the zero vector.",
        "When a vector is in two orthogonal subspaces, it must be zero. It is perpendicular to itself." ;
    ex:nameEn "Only the zero vector lies in two orthogonal subspaces" ;
    ex:nameZh "两个正交子空间只共有零向量" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "When a vector is in two orthogonal subspaces it must be zero, since it is perpendicular to itself. A vector orthogonal to itself is the zero vector." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-absolute-value-of-the-dot-product-breaks-homogeneity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\left| \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} y _ {i} \\right|.",
        "In each case, determine whether $(x, y)$ is an inner product for $V_{n}$ if $(x, y)$ is defined by the formula given." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\left| \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} y _ {i} \\right|." ;
    ex:nameEn "Counterexample: the absolute value of the dot product is not an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "反例:点积取绝对值后不是内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "The formula (x, y) = |sum x_i y_i| is symmetric and strictly positive on nonzero elements, so it passes axioms 1 and 4, but it is not an inner product: homogeneity fails for negative scalars, because (cx, y) = |c| |sum x_i y_i|, and additivity fails because |a + b| is not |a| + |b| in general: for n = 1 with x = 1, y = 1, z = -1 one gets (x, y + z) = 0 while (x, y) + (x, z) = 2. Wrapping a genuine inner product in an absolute value therefore destroys it." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-metric-criteria-for-orthogonality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "4. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + y\\|^2 = \\| x\\|^2 + \\| y\\|^2$ .",
        "6. $(x + y, x - y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x \\| = \\| y \\|$ .",
        "Prove that each of the statements in Exercises 3 through 7 is valid for all elements $x$ and $y$ in a real Euclidean space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "3. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + y\\| = \\| x - y\\|$ .",
        "4. $(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $\\| x + y\\|^2 = \\| x\\|^2 + \\| y\\|^2$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Purely metric criteria that detect orthogonality" ;
    ex:nameZh "纯度量条件即可检出正交性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-is-vanishing-of-the-inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:pythagorean-law-orthogonality> ;
    ns1:equivalent <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "In a real Euclidean space, Exercises 3, 4 and 6 give conditions that mention only norms yet are equivalent to the vanishing of an inner product: (x, y) = 0 exactly when ||x + y|| = ||x - y||, and exactly when ||x + y||^2 = ||x||^2 + ||y||^2, while (x + y, x - y) = 0 exactly when ||x|| = ||y||. Orthogonality is therefore recoverable, in the real case, from the norm alone, which is the abstract form of the rhombus and Pythagoras pictures." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:distance-between-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm $\\|x - y\\|$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm $\\|x - y\\|$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Distance between two elements of a Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "欧几里得空间中两元素间的距离" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm ||x - y||. This turns the norm of 15.10 into the quantity minimized in the approximation problem." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-chapter-12-finite-dependence-definition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is a finite set, the foregoing definition agrees with that given in Chapter 12 for the space $V_{n}$ .",
        "These ideas were encountered in Chapter 12 in our study of the vector space $V_{n}$ . Now we extend them to general linear spaces." ;
    ex:nameEn "The Chapter 12 definition of dependence and independence for finite sets in V_n" ;
    ex:nameZh "第12章中 V_n 内有限集的相依与独立定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.06/15.07的引用语。第12章原文不在手上,故不写出第12章的定义原文,也不核对两定义是否真的在有限集上一致 —— 第15章只是断言一致。" ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Chapter 15 does not import this definition; it replaces it and then asserts compatibility. 15.06 announces that dependence, independence, bases and dimension were encountered in Chapter 12 for V_n and are now being extended, and 15.07 gives a fresh definition and claims that on finite sets it agrees with the Chapter 12 one, the new definition being unrestricted as to finiteness. The agreement is asserted, not verified anywhere in Chapter 15." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-b> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The proof of (a) is identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Part (b) of Theorem 12.10 (Chapter 12), imported as the proof of Theorem 15.7(a)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理12.10 的 (b) 部分(第12章),作为定理15.7(a) 的证明被引入" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.08的引用语。第12章原文不在手上,故不陈述定理12.10(b) 的原始内容;此处只记录第15章向它索取的东西是 15.7(a) 的全部证明。" ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "15.08 needs the PROOF of this external part and nothing else: the proof of Theorem 15.7(a), that any independent set in V is a subset of some basis for V, is declared identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10. Chapter 15 supplies no argument for 15.7(a); the citation is the entire proof. Chapter 15 does not restate what part (b) of Theorem 12.10 says." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-c> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The proof of (b) is identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Part (c) of Theorem 12.10 (Chapter 12), imported as the proof of Theorem 15.7(b)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理12.10 的 (c) 部分(第12章),作为定理15.7(b) 的证明被引入" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.08的引用语。第12章原文不在手上,故不陈述定理12.10(c) 的原始内容。第15章只说 (b) 的证明与它相同,并未说 12.10 共有几部分或其余部分讲什么。" ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "15.08 needs the PROOF of this external part and nothing else: the proof of Theorem 15.7(b), that any set of n independent elements is a basis for V, is declared identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10. Chapter 15 supplies no argument for 15.7(b). Chapter 15 does not restate what part (c) of Theorem 12.10 says." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-2> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "the proof was a consequence of the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2 and did not depend on the particular definition used to deduce these properties" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 12.2 (Chapter 12), the list of dot-product properties the imported proof consumes" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理12.2(第12章),被引入的证明所消耗的点积性质清单" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.10的引用语。第12章原文不在手上:第15章只说定理12.2 列出了点积的若干性质,并未逐条列出它们,故本节点不枚举这些性质,也不断言它们与15.10 的四条内积公理逐条对应。" ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "15.10 needs this external result only as the premise set of the proof it imports: Apostol's ground for transplanting the Theorem 12.3 proof is that the proof was a consequence of the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2 and did not depend on the particular definition used to deduce these properties. Chapter 15 does not cite these properties as established facts; it instead re-declares properties of this kind as the inner-product axioms of 15.10." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-3> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore, the very same proof is valid in any real Euclidean space.",
        "When we proved the corresponding result for vectors in $V_{n}$ (Theorem 12.3), we were careful to point out that" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "the proof was a consequence of the properties of the dot product listed in Theorem 12.2 and did not depend on the particular definition used to deduce these properties" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 12.3 (Chapter 12), the V_n Cauchy-Schwarz result whose proof Theorem 15.8 reuses" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理12.3(第12章),定理15.8 复用其证明的 V_n 情形结果" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.10的引用语。第12章原文不在手上,故不陈述定理12.3 的原始表述;此处只记录第15章向它索取整个实情形证明,并自行补上复情形的一步。" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-2> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "15.10 needs the PROOF of this external result. Apostol calls it the corresponding result for vectors in V_n and reuses the very same proof for any real Euclidean space, on the stated ground that the original proof consumed only the dot-product properties listed in Theorem 12.2 and not the component definition. Chapter 15 writes no proof of Theorem 15.8 in the real case; it adds only the extra step carrying the argument into a complex Euclidean space." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-8> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore the proof given for Theorem 12.8 is valid for any linear space $V$ .",
        "When $V = V_{n}$ , Theorem 15.5 reduces to Theorem 12.8." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 12.8 (Chapter 12), imported as the V_n case of Theorem 15.5" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理12.8(第12章),作为定理15.5在 V_n 中的特例被引入" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "本节点的内容边界只来自第15章15.07的引用语。我们手上没有第12章原文,故此处不替 Apostol 陈述定理12.8 的完整内容;只记录第15章在此处需要它的哪一部分(整个证明)以及第15章未重证这一事实。" ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "What 15.07 needs from this external theorem is its PROOF, not merely its statement: Apostol writes no argument of his own for Theorem 15.5. He states that when V = V_n Theorem 15.5 reduces to Theorem 12.8, then certifies that the imported proof uses only the linear-space property of V_n, and declares it valid for any linear space V. Chapter 15 neither restates nor reproves Theorem 12.8." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-vn-as-a-prior-vector-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "These ideas were encountered in Chapter 12 in our study of the vector space $V_{n}$ . Now we extend them to general linear spaces.",
        "the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components" ;
    ex:nameEn "V_n as a vector space already constructed before Chapter 15" ;
    ex:nameZh "V_n:第15章之前已建成的向量空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章的引用语。第12章原文不在手上,故不陈述第12章如何构造 V_n。本节点只记录第15章把 V_n 当既有对象引入这一事实,以及第15章自己未做构造与公理验证。注意:15.03 例3 在 ch15 一侧另有 L1 节点 apostol:v-n-space;本节点记录的是该对象的外部来源身份,不重复表达例3本身,若审计认为二者重合可判 KILL。" ;
    ex:section "15.03",
        "15.06",
        "15.07",
        "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "What Chapter 15 imports here is an object, not a proof. 15.03 introduces V_n already labelled the vector space of all n-tuples, with its operations given only as the usual way in terms of components, and 15.06 names Chapter 12 as where V_n was studied. Chapter 15 never constructs V_n, never states its operations explicitly, and never verifies its axioms; verification of the 15.03 examples is handed to the reader. V_n is then used throughout as the standard instance and the source of intuition." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-volume-2-proof-of-the-legendre-closed-form> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We shall encounter these polynomials again in Volume II in our further study of differential equations, and we shall prove that" ;
    ex:nameEn "The Volume II proof of the closed form for the orthogonalized powers" ;
    ex:nameZh "第II卷中对正交化幂序列闭式的证明" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "source" ;
    ex:originNote "内容边界只来自第15章15.13的引用语。第II卷不在手上,故不陈述那里的证明如何进行;此处只记录第15章把该等式的证明推给了第II卷,而自己已经在用它。" ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "A forward outsourcing rather than a back-reference. Having produced y_0 through y_5 by Gram-Schmidt, 15.13 asserts a closed form for y_n as a constant times the n-th derivative of (t^2 - 1)^n and defers the proof to Volume II. Chapter 15 nevertheless uses that closed form immediately, defining the Legendre polynomials P_n by the same derivative expression, so an unproved identity is load-bearing for a definition in this chapter." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:homogeneous-de-solution-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 12. The set of all solutions of a homogeneous linear differential equation $y'' + ay' + by = 0$ , where a and b are given constants.",
        "The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following sets is a function space.",
        "The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "Solution space of a homogeneous linear differential equation" ;
    ex:nameZh "齐次线性微分方程的解空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:complete-solution> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Example 12: the set of all solutions of the homogeneous equation y'' + ay' + by = 0, with a and b given constants, is a function space. The 0 on the right is essential; the solution set of a nonhomogeneous equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomial-approximation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 2. Approximation of continuous functions on $[-1, 1]$ by polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ .",
        "This is the polynomial of degree $\\leq n$ for which the norm $\\| f - f_n\\|$ is smallest.",
        "span a subspace S of dimension $n + 1$ consisting of all polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $V = C(-1, 1)$ , the space of real continuous functions on $[-1, 1]$",
        "This is the polynomial of degree $\\leq n$ for which the norm $\\| f - f_n\\|$ is smallest.",
        "let $(f, g) = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(x)g(x) dx$ . The $n + 1$ normalized Legendre polynomials",
        "normalized Legendre polynomials $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\ldots, \\varphi_n$ , introduced in Section 15.13, span a subspace S of dimension",
        "span a subspace S of dimension $n + 1$ consisting of all polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Approximation of continuous functions on [-1, 1] by polynomials of degree at most n" ;
    ex:nameZh "用次数不超过 n 的多项式逼近 [-1, 1] 上的连续函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalized-legendre-polynomials>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "Application instance (Example 2) of the Approximation Theorem: in V = C(-1, 1) with (f, g) = integral_{-1}^{1} f g, the n + 1 normalized Legendre polynomials span the subspace of all polynomials of degree <= n, and the projection f_n = sum_{k=0}^{n} (f, phi_k) phi_k is the polynomial of degree <= n minimizing ||f - f_n||." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:normalized-legendre-polynomials> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The polynomials in the corresponding orthonormal sequence $\\varphi_{0}$ , $\\varphi_{1}$ , $\\varphi_{2}$ , $\\ldots$",
        "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The polynomials in the corresponding orthonormal sequence $\\varphi_{0}$ , $\\varphi_{1}$ , $\\varphi_{2}$ , $\\ldots$",
        "are known as the Legendre polynomials. The polynomials in the corresponding orthonormal sequence",
        "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." ;
    ex:nameEn "Normalized Legendre polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "规范化 Legendre 多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomials>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The orthonormal sequence phi_0, phi_1, phi_2, ... given by phi_n = y_n/||y_n||, obtained by dividing each orthogonalized polynomial by its norm; for instance phi_0(t) = 1/sqrt 2 and phi_1(t) = sqrt(3/2) t." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:ordered-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $V$ be a linear space of dimension $n$ and consider a basis whose elements $e_1, \\ldots, e_n$ are taken in a given order.",
        "We denote such an ordered basis as an $n$ -tuple $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "consider a basis whose elements $e_1, \\ldots, e_n$ are taken in a given order." ;
    ex:nameEn "Ordered basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "有序基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "In a linear space of dimension n one may consider a basis whose elements e_1, ..., e_n are taken in a given order; such an ordered basis is denoted as the n-tuple (e_1, ..., e_n)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:real-euclidean-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A real linear space with an inner product is called a real Euclidean space.",
        "Therefore, the very same proof is valid in any real Euclidean space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A real linear space with an inner product is called a real Euclidean space.",
        "Although we are interested primarily in examples of real Euclidean spaces, the theorems of this chapter are valid for complex Euclidean spaces as well." ;
    ex:nameEn "Real Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "实欧几里得空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "A real linear space with an inner product is called a real Euclidean space. These are the examples Apostol is primarily interested in, and proofs given for V_n carry over verbatim to any real Euclidean space." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:s-perp-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation S perp for the set of all elements orthogonal to S" ;
    ex:nameZh "记号 S 垂直(与 S 正交的全体元素)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement-notation> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all elements orthogonal to a subset S is denoted by S with a perpendicular superscript and is read 'S perpendicular'. The same symbol is used for the orthogonal complement when S is a subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.1. UNIQUENESS OF THE ZERO ELEMENT. In any linear space there is one and only one zero element." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Axiom 5 tells us that there is at least one zero element.",
        "But $O_1 + O_2 = O_2 + O_1$ because of the commutative law, so $O_1 = O_2$ .",
        "Proof. Axiom 5 tells us that there is at least one zero element.",
        "because of the commutative law, so $O_1 = O_2$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.1: uniqueness of the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.1:零元素的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "In any linear space there is one and only one zero element. The proof uses Axiom 5 for existence and the commutative law to show two candidates O_1 and O_2 must coincide." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:vanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Since $y_{3}=O$ , the three vectors $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$ must be dependent.",
        "Then (15.13) shows that $x_{r+1}$ is a linear combination of $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ , and hence of $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r}$ , so the elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r+1}$ are dependent.",
        "if the first k elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are independent, then the corresponding elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{k}$ are nonzero" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In the foregoing construction, suppose we have $y_{r+1}=O$ for some r.",
        "Then (15.13) shows that $x_{r+1}$ is a linear combination of $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ , and hence of $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r}$ , so the elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{r+1}$ are dependent.",
        "if the first k elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are independent, then the corresponding elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{k}$ are nonzero" ;
    ex:nameEn "A vanishing orthogonalized element detects dependence" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交化结果为零即检出线性相依" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "If the Gram-Schmidt construction yields y_{r+1} = O for some r, then x_{r+1} is a linear combination of y_1, ..., y_r and hence of x_1, ..., x_r, so x_1, ..., x_{r+1} are dependent. Equivalently, if the first k elements x_1, ..., x_k are independent then the corresponding y_1, ..., y_k are all nonzero, so the process doubles as an independence test." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-11-hypotheses-finite-dimension-and-orthogonal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.11. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dimension $n$ , and assume that $S = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$ .",
        "x = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} c _ {i} e _ {i},\\tag{15.7}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.11. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dimension $n$ , and assume that $S = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Hypotheses of Theorem 15.11: finite dimension n and an orthogonal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.11 的前提:有限维 n 与一个正交基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:existence-of-an-orthogonal-basis-deferred> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.11 assumes V finite-dimensional of dimension n and S = {e_1, ..., e_n} an ORTHOGONAL BASIS, and it starts from a given representation x = sum of c_i e_i, Equation (15.7). Note what is not assumed: the e_i are not required to be orthonormal, and their norms are not required to be known. The basis hypothesis is what supplies existence of the representation; the orthogonality hypothesis is what makes the coefficients computable." .

<urn:studium:node:d:an-example-is-a-set-plus-two-explicit-operations> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If we specify the set V and tell how to add its elements and how to multiply them by numbers, we get a concrete example of a linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If we specify the set V and tell how to add its elements and how to multiply them by numbers, we get a concrete example of a linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "An example is produced by naming the set and both operations" ;
    ex:nameZh "给出一个例子=指定集合+两个运算" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:nature-of-the-elements-is-left-unspecified> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "To exhibit a concrete linear space one must supply three data: the set V, a rule for adding two elements, and a rule for multiplying an element by a number. Omitting either rule leaves the axioms with nothing to speak about, which is why the section fixes the operations before the sets: Examples 1 to 3 name their own operations, Example 4 inherits those of V_n, and Examples 5 to 12 share the ones given in the function-space preamble." .

<urn:studium:node:d:angle-cosine-equation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\cos \\theta = \\frac {(x , y)}{\\| x \\| \\| y \\|}.\\tag{15.6}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\cos \\theta = \\frac {(x , y)}{\\| x \\| \\| y \\|}.\\tag{15.6}" ;
    ex:nameEn "The defining equation (15.6) for the angle" ;
    ex:nameZh "角的定义方程 (15.6)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Equation (15.6): cos(theta) = (x, y) / (||x|| ||y||). The angle is not defined directly; it is defined as the solution theta of this equation, which is why two separate well-definedness facts must be checked, namely that the right-hand side exists (nonzero denominator) and that it lies in the range of cosine on [0, pi]." .

<urn:studium:node:d:angle-defined-only-in-real-euclidean-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "In a real Euclidean space V, the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is defined to be that number $\\theta$ in the interval $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ which satisfies the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "The angle is defined only in a REAL Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "角只在实欧氏空间中定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Unlike the norm, which Apostol defines in any Euclidean space, the angle is defined only in a real Euclidean space. The reason is visible in (15.6): in a complex space the numerator (x, y) is a complex number while cos(theta) is real, so the equation has no solution in general. This is the one place in 15.10 where the real and complex cases genuinely diverge in what can be defined, rather than merely in how it is proved." .

<urn:studium:node:d:angle-requires-nonzero-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is defined to be that number" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In a real Euclidean space V, the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is defined to be that number $\\theta$ in the interval $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ which satisfies the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "The angle is defined only for nonzero elements, so that the denominator exists" ;
    ex:nameZh "角只对非零元素定义,以保证分母有定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:norm-positivity-property> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The definition restricts x and y to nonzero elements. This is not a convenience: the denominator ||x|| ||y|| of (15.6) is nonzero precisely because of Theorem 15.9(b), hence ultimately because of the positivity axiom, so for x = O or y = O the defining equation has no right-hand side at all." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-states-the-zero-only-as-a-right-neutral-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "x + O = x \\quad f o r a l l x i n V.",
        "y _ {2} + (x + y _ {1}) = (y _ {2} + x) + y _ {1} = O + y _ {1} = y _ {1} + O = y _ {1}." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that",
        "x + O = x \\quad f o r a l l x i n V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 5 states the zero only as a right neutral element" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理5只把零元写成右中性元" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom 5 asserts x + O = x and says nothing about O + x. The two-sided property, used freely from Theorem 15.1 onward, is supplied by Axiom 3, so the commutative law is doing quiet work every time O is added on the left." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-in-norm-form> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "When the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is expressed in terms of norms, it becomes",
        "| (x, y) | \\leq \\| x \\| \\| y \\|." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is expressed in terms of norms, it becomes" ;
    ex:nameEn "Cauchy-Schwarz in norm form: |(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y||" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 的范数形式:|(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y||" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-inequality-statement>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Once the norm is defined, taking square roots in (15.7) restates Theorem 15.8 as |(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y||. This is the form actually used twice later: to bound the cross terms in the proof of the triangle inequality, and to place the angle quotient (x, y)/(||x|| ||y||) inside [-1, 1]." .

<urn:studium:node:d:conjugate-homogeneity-in-second-argument> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, c y) = \\overline {{(c y , x)}} = \\bar {c} (\\overline {{y , x}}) = \\bar {c} (x, y).",
        "From the homogeneity axiom and $(1')$ , we get" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, c y) = \\overline {{(c y , x)}} = \\bar {c} (\\overline {{y , x}}) = \\bar {c} (x, y).",
        "From the homogeneity axiom and $(1')$ , we get" ;
    ex:nameEn "Companion relation: (x, cy) = conjugate(c) (x, y)" ;
    ex:nameZh "配套关系:(x, cy) = c 的共轭乘 (x, y)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:complex-homogeneity-allows-complex-scalars>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's 'companion relation', obtained from the homogeneity axiom together with (1'): (x, cy) = conjugate of (cy, x) = conjugate(c) times conjugate of (y, x) = conjugate(c) (x, y). This is the exact point at which changing the symmetry axiom propagates into the homogeneity behaviour: homogeneity is left untouched in the first slot but becomes CONJUGATE homogeneity in the second slot. Nothing about slot two was postulated; it is forced." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cross-term-sum-is-real> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The sum $(x,y) + (\\overline{x,y})$ is real" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The sum $(x,y) + (\\overline{x,y})$ is real" ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 2: the cross-term sum (x, y) + conjugate(x, y) is real" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第二步:交叉项之和 (x, y) + 其共轭为实数" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol records that the sum (x, y) + conjugate of (x, y) is real. This is not decoration: the left side of (d) is real, so the inequality can only be argued if the cross terms are real, and the reason they are real is again (1'), since z + conjugate(z) = 2 Re(z). This is the step that makes the real proof and the complex proof one proof." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-coefficients-not-all-zero> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "and corresponding set of scalars $c_{1}, \\ldots, c_{k}$ , not all zero, such that" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "and corresponding set of scalars $c_{1}, \\ldots, c_{k}$ , not all zero, such that" ;
    ex:nameEn "The coefficients must not all be zero" ;
    ex:nameZh "系数必须不全为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "At least one c_i is required to be nonzero. Drop this clause and every nonempty set, in particular every singleton, becomes dependent, since the all-zero tuple always yields O and the notion would carry no information at all. The empty set is unaffected, because the witnessing family x_1, ..., x_k is understood to have k >= 1, so it has no witness either way." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dependence-finiteness-of-the-witnessing-family> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "is called dependent if there is a finite set of distinct elements in S" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "is called dependent if there is a finite set of distinct elements in S" ;
    ex:nameEn "The witnessing family must be finite" ;
    ex:nameZh "见证族必须是有限的" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The relation exhibiting dependence involves only finitely many elements of S, even when S is infinite. Dropping finiteness would require summing infinitely many terms, which is undefined in a linear space with no topology, so the definition would not even parse." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n-is-n-plus-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The space of all polynomials $p(t)$ of degree $\\leq n$ has dimension $n + 1$ . One basis is the set of $n + 1$ polynomials" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The space of all polynomials $p(t)$ of degree $\\leq n$ has dimension $n + 1$ . One basis is the set of $n + 1$ polynomials" ;
    ex:nameEn "The polynomials of degree at most n have dimension n+1" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数不超过 n 的多项式空间维数为 n+1" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:powers-independence-by-differentiating-and-evaluating-at-zero> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Example 2 counts the basis of powers from the constant through the n-th power, giving n+1 elements. The off-by-one is a standing trap: the degree bound is n but the dimension is n+1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-1-the-real-numbers-form-a-linear-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. Let $V = \\mathbf{R}$ , the set of all real numbers, and let $x + y$ and $ax$ be ordinary addition and multiplication of real numbers." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 1. Let $V = \\mathbf{R}$ , the set of all real numbers, and let $x + y$ and $ax$ be ordinary addition and multiplication of real numbers.",
        "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 1: R itself is a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "例1:实数集 R 自身是线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Taking V = R with ordinary addition and ordinary multiplication gives the smallest interesting example, in which elements and scalars are the same objects. It shows that the linear space axioms do not require the elements to be arrows or tuples, and it is the degenerate case against which the other examples should be read." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-11-functions-vanishing-at-a-point> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 11. The set of all functions f defined at 1 with $f(1)=0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 11. The set of all functions f defined at 1 with $f(1)=0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 11: the functions defined at 1 that vanish there" ;
    ex:nameZh "例11:在 1 处有定义且取值为 0 的函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The set of functions f defined at 1 with f(1) = 0 is a function space: if f(1) = g(1) = 0 then (f+g)(1) = 0 and (af)(1) = 0. The constraint is a linear condition on the value, and that is exactly what closure under the pointwise operations requires." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-2-the-complex-numbers-with-real-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 2. Let V = C, the set of all complex numbers, define $x + y$ to be ordinary addition of complex numbers",
        "Even though the elements of V are complex numbers, this is a real linear space because the scalars are real." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 2. Let V = C, the set of all complex numbers, define $x + y$ to be ordinary addition of complex numbers",
        "Even though the elements of V are complex numbers, this is a real linear space because the scalars are real." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 2: C with real scalars is a real linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "例2:以实数为标量的复数集是实线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Taking V = C with ordinary complex addition, but allowing only real numbers as multipliers, produces a real linear space whose elements are complex. It separates two things the notation tends to fuse, the field the elements come from and the field the scalars come from." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-3-v-n-with-componentwise-operations> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. Let $V = V_{n}$ , the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components.",
        "Other examples are real-valued functions, the complex numbers, infinite series, vectors in n-space, and vector-valued functions." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 3. Let $V = V_{n}$ , the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components.",
        "the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components" ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 3: V_n with componentwise operations" ;
    ex:nameZh "例3:按分量定义运算的 V_n" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-vn-as-a-prior-vector-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The space V_n of n-tuples of real numbers, with the usual componentwise operations, is one of the several familiar examples 15.1 cites as motivating the general axioms. Everything in the axiom list can be read off from it, which makes it a poor test of whether a given axiom is really needed." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-7-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 7. The set of all polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , where n is fixed." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 7. The set of all polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , where n is fixed.",
        "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 7: polynomials of degree at most n, with the zero polynomial included" ;
    ex:nameZh "例7:次数不超过 n 的多项式(含零多项式)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:dim-of-polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n-is-n-plus-one> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For fixed n the polynomials of degree at most n form a function space, because a sum of two such polynomials and a scalar multiple of one both again have degree at most n. Bounding the degree from above is compatible with closure precisely because the bound is an inequality and can be met with room to spare." .

<urn:studium:node:d:examples-are-asserted-to-satisfy-the-axioms-and-left-to-the-reader> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space.",
        "The reader can easily verify that each of the following sets is a function space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "The axiom verifications in this section are left to the reader" ;
    ex:nameZh "本节各例的公理验证留给读者" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol asserts rather than proves that each of the twelve sets satisfies all ten axioms for a real linear space. The omission is safe only because in every example the operations are inherited from the real numbers componentwise or pointwise, so nine axioms are mechanical and closure is the sole item carrying content." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-limit-argument-isolates-one-coefficient> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore, when $x \\to +\\infty$ in Equation (15.3), each term with $k \\neq M$ tends to zero and we find that $c_M = 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $k \\neq M$ , the number $a_k - a_M$ is negative. Therefore, when $x \\to +\\infty$ in Equation (15.3), each term with $k \\neq M$ tends to zero",
        "Therefore, when $x \\to +\\infty$ in Equation (15.3), each term with $k \\neq M$ tends to zero and we find that $c_M = 0$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Non-algebraic input: a limit as x tends to infinity kills all but one term" ;
    ex:nameZh "非代数输入:令 x 趋于无穷的极限论证消去除一项外的全部项" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-multiply-by-a-normalizing-exponential>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-negativity-of-the-shifted-exponents> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Letting x tend to plus infinity makes every term with a negative shifted exponent tend to zero, leaving c_M = 0. This is the decisive step and it is analytic: it uses limits and the decay of the exponential, notions absent from the linear space axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-multiply-by-a-normalizing-exponential> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Multiplying both members of (15.2) by $e^{-a_M x}$ , we obtain" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $a_{M}$ be the largest of the n numbers $a_{1},\\ldots ,a_{n}$ . Multiplying both members of (15.2) by $e^{-a_M x}$ , we obtain",
        "Multiplying both members of (15.2) by $e^{-a_M x}$ , we obtain" ;
    ex:nameEn "Multiplying the relation by the reciprocal of the largest exponential" ;
    ex:nameZh "用最大指数项的倒数乘整个关系" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-select-the-largest-exponent> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Multiplying the relation through by e to the minus a_M x replaces each exponent a_k by the shifted exponent a_k minus a_M. The step is legitimate and reversible because the exponential function never vanishes, and it collapses the M-th term to the constant c_M." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-select-the-largest-exponent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $a_{M}$ be the largest of the n numbers $a_{1},\\ldots ,a_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $a_{M}$ be the largest of the n numbers $a_{1},\\ldots ,a_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Selecting the largest exponent" ;
    ex:nameZh "选取最大的指数" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The step picks a_M to be the largest of the n exponents. This uses the total ordering of the reals and the finiteness of the index set, so a maximum exists; the choice is what makes all the other exponents smaller by a strictly negative amount." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-weight-makes-improper-integral-converge> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f, g) = \\int_ {0} ^ {\\infty} e ^ {- t} f (t) g (t) d t.",
        "Because of the exponential factor, this improper integral converges for every choice of polynomials f and g." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Because of the exponential factor, this improper integral converges for every choice of polynomials f and g." ;
    ex:nameEn "On polynomials, the exponential weight secures convergence" ;
    ex:nameZh "多项式空间上,指数权保证反常积分收敛" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:weight-function-must-be-positive> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Example 5 puts (f, g) = integral from 0 to infinity of e^(-t) f(t) g(t) dt on the space of all real polynomials. Apostol points out that the exponential factor is what makes this improper integral converge for every choice of polynomials. Here the weight does double duty: positivity gives axiom (4), and decay gives well-definedness of the value at all, which on an unbounded interval is a prerequisite for the axioms even to be checkable." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-dimensionality-supplies-a-finite-orthonormal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V.",
        "Since S is finite-dimensional, it has a finite orthonormal basis, say $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since S is finite-dimensional, it has a finite orthonormal basis, say $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The one step that uses finite-dimensionality: S has a finite orthonormal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "有限维假设的唯一使用点:S 有有限正交规范基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The hypothesis that S is finite-dimensional is used exactly once, to invoke Theorem 15.14 and obtain a finite orthonormal basis e_1,...,e_n, which is what makes the finite sum defining s meaningful. The uniqueness half and the Pythagorean formula never use it." .

<urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-lives-on-the-intersection-of-domains> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 9. The set of all functions differentiable at a given point.",
        "for every real x in the intersection of the domains of f and g." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "for every real x in the intersection of the domains of f and g." ;
    ex:nameEn "The sum of two functions is defined only on the intersection of their domains" ;
    ex:nameZh "两函数之和只在定义域交集上有定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The pointwise sum is declared for every real x lying in the intersection of the domains of f and g, so f + g may have a smaller domain than either summand. Unless the set of functions under consideration shares one fixed domain, this shrinking can push f + g out of the set and break Axiom 1, which is why Examples 5, 8, 9, 10 and 11 each fix an interval or a point in advance." .

<urn:studium:node:d:function-space-zero-is-the-everywhere-zero-function> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The zero element is the function whose values are everywhere zero." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The zero element is the function whose values are everywhere zero." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero element of a function space is the everywhere-zero function" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数空间的零元是处处取零的函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-of-a-function-space-is-a-pointwise-identity> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "The element O required by Axiom 5 is realized in a function space by the function whose value is zero at every point of the domain, not by the number 0. A set of functions that happens to exclude this function, such as the polynomials of degree exactly n, cannot be a linear space under the pointwise operations, since Axiom 2 forces 0f = O into the set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:independence-universal-quantifier-over-choices> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In this case, for all choices of distinct elements $x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{k}$ in $S$ and scalars $c_{1},\\ldots ,c_{k}$ ," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In this case, for all choices of distinct elements $x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{k}$ in $S$ and scalars $c_{1},\\ldots ,c_{k}$ ," ;
    ex:nameEn "Independence is a universal statement over all finite subfamilies and all coefficient tuples" ;
    ex:nameZh "独立性是对全部有限子族与全部系数组的全称陈述" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Negating the existential in the dependence definition turns independence into a claim about all choices of distinct elements of S and all scalar tuples. This is why an independence proof must handle an arbitrary relation rather than exhibit one." .

<urn:studium:node:d:infinite-dimensional-operational-criterion-no-finite-spanning-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Although the infinite set $\\{1, t, t^{2}, \\ldots\\}$ spans this space, no finite set of polynomials spans the space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Although the infinite set $\\{1, t, t^{2}, \\ldots\\}$ spans this space, no finite set of polynomials spans the space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Operational criterion: no finite set spans the space" ;
    ex:nameZh "可操作判据:没有有限集能张成该空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:infinite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Example 4 shows the usable form of the criterion. The polynomials are infinite dimensional because although the infinite set of powers spans them, no finite set of polynomials does. The failure is located in spanning, not in independence." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-not-unique-on-a-given-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = 2 x _ {1} y _ {1} + x _ {1} y _ {2} + x _ {2} y _ {1} + x _ {2} y _ {2}.",
        "This example shows that there may be more than one inner product in a given linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 2. If $x = (x_{1}, x_{2})$ and $y = (y_{1}, y_{2})$ are any two vectors in $V_{2}$ , define $(x, y)$ by the formula",
        "This example shows that there may be more than one inner product in a given linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "A linear space can carry more than one inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一线性空间上可有多个内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Example 2 exhibits on V_2 the form (x, y) = 2x_1y_1 + x_1y_2 + x_2y_1 + x_2y_2, which satisfies all the axioms yet differs from the dot product. Apostol draws the conclusion explicitly: there may be more than one inner product in a given linear space. Hence 'Euclidean space' is a linear space together with a chosen inner product, not a property of the linear space alone." .

<urn:studium:node:d:norm-positivity-property> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "Proof. Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product. To prove (d), we note that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Property (b): ||x|| > 0 if x is not the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "性质 (b):x 非零时 ||x|| > 0" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.9(b): ||x|| > 0 if x is not O. This is the one norm property that is equivalent to an axiom rather than derived from several: it is the positivity axiom (x, x) > 0 read through the monotone square root. Consequently it is the property that fails first if positive definiteness is weakened to semi-definiteness, and with it fail the characterization of O by its norm and the nonvanishing of the denominators in the angle formula and in (15.8)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:normalizing-converts-an-orthogonal-basis-into-an-orthonormal-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1.",
        "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." ;
    ex:nameEn "Normalizing turns an orthogonal basis into an orthonormal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "逐个除以范数把正交基变成正交规范基" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Dividing each element of an orthogonal basis by its norm produces an orthonormal basis of the same space. Apostol asserts this in one clause; it silently combines two separate facts, that the rescaled elements have norm 1 and that rescaling does not destroy orthogonality." .

<urn:studium:node:d:operations-of-example-3-are-componentwise> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:nameEn "Operations of Example 3: addition and scaling act in each component" ;
    ex:nameZh "例3的运算:逐分量加法与逐分量数乘" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:example-3-v-n-with-componentwise-operations> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:an-example-is-a-set-plus-two-explicit-operations> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "In V_n the sum of two n-tuples is the n-tuple of componentwise sums and a scalar multiple is obtained by multiplying every component by that scalar. Both operations are therefore n independent copies of arithmetic in R, performed simultaneously." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonality-to-generators-extends-to-their-span> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$ , which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$ .",
        "Therefore, it is orthogonal to every element in the subspace" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "Therefore, it is orthogonal to every element in the subspace",
        "Thus, the element $y_{r+1}$ is well defined and is orthogonal to each of the earlier elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{r}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality to a spanning set extends to the whole span" ;
    ex:nameZh "对生成元正交即对其张成的整个子空间正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-a-orthogonal-to-earlier-span> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:new-element-is-well-defined-and-orthogonal-to-all-earlier-ones> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Being orthogonal to y_1,...,y_r implies being orthogonal to every element of L(y_1,...,y_r), because an arbitrary element of the span is a linear combination of the generators, the inner product is linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second, and the conjugate of 0 is still 0. Apostol uses this bridge twice: here, and again in Theorem 15.15 when passing from the basis elements e_j to all of S." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-expansion-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis, Equation (15.7) can be written in the form",
        "x = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}.\\tag{15.10}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis, Equation (15.7) can be written in the form" ;
    ex:nameEn "The orthonormal expansion (15.10): x = sum of (x, e_i) e_i" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交规范展开 (15.10):x = sum (x, e_i) e_i" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-basis-component-is-the-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Substituting (15.9) into (15.7) rewrites any x in an orthonormal basis as x = sum over i of (x, e_i) e_i. This self-reproducing form, in which the element's own inner products against the basis serve as its coefficients, is the identity that is paired with y to produce Parseval's formula." .

<urn:studium:node:d:parseval-proof-pair-expansion-with-y> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof. Taking the inner product of both members of Equation (15.10) with y and using the linearity property of the inner product, we obtain (15.11)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Taking the inner product of both members of Equation (15.10) with y and using the linearity property of the inner product, we obtain (15.11)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof: pair the expansion (15.10) with y and use linearity" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明:把展开式 (15.10) 与 y 作内积并用线性性" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:additivity-in-first-argument-derived>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:orthonormal-expansion-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Apostol's whole proof of (15.11) is one move: take the inner product of both members of Equation (15.10) with y and use the linearity property of the inner product. This is the third occurrence of the same technique in two sections, after Theorem 15.10 and Theorem 15.11, and it is the technique that orthonormality is designed to enable." .

<urn:studium:node:d:self-inner-product-is-real-in-complex-case> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\overline {{(y , x)}},\\tag{1'}",
        "In a complex linear space, an inner product $(x, y)$ is a complex number satisfying the same axioms as those for a real inner produ" ;
    ex:nameEn "Hermitian symmetry makes (x, x) real, so the positivity axiom still parses" ;
    ex:nameZh "Hermite 对称使 (x, x) 为实数,正定性公理才有意义" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol says the other axioms are kept unchanged but never remarks that (1') is what makes (x, x) real. The anchors give his statement of (1') and of 'same axioms'; the deduction y = x is supplied here." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Setting y = x in (1') gives (x, x) = conjugate of (x, x), so (x, x) is real. This is the hidden step that lets the unchanged positivity axiom '(x, x) > 0 if x is not O' remain meaningful in a complex space, where the inequality sign would otherwise be applied to a non-real number. It is also why (1') and not plain symmetry is the correct replacement." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3d-a-vanishing-product-forces-a-vanishing-factor> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) If $ax = O$ , then either $a = 0$ or $x = O$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(d) If $ax = O$ , then either $a = 0$ or $x = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(d): a vanishing product forces one factor to vanish" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(d):乘积为零迫使某一因子为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-7-associative-law-for-multiplication-by-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3b-every-scalar-annihilates-the-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "If ax = O then either a = 0 or x = O. It forbids zero divisors between scalars and elements, and it is the converse direction to (a) and (b) taken together: those two produce the zero element, this one says nothing else does." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-closure-under-scalar-multiplication-is-the-active-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "By Axiom 2, $ax$ is in $S$ for every scalar $a$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "By Axiom 2, $ax$ is in $S$ for every scalar $a$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 2 (closure under scalar multiplication) is the axiom actually applied" ;
    ex:nameZh "真正被使用的是公理2(数乘封闭)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:originNote "The second sentence (Axiom 1 is never invoked in the sufficiency direction) is a model inference from reading the proof, not a claim Apostol makes; the anchor supports only the first sentence." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-sufficiency-direction> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Both constructions run through Axiom 2 with specific scalars, applied to the single witness x. Closure under addition (Axiom 1) is never invoked in the proof of the sufficiency direction." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-remaining-obligations-zero-and-negative> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "It remains to verify Axioms 5 and 6, the existence of a zero element in S, and the existence of a negative for each element in S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "It remains to verify Axioms 5 and 6, the existence of a zero element in S, and the existence of a negative for each element in S." ;
    ex:nameEn "Only Axioms 5 and 6 remain to be verified inside S" ;
    ex:nameZh "只剩公理5与6需在 S 内验证" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-sufficiency-direction> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Existence statements do not restrict to subsets, so the existence of a zero element in S and of a negative for each element of S are the only two obligations left. This is the exact point where the proof needs real work." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-witness-element-supplied-by-nonemptiness> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $x$ be any element of $S$ . (S has at least one element since $S$ is not empty.)" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let $x$ be any element of $S$ . (S has at least one element since $S$ is not empty.)" ;
    ex:nameEn "Nonemptiness supplies the witness element x used to build O and negatives" ;
    ex:nameZh "非空性提供了用来造出零元与负元的见证元 x" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-sufficiency-direction> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-hypothesis-nonempty-subset> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Both constructions below start from a fixed element x of S, and the only justification for having one is that S is nonempty. This is the single load-bearing use of the nonemptiness hypothesis in the whole proof." .

<urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-system-norms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(u _ {2 n - 1}, u _ {2 n - 1}) = \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} \\cos^ {2} n x d x = \\pi , \\quad (u _ {2 n}, u _ {2 n}) = \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} \\sin^ {2} n x d x = \\pi .",
        "Therefore, $\\| u_0\\| = \\sqrt{2\\pi}$ and $\\| u_n\\| = \\sqrt{\\pi}$ for $n\\geq 1$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The norm of each element of S is easily calculated. We have $(u_{0}, u_{0}) = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} dx = 2\\pi$ and, for $n \\geq 1$ , we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "Norms in the trigonometric system: sqrt(2pi) for u_0, sqrt(pi) otherwise" ;
    ex:nameZh "三角函数系的范数:u_0 为 sqrt(2pi),其余为 sqrt(pi)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-orthogonal-system> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Computation: (u_0, u_0) is the integral of dx over [0, 2pi] = 2pi, while for n >= 1 the integrals of cos^2(nx) and of sin^2(nx) over [0, 2pi] both equal pi. Hence ||u_0|| = sqrt(2pi) and ||u_n|| = sqrt(pi) for n >= 1. The asymmetry between u_0 and the rest is why the normalized system has two different denominators, and it is a concrete illustration that an orthogonal set is generally not orthonormal." .

<urn:studium:node:d:weight-function-must-be-positive> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f, g) = \\int_ {a} ^ {b} w (t) f (t) g (t) d t,",
        "where w is a fixed positive function in $C(a, b)$ . The function w is called a weight function. In Example 3 we have $w(t) = 1$ for all t." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "where w is a fixed positive function in $C(a, b)$ . The function w is called a weight function. In Example 3 we have $w(t) = 1$ for all t." ;
    ex:nameEn "The weight function must be a fixed positive continuous function" ;
    ex:nameZh "权函数必须是固定的正连续函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-formula> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:weighted-integral-inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In Example 4 the weighted form (f, g) = integral of w(t)f(t)g(t) dt is an inner product only because w is a FIXED POSITIVE function in C(a, b). Positivity of w is exactly what carries the positive definiteness axiom: if w were allowed to change sign or vanish on a subinterval, (f, f) could be zero or negative for some nonzero continuous f, and axiom (4) would fail while axioms (1)-(3) would still hold. Example 3 is the special case w(t) = 1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-earlier-element-makes-the-coefficient-irrelevant> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $y_{j} = O$ , then $y_{r+1}$ is orthogonal to $y_{j}$ for any choice of $a_{j}$ , and in this case we choose $a_{j} = 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $y_{j} = O$ , then $y_{r+1}$ is orthogonal to $y_{j}$ for any choice of $a_{j}$ , and in this case we choose $a_{j} = 0$ .",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:nameEn "If y_j = O any coefficient works, and 0 is chosen" ;
    ex:nameZh "若 y_j = O 则任何系数都行,取 a_j = 0" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-inductive-step-defines-y-r-plus-one> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "If some earlier y_j is the zero element, then y_{r+1} is orthogonal to it whatever a_j is, because the zero element is orthogonal to every element; the proof picks a_j = 0. This case is exactly why the theorem needs no independence hypothesis on the x's." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:all-bases-have-the-same-size> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "All bases for a space have the same number of vectors. This number of vectors in a basis is the dimension of the space.",
        "If $\\pmb { v } _ { 1 } , \\ldots , \\pmb { v } _ { m }$ and $w _ { 1 } , \\ldots , w _ { n }$ are both bases for the same vector space, then $m = n$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A is a short wide matrix, since we assumed $n > m$ . So $A x = \\mathbf { 0 }$ has a nonzero solution.",
        "If $\\pmb { v } _ { 1 } , \\ldots , \\pmb { v } _ { m }$ and $w _ { 1 } , \\ldots , w _ { n }$ are both bases for the same vector space, then $m = n$" ;
    ex:nameEn "All bases for a space have the same number of vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一空间的所有基元素个数相同" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:all-bases-are-equinumerous>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:right-count-upgrades-one-basis-property-to-both> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:short-wide-matrix-has-dependent-columns> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "If v1,...,vm and w1,...,wn are both bases for the same vector space, then m = n. The proof assumes n > m, writes W = VA with A short and wide, and gets a nonzero solution of Ax = 0, so a combination of the w's would be zero." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:augmented-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "One way to organize that is to add bas an extra column of the matrix.",
        "Solution Use the augmented matrix, with its extra column b." ;
    ex:nameEn "Augmented matrix [A b]" ;
    ex:nameZh "增广矩阵 [A b]" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "The augmented matrix [A b] adds the right side b as an extra column of A so that row operations act on both sides at once." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:column-space-orthogonal-to-left-nullspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The column space of A and the nullspace of $\\dot { \\boldsymbol { A } } ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ are always orthogonal subspaces.",
        "The left nullspace $N ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$ and the column space $C ( A )$ are orthogonal in" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The column space of A and the nullspace of $\\dot { \\boldsymbol { A } } ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ are always orthogonal subspaces." ;
    ex:nameEn "Column space is orthogonal to the left nullspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "列空间与左零空间正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The column space C(A) and the left nullspace N(A^T) are always orthogonal subspaces of R^m. Every vector in N(A^T) is perpendicular to every column of A." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:complete-solution> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Figure 3.3: Complete solution = one particular solution + all nullspace solutions.",
        "Please notice how I write the complete solution" ;
    ex:nameEn "Complete solution to Ax = b" ;
    ex:nameZh "Ax=b 的完整解" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:augmented-matrix>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:solvability-condition> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "The complete solution to Ax = b is one particular solution x_p plus any vector x_n in the nullspace: x = x_p + x_n." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:error-vector> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The dotted line $b - p$ is the \"error\" $e = b - { \\widehat { x } } a$",
        "The error vector between $^ { b }$ and $_ p$ is $e = b - p$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "error vector e = b - p" ;
    ex:nameZh "误差向量 e = b - p" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The error vector is e = b - p, the difference between b and its projection p. The vectors p and e add to b." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:full-column-rank> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Every matrix A with full column rank ( r = n) has all these properties:",
        "With full column rank, Ax = b has one solution or no solution" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The other extreme case is full row rank." ;
    ex:nameEn "Full column rank (r = n)" ;
    ex:nameZh "列满秩(r = n)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:full-row-rank> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "A has full column rank when r = n: all columns are pivot columns, there are no free variables, the nullspace is only the zero vector, and Ax = b has one solution or none." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:independent-columns> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION The columns of A are linearly independent when the only solution to Ax = 0 is x = 0. No other combination Ax of the columns gives the zero vector.",
        "The columns are independent when the nullspace $N ( A )$ contains only the zero vector." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "When the vectors are the columns of $A ,$ the two definitions say exactly the same thing." ;
    ex:nameEn "Independent columns of A" ;
    ex:nameZh "矩阵 A 的列无关" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:equivalent <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The columns of A are linearly independent when the only solution to Ax = 0 is x = 0; no other combination Ax of the columns gives the zero vector. Equivalently the nullspace of A contains only the zero vector." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:normal-equation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Now solve the normal equation $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A { \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } = A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } t$ to find x:",
        "in its famous form $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A { \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } = A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } b$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Find the vector ${ \\widehat x } ,$ find the projection $p = A { \\widehat { x } } ,$ find the projection matrix $P \\cdot$",
        "The number ${ \\pmb a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb a }$ becomes the matrix $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A$" ;
    ex:nameEn "the equation A^T A x-hat = A^T b" ;
    ex:nameZh "法方程 A^T A x-hat = A^T b" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-coefficient-x-hat> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The equation for the best x-hat is A^T A x-hat = A^T b, the \"famous form\" of A^T(b - A x-hat) = 0 with coefficient matrix A^T A." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:pivot-columns-basis-for-column-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Conclusion The r pivot columns of A are a basis for its column space $C ( A )$",
        "The pivot columns are independent, and they span, so they are a basis for C(R).",
        "The pivot columns of A are a basis for its column space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The pivot columns are independent, and they span, so they are a basis for C(R)." ;
    ex:nameEn "The r pivot columns of A are a basis for C(A)" ;
    ex:nameZh "A 的 r 个主元列构成列空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The pivot columns of A are a basis for its column space: they are independent because they start with the r by r identity matrix, and every free column is a combination of them. The dimension of the column space is r. Note that the pivot columns of A must be used, not those of R." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-matrix> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection matrix P multiplies b to give p. This section finds p and also P.",
        "symmetric matrices with $P ^ { 2 } = P$ . The projection of bis Pb." ;
    ex:nameEn "projection matrix" ;
    ex:nameZh "投影矩阵" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "A projection matrix P is a symmetric matrix with P^2 = P; it multiplies b to give its projection p = Pb." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-subspace-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection of b onto the subspace is p:",
        "Then the projection of b onto the column space of A is the vector" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Compare with projection onto a line, when A has only one column",
        "Find the vector ${ \\widehat x } ,$ find the projection $p = A { \\widehat { x } } ,$ find the projection matrix $P \\cdot$",
        "The linear independence of the columns $\\mathbf { a } _ { 1 } , \\ldots , \\mathbf { a } _ { n }$ will guarantee that this inverse matrix exists.",
        "The solution is ${ \\widehat { \\pmb { x } } } = ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A ) ^ { - 1 } A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb { b } }$" ;
    ex:nameEn "projection formula p = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T b" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间投影公式 p = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T b" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-of-vector-onto-line-formula> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:ata-invertible-iff-independent-columns>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:normal-equation> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection of b onto the column space of A is the vector p = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T b." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:short-wide-matrix-has-dependent-columns> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The columns are certainly dependent if $n > m$ , because $A x = \\mathbf { 0 }$ has a nonzero solution.",
        "Then the columns must be dependent. Any seven vectors from $\\mathbf { R } ^ { 5 }$ are dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The columns are certainly dependent if $n > m$ , because $A x = \\mathbf { 0 }$ has a nonzero solution." ;
    ex:nameEn "A matrix with m < n has dependent columns" ;
    ex:nameZh "宽矩阵的列必相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-dependence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "If a matrix has more columns than rows (n > m), its columns are certainly dependent: Ax = 0 has at least n - m free variables and therefore a nonzero solution. Any seven vectors from R^5 are dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-in-a-finite-dimensional-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "15.15 Best approximation of elements in a Euclidean space by elements in a finite-dimensional subspace",
        "This is the polynomial of degree $\\leq n$ for which the norm $\\| f - f_n\\|$ is smallest." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "15.15 Best approximation of elements in a Euclidean space by elements in a finite-dimensional subspace",
        "This is the polynomial of degree $\\leq n$ for which the norm $\\| f - f_n\\|$ is smallest." ;
    ex:nameEn "Best approximation of an element by elements of a finite-dimensional subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "用有限维子空间中的元素对元素作最佳逼近" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:best-approximation-by-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The approximation problem of the section: given x in a Euclidean space V and a finite-dimensional subspace S, find the element of S that minimizes the norm ||x - t|| over all t in S. 'Nearest' is measured by the norm induced by the inner product; that inner-product structure is not a precondition for stating the problem, which makes sense in any normed linear space, but it is what makes the projection the solution and makes the Pythagorean equality-condition argument available." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-problem> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We prove next that the projection of $x$ on $S$ is the solution to the approximation problem stated at the beginning of this section.",
        "We wish to consider the following type of approximation problem: Given an element x in V, to determine an element in S whose distance from x is as small as possible." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Here V is the vector space $V_{3}$ and S is a two-dimensional subspace, a plane through the origin.",
        "Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace.",
        "to determine an element in S whose distance from x is as small as possible. The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm" ;
    ex:nameEn "Best approximation problem in a Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "欧几里得空间中的最佳逼近问题" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:distance-between-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Given a Euclidean space V, a finite-dimensional subspace S, and an element x in V, determine the element of S whose distance from x is as small as possible. Section 15.14 sets up this problem, illustrates it by a plane in V_3, and announces that the projection of x on S is its solution." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-linear-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Although we shall deal primarily with examples of real linear spaces, all the theorems are valid for complex linear spaces as well.",
        "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8, and 9, the resulting structure is called a complex linear space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8, and 9, the resulting structure is called a complex linear space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Complex linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "复线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8 and 9, the resulting structure is a complex linear space; its scalars are the complex numbers. All the theorems proved for real linear spaces are valid for complex linear spaces as well." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-convergence-must-be-proved-before-the-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) Prove that this improper integral converges absolutely for all polynomials $f$ and $g$ .",
        "[Hint: Use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to estimate the integral $\\int_0^M e^{-t}|f(t)g(t)|dt.$ ]",
        "[Hint: Use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to estimate the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{M}|x_n y_n|.$ ]" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) Prove that $V$ is a linear space with $(x, y)$ as an inner product.",
        "[Hint: Use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to estimate the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{M}|x_n y_n|.$ ]" ;
    ex:nameEn "On infinite-dimensional spaces the pairing must first be shown finite" ;
    ex:nameZh "无限维空间上必须先证明配对有限" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:infinite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "In Exercises 11, 13 and 14 the candidate inner product is an infinite series or an improper integral, and part (a) of each asks for absolute convergence first. In Exercises 13 and 14 part (b) then asks for the axioms; Exercise 11 does not, because its pairing is the one 15.10 Example 5 has already presented as an inner product, so its later parts compute with it instead. In both of those the hint is to bound a partial sum or a truncated integral by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. Well-definedness is thus a fifth obligation that has no analogue in V_n, where every pairing is a finite sum." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cx-orthogonality-depends-on-the-interval> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "9. In the real linear space $C(-1, 1)$ , let $(f, g) = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t)dt$ .",
        "Prove that two of them are orthogonal, two make an angle $\\pi /3$ with each other, and two make an angle $\\pi /6$ with each other." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "9. In the real linear space $C(-1, 1)$ , let $(f, g) = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t)dt$ .",
        "Prove that two of them are orthogonal, two make an angle $\\pi /3$ with each other, and two make an angle $\\pi /6$ with each other." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality of two fixed functions depends on the interval, not on the functions alone" ;
    ex:nameZh "两个固定函数是否正交取决于区间,而非仅取决于函数本身" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-not-unique-on-a-given-space> ;
    ex:section "15.12" ;
    ex:statement "In C(-1, 1) with the unweighted integral inner product, Exercise 9 shows the constant 1 and the function t are orthogonal, because the integrand is odd. The same two functions are not orthogonal in C(0, 1), where the integral equals 1/2, nor in the weighted space of Exercise 8. Orthogonality is a property of the pair together with the chosen inner product, never of the functions in isolation." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:fourier-coefficients> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The numbers $(f, \\varphi_k)$ are called Fourier coefficients of $f$ .",
        "f _ {n} = \\sum_ {k = 0} ^ {2 n} (f, \\varphi_ {k}) \\varphi_ {k}, \\quad \\text {where} \\quad (f, \\varphi_ {k}) = \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} f (x) \\varphi_ {k} (x) d x." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $f \\in C(0, 2\\pi)$ , let $f_n$ denote the projection of $f$ on the subspace $S$ . Then we have",
        "In Section 15.11 we exhibited an orthonormal set of trigonometric functions $\\varphi_0, \\varphi_1, \\varphi_2, \\ldots$ , where",
        "define an inner product by the equation $(f, g) = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ .",
        "f _ {n} = \\sum_ {k = 0} ^ {2 n} (f, \\varphi_ {k}) \\varphi_ {k}, \\quad \\text {where} \\quad (f, \\varphi_ {k}) = \\int_ {0} ^ {2 \\pi} f (x) \\varphi_ {k} (x) d x." ;
    ex:nameEn "Fourier coefficients of f" ;
    ex:nameZh "f 的 Fourier 系数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The numbers (f, phi_k) obtained by taking the inner product of f with each element of the orthonormal trigonometric set, i.e. (f, phi_k) = integral_0^{2pi} f(x) phi_k(x) dx. They are the coefficients of the projection f_n of f on the span of phi_0, ..., phi_{2n}, and in the cos/sin normalization they become a_k and b_k." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span-notation-l-of-s> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S, and denote it by $L(S)$",
        "and let $L(S)$ be the subspace spanned by S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S, and denote it by $L(S)$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Notation L(S) for the linear span" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性张成的记号 L(S)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "L(S) denotes the subspace spanned by the set S, i.e. the set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S. It is used throughout, for instance in the statement of Theorem 15.5." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one.",
        "whether or not S itself is one. In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S." ;
    ex:nameEn "S perp is a subspace whether or not S is one" ;
    ex:nameZh "S 垂直总是子空间(无论 S 本身是否为子空间)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "For any subset S of a Euclidean space V, the set of all elements orthogonal to S is a subspace of V, even when S itself is not a subspace. Apostol states this and leaves the verification as a simple exercise." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(c) The sequence $y_1, y_2, \\ldots$ , is unique, except for scalar factors.",
        "if $y_1', y_2', \\ldots$ , is another sequence of elements in $V$ satisfying properties (a) and (b), then for each $k$ there is a scalar $c_k$ such that $y_k' = c_k y_k$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(c) The sequence $y_1, y_2, \\ldots$ , is unique, except for scalar factors.",
        "By property (a), both $y_{r+1}'$ and $c_{r+1}y_{r+1}$ are orthogonal to $z_{r}$ .",
        "if $y_1', y_2', \\ldots$ , is another sequence of elements in $V$ satisfying properties (a) and (b), then for each $k$ there is a scalar $c_k$ such that $y_k' = c_k y_k$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness of the orthogonalized sequence up to scalar factors" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交化序列在相差标量因子下的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Part (c) of the orthogonalization theorem: if y_1', y_2', ... is another sequence in V satisfying properties (a) and (b), then for each k there is a scalar c_k with y_k' = c_k y_k. The proof writes y_{r+1}' = z_r + c_{r+1} y_{r+1} with z_r in L(y_1, ..., y_r) and shows z_r is orthogonal to itself, hence z_r = O." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-along-an-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element",
        "In the Gram-Schmidt process (15.15), we construct the element $y_{r+1}$ by subtracting from $x_{r+1}$ the projection of $x_{r+1}$ along each of the earlier elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ .",
        "is called the projection of x along y." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element",
        "The element s is the sum of the projections of x along each basis element.",
        "is called the projection of x along y." ;
    ex:nameEn "Projection of x along y" ;
    ex:nameZh "x 沿 y 方向的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13",
        "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "For elements x and y of a Euclidean space with y not the zero element, the element ((x, y)/(y, y)) y is called the projection of x along y. The Gram-Schmidt step forms y_{r+1} by subtracting from x_{r+1} its projection along each earlier y_i. In 15.14 the same notion is applied to an orthonormal basis element, where (y, y) = 1 reduces the coefficient to (x, e_i)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:uniqueness-of-components> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i",
        "The coefficients in this equation determine an n-tuple of numbers $(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{n})$ that is uniquely determined by x." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i" ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness of the coefficients in a basis expansion" ;
    ex:nameZh "基展开系数的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:unique-coordinates-in-a-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The coefficients in the expansion x = sum c_i e_i over a basis determine an n-tuple (c_1, ..., c_n) that is uniquely determined by x: subtracting a second representation and using independence of the basis elements gives c_i = d_i for each i." .

<urn:studium:node:d:15-10-nonzero-hypothesis-is-essential> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.10. In a Euclidean space V, every orthogonal set of nonzero elements is independent.",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.10. In a Euclidean space V, every orthogonal set of nonzero elements is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "The nonzero hypothesis in Theorem 15.10 cannot be dropped" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.10 中的「非零」假设不可去掉" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element-forces-dependence>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-orthogonal-to-every-element> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.10 asserts independence for every orthogonal set OF NONZERO ELEMENTS, and the restriction is genuinely needed, not cosmetic. Adjoining O to an orthogonal set leaves it orthogonal, because the condition constrains only pairs of distinct elements and O is orthogonal to everything; but the enlarged set is dependent, so without the nonzero hypothesis the theorem would be false. Containing O is not by itself sufficient for orthogonality: {O, i, i + j} contains O and is dependent, yet is not an orthogonal set. Inside the proof the hypothesis is used at exactly one point, to divide by (x_1, x_1)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:additivity-in-first-argument-derived> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "\\begin{array}{r} \\| x + y \\| ^ {2} = (x + y, x + y) = (x, x) + (y, y) + (x, y) + (y, x) \\\\ = \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} + (x, y) + (\\overline {{x , y}}). \\end{array}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Additivity in the first argument is derived, not assumed" ;
    ex:nameZh "对第一个变元的可加性是推论,不是公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ex:origin "model" ;
    ex:originNote "Apostol does not spell out this derivation; the two anchors give the axiom he does state and the computation that silently uses the first-slot version. The derivation itself is supplied by this node." ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "(x + y, z) = (x, z) + (y, z) is NOT one of Apostol's axioms. It follows by using symmetry (1) to swap into the second slot, applying additivity (2) there, and swapping back. This is the precise reason the symmetry axiom is load-bearing in the proofs: the expansion (x + y, x + y) = (x, x) + (y, y) + (x, y) + (y, x) that opens the proof of the triangle inequality, and the step 'pair the vanishing combination with x_1' in Theorem 15.10, both split a sum in the FIRST slot, which axiom (2) alone does not license." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-7-associative-law-for-multiplication-by-numbers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 7. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For every x in V and all real numbers a and b, we have" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 7. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For every x in V and all real numbers a and b, we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 7: associative law for multiplication by numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理7:数乘结合律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For every x in V and all real a, b we have a(bx) = (ab)x. It forbids scaling twice from disagreeing with scaling once by the product, so that any chain of rescalings can be collapsed. The rule a . x = 2ax on V_n obeys Axioms 8 and 9 yet sends a(bx) to 4abx while (ab)x is 2abx, so it violates this axiom (it fails Axioms 6 and 10 as well, since (-1) . x = -2x and 1 . x = 2x, so it does not by itself establish the independence of Axiom 7)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-inequality-statement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.8. In a Euclidean space V, every inner product satisfies the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality:",
        "| (x, y) | ^ {2} \\leq (x, x) (y, y) \\quad f o r a l l x a n d y i n V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "| (x, y) | ^ {2} \\leq (x, x) (y, y) \\quad f o r a l l x a n d y i n V." ;
    ex:nameEn "The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality proper: |(x, y)|^2 <= (x, x)(y, y)" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 不等式本体:|(x, y)|^2 <= (x, x)(y, y)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The inequality clause of Theorem 15.8: in a Euclidean space, every inner product satisfies |(x, y)|^2 <= (x, x)(y, y) for all x and y in V. This clause alone needs no strict positivity: the standard argument bounds the quantity (x - ty, x - ty) >= 0, so positive SEMI-definiteness suffices to obtain the inequality. Strict positivity is needed only for the equality clause." .

<urn:studium:node:d:dot-product-component-sum-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "x \\cdot y = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} y _ {i}.\\tag{15.5}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "x \\cdot y = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} x _ {i} y _ {i}.\\tag{15.5}" ;
    ex:nameEn "The dot product as the component sum (Equation 15.5)" ;
    ex:nameZh "点积作为分量之和(式 15.5)" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Equation (15.5): x . y is the sum over i from 1 to n of x_i y_i, for x and y in V_n. This concrete formula is the object that the axioms abstract from; the integral inner product on C(a, b) is built by the same pattern with function values for components and integration for summation." .

<urn:studium:node:d:example-4-vectors-orthogonal-to-a-fixed-nonzero-vector> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 4. Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 4. Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N.",
        "Let V be the set of all vectors in $V_{n}$ orthogonal to a given nonzero vector N." ;
    ex:nameEn "Example 4: the vectors of V_n orthogonal to a fixed nonzero N" ;
    ex:nameZh "例4:V_n 中与固定非零向量 N 正交的全体向量" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Fixing a nonzero N in V_n and taking all x with x orthogonal to N gives a linear space that is strictly smaller than V_n. It is the first example in the section that is a selected subset rather than a whole familiar set, so it is the first one where closure is not automatic." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-hypothesis-distinct-exponents> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 7. If $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are distinct real numbers, the $n$ exponential functions" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 7. If $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are distinct real numbers, the $n$ exponential functions" ;
    ex:nameEn "Hypothesis of Example 7: the exponents are distinct real numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "例7的前提:指数是互不相同的实数" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "Independence is asserted only for exponential functions whose exponents are pairwise distinct. If two exponents coincide the two functions are equal, hence dependent by the scalar-multiple criterion, so distinctness is not removable." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-independence-clause> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V." ;
    ex:nameEn "A basis must be independent" ;
    ex:nameZh "基必须是独立的" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The first of the two clauses defining a finite basis. Independence is what makes the representation of an element unique; drop it and the set may still span but the coefficients cease to be determined." .

<urn:studium:node:d:independence-vanishing-combination-forces-vanishing-coefficients> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O \\quad i m p l i e s \\quad c _ {1} = c _ {2} = \\dots = c _ {k} = 0." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O \\quad i m p l i e s \\quad c _ {1} = c _ {2} = \\dots = c _ {k} = 0." ;
    ex:nameEn "A vanishing combination forces every coefficient to vanish" ;
    ex:nameZh "组合为零迫使每个系数为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-coefficients-not-all-zero>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:dependence-target-is-the-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:independence-universal-quantifier-over-choices> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The working form of independence: if a finite combination of distinct elements of S equals O, then all the coefficients are zero. This implication is the statement every independence proof in the chapter actually verifies." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-symmetry-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(1) $(x,y) = (y,x)$ (commutativity, or symmetry)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(1) $(x,y) = (y,x)$ (commutativity, or symmetry)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 1: symmetry (commutativity) of the real inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理1:实内积的对称性(交换性)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom (1): (x, y) = (y, x) for all x, y. What it forbids: any product that distinguishes the order of its arguments. Dropping only this axiom leaves a form linear in the second variable but with no transfer of that linearity to the first variable, so (x + y, z) can no longer be expanded, the expansion (x+y, x+y) = (x,x) + (y,y) + (x,y) + (y,x) used for the triangle inequality collapses, and the step 'pair the equation with x_1' in Theorems 15.10 and 15.11 becomes unavailable." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-set-condition-on-distinct-pairs-only> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A subset S of V is called an orthogonal set if $(x, y) = 0$ for every pair of distinct elements x and y in S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A subset S of V is called an orthogonal set if $(x, y) = 0$ for every pair of distinct elements x and y in S." ;
    ex:nameEn "An orthogonal set requires (x, y) = 0 only for DISTINCT pairs" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交集只要求相异元素对的内积为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-nonzero-hypothesis-is-essential> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "Definition: a subset S of V is an orthogonal set if (x, y) = 0 for every pair of DISTINCT elements x and y in S. The word distinct is load-bearing. It exempts the diagonal, so nothing is asserted about (x, x); an orthogonal set may therefore contain the zero element, and its elements are not required to be nonzero. This is precisely the gap that forces the extra hypothesis in Theorem 15.10." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-a-orthogonal-to-earlier-span> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) The element $y_{k}$ is orthogonal to every element in the subspace $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{k - 1})$ .",
        "Then there is a corresponding sequence of elements $y_1, y_2, \\ldots,$ in $V$ which has the following properties for each integer $k$ :" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) The element $y_{k}$ is orthogonal to every element in the subspace $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{k - 1})$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Conclusion (a): y_k is orthogonal to the span of the earlier y's" ;
    ex:nameZh "结论(a):y_k 与前面各元张成的子空间正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Part (a) of Theorem 15.13 asserts that for each k the constructed element y_k is orthogonal not merely to y_1,...,y_{k-1} but to every element of the subspace L(y_1,...,y_{k-1}). It is the orthogonality half of the theorem's conclusion." .

<urn:studium:node:d:s-defined-as-the-sum-of-projections-along-the-basis-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Note that each term $(x, e_{i})e_{i}$ is the projection of x along $e_{i}$ . The element s is the sum of the projections of x along each basis element.",
        "s = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}, \\quad s ^ {\\perp} = x - s.\\tag{15.18}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Given x, define the elements s and $s^{\\perp}$ as follows:",
        "Note that each term $(x, e_{i})e_{i}$ is the projection of x along $e_{i}$ .",
        "Since S is finite-dimensional, it has a finite orthonormal basis, say $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$ .",
        "x = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}.\\tag{15.10}" ;
    ex:nameEn "s is defined as the sum of the projections of x along the basis elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "s 定义为 x 沿各基元投影之和" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-along-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-dimensionality-supplies-a-finite-orthonormal-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "The candidate is s = sum_{i=1..n} (x, e_i) e_i and s perp = x - s. Each term (x, e_i) e_i is the projection of x along e_i, so s is literally the sum of the projections of x along the basis elements." .

<urn:studium:node:d:triangle-inequality-proof-expand-norm-squared> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\begin{array}{r} \\| x + y \\| ^ {2} = (x + y, x + y) = (x, x) + (y, y) + (x, y) + (y, x) \\\\ = \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} + (x, y) + (\\overline {{x , y}}). \\end{array}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "\\begin{array}{r} \\| x + y \\| ^ {2} = (x + y, x + y) = (x, x) + (y, y) + (x, y) + (y, x) \\\\ = \\| x \\| ^ {2} + \\| y \\| ^ {2} + (x, y) + (\\overline {{x , y}}). \\end{array}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof step 1: expand ||x + y||^2 into four inner products" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明第一步:把 ||x + y||^2 展开为四个内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:additivity-in-first-argument-derived>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The opening computation: ||x + y||^2 = (x + y, x + y) = (x, x) + (y, y) + (x, y) + (y, x) = ||x||^2 + ||y||^2 + (x, y) + conjugate of (x, y). Two distinct axiom uses are hidden here. The first equality after the definition splits the sum in BOTH slots, and splitting the first slot is licensed only by additivity plus symmetry; the final rewriting of (y, x) as the conjugate of (x, y) is Hermitian symmetry (1'), which is what keeps the identity valid in the complex case." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-has-zero-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Note: Taking $c = 0$ in (3), we find that $(O, y) = 0$ for all $y$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Note: Taking $c = 0$ in (3), we find that $(O, y) = 0$ for all $y$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero element has zero inner product with everything" ;
    ex:nameZh "零元与任何元素的内积为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Taking c = 0 in the homogeneity axiom (3) gives (O, y) = 0 for all y. This is Apostol's own remark. It is what makes the zero element orthogonal to every element in 15.11, and it is used implicitly whenever a vanishing linear combination is paired with a basis element." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-element-of-a-function-space-is-a-pointwise-identity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A relation of the form $\\sum c_{k} u_{k} = O$ means that" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A relation of the form $\\sum c_{k} u_{k} = O$ means that",
        "In each case the underlying linear space V is the set of all real-valued functions defined on the real line." ;
    ex:nameEn "In a function space the relation equals O means a pointwise identity" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数空间中等于零元意为逐点恒等" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "When V is a space of real-valued functions, the zero element O is the function that vanishes everywhere, so a relation summing to O is an identity holding for every value of the variable. Example 6 exploits this by substituting t = 0 and then differentiating repeatedly; Example 7 exploits it by multiplying by e^{-a_M x} and letting x tend to +infinity." .

<urn:studium:node:d:zero-is-the-only-element-orthogonal-to-itself> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In other words, $z_{r}$ is orthogonal to itself, so $z_{r} = O$ .",
        "Since the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself, we must have $s - t = O$ .",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:nameEn "The zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself" ;
    ex:nameZh "零元是唯一与自身正交的元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.11",
        "15.13",
        "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "If (x, x) = 0 then x = O, so no nonzero element is orthogonal to itself. Apostol states this in 15.11 and then leans on it at exactly two load-bearing places: the uniqueness half of Theorem 15.13(c) and the uniqueness half of Theorem 15.15." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:dimension> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION The dimension of a space is the number of vectors in every basis",
        "The number of vectors, in any and every basis, is the \"dimension\" of the space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "All bases for a space have the same number of vectors. This number of vectors in a basis is the dimension of the space.",
        "DEFINITION The dimension of a space is the number of vectors in every basis" ;
    ex:nameEn "Dimension of a vector space" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量空间的维数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:all-bases-have-the-same-size>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:basis> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The dimension of a space is the number of vectors in every basis for that space. It counts the degrees of freedom in the space; the dimension of R^n is n. We never speak of the rank of a space or the dimension of a basis." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:four-fundamental-subspaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "4. The left nullspace is $N(A^{\\mathrm{T}})$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{m}$. This is our new space.",
        "Four Fundamental Subspaces 1. The row space is $C(A^{\\mathrm{T}})$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{n}$. 2. The column space is $C(A)$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{m}$.",
        "The rank of A reveals the dimensions of all four fundamental subspaces." ;
    ex:nameEn "The four fundamental subspaces" ;
    ex:nameZh "四个基本子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The four fundamental subspaces of an m by n matrix are the row space C(A^T) and nullspace N(A) in R^n, and the column space C(A) and left nullspace N(A^T) in R^m. Two come directly from A and two from A transpose; the rank of A reveals all four dimensions." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:full-row-rank> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "2. Ax = b has a solution for every right side b.",
        "Every matrix A with full row rank" ;
    ex:nameEn "Full row rank (r = m)" ;
    ex:nameZh "行满秩(r = m)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "A has full row rank when r = m: every row has a pivot, the column space is all of R^m, so Ax = b has a solution for every right side b (one solution if m = n, infinitely many if m < n)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-coefficient-x-hat> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Our formula ${ \\widehat { \\pmb x } } = { \\pmb a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb b } / { \\pmb a } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } { \\pmb a }$ gives the projection",
        "The hat over x indicates the best choice x, to give the closest vector in the column space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "These three steps will lead to all projection matrices: find ${ \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } ,$ , then find the vector" ;
    ex:nameEn "the coefficient x-hat" ;
    ex:nameZh "系数 x hat" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "x-hat (\"x hat\") denotes the best coefficient giving the closest vector in the subspace. For a line, x-hat = a^T b / a^T a." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:pythagorean-law-orthogonality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "\\| \\boldsymbol {v} \\| ^ {2} + \\| \\boldsymbol {w} \\| ^ {2} = \\| \\boldsymbol {v} + \\boldsymbol {w} \\| ^ {2}.",
        "for a right triangle with sides v and w" ;
    ex:nameEn "Pythagorean law for orthogonal vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交向量的勾股定理" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "For orthogonal vectors v and w (v^T w = 0), the squared lengths add: ||v||^2 + ||w||^2 = ||v + w||^2. This is the right-triangle relation a^2 + b^2 = c^2." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:rank-theorem> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "2 The column space of A has dimension r. The column rank equals the row rank.",
        "Rank Theorem: The number of independent columns =the number of independent rows" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "2 The column space of A has dimension r. The column rank equals the row rank." ;
    ex:nameEn "Rank Theorem: column rank equals row rank" ;
    ex:nameZh "秩定理:列秩等于行秩" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> ;
    ex:section "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The number of independent columns equals the number of independent rows: the column space of A has dimension r, the same as the row space. The right reason is that the same combinations of columns are zero for A and for R, since Ax = 0 exactly when Rx = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:reduce-augmented-to-rd> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Elimination on [A b] leads to [R d]. Then Ax = b is equivalent to Rx = d.",
        "When we apply the usual elimination steps to A, reaching R, we also apply them to b." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Elimination on [A b] leads to [R d]. Then Ax = b is equivalent to Rx = d." ;
    ex:nameEn "Reduce [A b] to [R d] by elimination" ;
    ex:nameZh "消元把 [A b] 化为 [R d]" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:strang:augmented-matrix> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "Elimination applied to the augmented matrix [A b] produces [R d]; then Ax = b has the same solutions as the simpler system Rx = d." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:solvability-condition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Ax = b and Rx = d are solvable only when all zero rows of R have zeros in d.",
        "This is the condition to put b in the column space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Ax = b and Rx = d are solvable only when all zero rows of R have zeros in d." ;
    ex:nameEn "Solvability condition for Ax = b" ;
    ex:nameZh "Ax=b 可解条件" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:reduce-augmented-to-rd> ;
    ex:section "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "Ax = b is solvable exactly when every zero row of R has a zero in d; equivalently b must lie in the column space of A." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:vector-splitting-row-null> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Each x is the sum $x _ { r } + x _ { n }$ of a row space vector",
        "every x can be split into a row space component" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The splitting in Figure 4.3 shows the key point of orthogonal complements" ;
    ex:nameEn "Splitting a vector into row-space and nullspace components" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量分解为行空间与零空间分量" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Because the row space and nullspace are orthogonal complements, every x in R^n splits uniquely as x = x_r + x_n, a row space component plus a nullspace component." .

<urn:studium:node:x:all-bases-are-equinumerous> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $\\pmb { v } _ { 1 } , \\ldots , \\pmb { v } _ { m }$ and $w _ { 1 } , \\ldots , w _ { n }$ are both bases for the same vector space, then $m = n$",
        "THEOREM 15.6. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:nameEn "Any two bases of the same space have the same number of elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "同一空间的任两个基元素个数相同" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Two bases of one space cannot have different sizes. This is the theorem that makes 'dimension' a well-defined function of the space rather than of a chosen basis." .

<urn:studium:node:x:right-count-upgrades-one-basis-property-to-both> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V.",
        "Any n independent vectors in $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ must span $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ . So they are a basis.",
        "Starting with the correct number of vectors, one property of a basis produces the other." ;
    ex:nameEn "With the right number of elements, one basis property implies the other" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素个数正确时,基的一个性质推出另一个" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.4",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "In a space of dimension n, any n independent elements already span it, and hence form a basis. Only one of the two basis clauses has to be checked once the count is known to be right." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-euclidean-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A complex linear space with an inner product is called a complex Euclidean space. (Sometimes the term unitary space is also used.)" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A complex linear space with an inner product is called a complex Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Complex Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "复欧几里得空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "A complex linear space with an inner product is called a complex Euclidean space; the term unitary space is also used. One example is the complex vector space V_n(C)." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:infinite-dimensional-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 4. The space of all polynomials $p(t)$ is infinite-dimensional.",
        "Otherwise V is called infinite dimensional.",
        "spans this space, no finite set of polynomials spans the space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of O alone. Otherwise V is called infinite dimensional." ;
    ex:nameEn "Infinite-dimensional linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "无限维线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space that is not finite dimensional is called infinite dimensional. The space of all polynomials is an example: an infinite set of powers of t spans it, but no finite set of polynomials does." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:legendre-polynomials> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 2. The Legendre polynomials. In the linear space of all polynomials, with the inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt$",
        "The first few polynomials are easily calculated by the Gram-Schmidt process.",
        "When the orthogonalization theorem is applied to this sequence it yields another sequence of polynomials $y_0, y_1, y_2, \\ldots$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The first few polynomials are easily calculated by the Gram-Schmidt process.",
        "We shall encounter these polynomials again in Volume II in our further study of differential equations, and we shall prove that",
        "When the orthogonalization theorem is applied to this sequence it yields another sequence of polynomials $y_0, y_1, y_2, \\ldots$",
        "with the inner product $(x, y) = \\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt$ , consider the infinite sequence" ;
    ex:nameEn "Legendre polynomials" ;
    ex:nameZh "Legendre 多项式" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-volume-2-proof-of-the-legendre-closed-form>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Applying the orthogonalization theorem to the sequence x_n(t) = t^n in the space of all polynomials with inner product (x, y) = int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt yields orthogonal polynomials y_0, y_1, y_2, ...; the Legendre polynomials are their rescalings P_n(t) = ((2n)!/(2^n (n!)^2)) y_n(t) = (1/(2^n n!)) d^n/dt^n (t^2 - 1)^n. The first few are y_0 = 1, y_1 = t, y_2 = t^2 - 1/3." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-decomposition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "First we prove that an orthogonal decomposition (15.16) actually exists.",
        "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "can be represented uniquely as a sum of two elements, one in S and one in $S^{\\perp}$",
        "where $s$ and $t$ are in $S$ , and $s^{\\perp}$ and $t^{\\perp}$ are in $S^{\\perp}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal decomposition of an element relative to a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素相对于子空间的正交分解" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:alias-of <urn:studium:node:strang:vector-splitting-row-null> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "A representation of an element x as a sum x = s + s perp with s in the subspace S and s perp in S perp. Theorem 15.15 asserts that such a decomposition exists and is unique whenever S is finite-dimensional." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Dividing each of $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ by its norm we get an orthonormal basis consisting of the two vectors",
        "In $V_{4}$ , find an orthonormal basis for the subspace spanned by the three vectors",
        "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthonormal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交规范基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "An orthogonal basis in which every element has norm 1, obtained from an orthogonal basis by dividing each element by its norm. Example 1 exhibits the orthonormal basis {(1/2)(1,-1,1,-1), (1/sqrt 6)(2,1,0,1)} for a two-dimensional subspace of V_4." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:weighted-integral-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 4. In the space $C(a, b)$ , define",
        "where w is a fixed positive function in $C(a, b)$ . The function w is called a weight function." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Because of the exponential factor, this improper integral converges for every choice of polynomials f and g.",
        "where w is a fixed positive function in $C(a, b)$ . The function w is called a weight function. In Example 3 we have $w(t) = 1$ for all t." ;
    ex:nameEn "Weighted integral inner product and weight function" ;
    ex:nameZh "带权积分内积与权函数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-weight-makes-improper-integral-converge> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "On C(a, b) one may also define (f, g) as the integral of w(t)f(t)g(t) over [a, b], where w is a fixed positive function in C(a, b) called a weight function. The unweighted integral inner product is the case w(t) = 1 for all t." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element-forces-dependence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. If $O \\in S$ , then $S$ is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE 3. If $O \\in S$ , then $S$ is dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "A set containing the zero element is dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "含零元的集合是相依的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:zero-element-destroys-independence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "EXAMPLE 3: if the zero element O belongs to S, then S is dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:d:approximation-inequality-with-equality-only-at-the-projection> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Then the projection of x on S is nearer to x than any other element of S.",
        "for all $t$ in $S$ ; the equality sign holds if and only if $t = s$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "for all $t$ in $S$ ; the equality sign holds if and only if $t = s$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.16 as a two-step verification: Pythagorean split, then a vanishing term" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.16 的两步验证结构:先勾股拆分,再判非负项为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "The proof is two steps that discharge the two halves of the claim from a single identity. Step one rewrites x - t = (x - s) + (s - t) and applies the Pythagorean formula, which is licensed because x - s lies in S perp while s - t lies in S; this yields ||x - t||^2 = ||x - s||^2 + ||s - t||^2 and hence the inequality, with no minimization argument anywhere. Step two reads the equality case off the same identity: equality forces ||s - t|| = 0, and it is Theorem 15.9(a),(b) (||z|| = 0 iff z = O) -- not any property of S -- that turns this into t = s." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-4-associative-law-for-addition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 4. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For all $x, y$ , and $z$ in $V$ , we have $(x + y) + z = x + (y + z)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 4. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For all $x, y$ , and $z$ in $V$ , we have $(x + y) + z = x + (y + z)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 4: associative law for addition" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理4:加法结合律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For all x, y, z in V we have (x + y) + z = x + (y + z). It forbids the bracketing of a three-term sum from mattering, which is what makes the notation x + y + z legitimate; without it the regrouping of y_2 + (x + y_1) into (y_2 + x) + y_1 used in Theorem 15.2 is unavailable, and an element could carry two different negatives." .

<urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-inductive-step-defines-y-r-plus-one> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Now assume we have constructed $y_1, \\ldots, y_r$ so that (a) and (b) are satisfied when $k = r$ . Then we define $y_{r+1}$ by the equation",
        "where the scalars $a_{1},\\ldots,a_{r}$ are to be determined.",
        "y _ {r + 1} = x _ {r + 1} - \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {r} a _ {i} y _ {i},\\tag{15.13}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Then we define $y_{r+1}$ by the equation",
        "We construct the elements $y_1, y_2, \\ldots$ , by induction. To start the process, we take $y_1 = x_1$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Inductive step: define y_{r+1} by subtracting a combination of the earlier y's" ;
    ex:nameZh "递推步:用减去前面各 y 的线性组合定义 y_{r+1}" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:gram-schmidt-base-step-y1-equals-x1> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Assuming y_1,...,y_r already satisfy (a) and (b) for k = r, the next element is defined by y_{r+1} = x_{r+1} - sum_{i=1..r} a_i y_i with the scalars a_i still to be determined. Everything else in the proof is the determination of those scalars and the verification of (a) and (b) at k = r+1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(f, g) = \\int_ {a} ^ {b} f (t) g (t) d t.",
        "EXAMPLE 3. Let $C(a, b)$ denote the linear space of all real-valued functions continuous on an interval $[a, b]$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Define an inner product of two functions $f$ and $g$ by the formula",
        "EXAMPLE 3. Let $C(a, b)$ denote the linear space of all real-valued functions continuous on an interval $[a, b]$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The integral inner product on C(a, b)" ;
    ex:nameZh "C(a, b) 上的积分内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Example 3 defines, on the space of real-valued functions continuous on [a, b], the inner product (f, g) = integral from a to b of f(t)g(t) dt. This is the first model of the axioms in which the elements are not tuples, and it is the model Apostol uses to turn Theorem 15.8 into an inequality about integrals." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-uniqueness> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique.",
        "Suppose that $x$ has two such representations, say" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique." ;
    ex:nameEn "Uniqueness half of Theorem 15.15" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.15 的唯一性一半" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Given two representations x = s + s perp = t + t perp with s, t in S and s perp, t perp in S perp, the proof shows s = t and s perp = t perp. No finiteness or basis is used in this half." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonalization-conclusion-b-spans-agree> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) The subspace spanned by $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is the same as that spanned by $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ :",
        "we must show that $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{r + 1}) = L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r + 1})$ , given that $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{r}) = L(x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{r})$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) The subspace spanned by $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is the same as that spanned by $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ :",
        "and let $L(x_1, \\ldots, x_k)$ denote the subspace spanned by the first $k$ of these elements" ;
    ex:nameEn "Conclusion (b): the first k y's and the first k x's span the same subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "结论(b):前 k 个 y 与前 k 个 x 张成同一子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span-notation-l-of-s> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Part (b) of Theorem 15.13 asserts L(y_1,...,y_k) = L(x_1,...,x_k) for every k. It is what makes the orthogonalized sequence a substitute for the original one." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3b-every-scalar-annihilates-the-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (b). Let $z = aO$ , add $z$ to itself, and use Axiom 8." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof of (b). Let $z = aO$ , add $z$ to itself, and use Axiom 8." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(b): every scalar annihilates the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(b):任何标量乘零元仍为零元" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "For every scalar a, aO = O. This is the companion of (a) with the roles of number and element exchanged, and it is proved by the same doubling argument with Axiom 8 in place of Axiom 9." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3c-negation-passes-through-scalar-multiplication> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (c). Let $z = (-a)x$ . Adding $z$ to $ax$ and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + a x = (- a) x + a x = (- a + a) x = 0 x = O," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof of (c). Let $z = (-a)x$ . Adding $z$ to $ax$ and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "if we add $a(-x)$ to ax and use Axiom 8 and property (b), we find that $a(-x) = -(ax)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(c): negation passes through scalar multiplication" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(c):负号可在标量与元素之间穿行" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3b-every-scalar-annihilates-the-zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "For all a and x, (-a)x = -(ax) = a(-x). Negating the scalar, negating the product, and negating the element all give the same element, which is what licenses the routine sign manipulations of ordinary algebra inside an abstract linear space." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-4-sufficiency-direction> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Now we show that if S satisfies the closure axioms it satisfies the others as well." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Now we show that if S satisfies the closure axioms it satisfies the others as well." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.4, substantive direction: closure implies all remaining axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.4的充分性方向:封闭性推出其余公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The content of Theorem 15.4 is that closure alone forces the other eight axioms in S. The proof splits the eight into two groups: those inherited free from V, and Axioms 5 and 6 which must be constructed." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7b-n-independent-elements-form-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V.",
        "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ . Then we have the following:",
        "The proof of (b) is identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.7(b): n independent elements already form a basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.7(b):n 个独立元已构成基" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-c>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-7-hypothesis-dim-v-equals-n> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Part (b) says that once the count reaches the dimension, independence alone forces spanning. This is the practical half of the theorem: it removes the obligation to check spanning whenever the count is right." .

<urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-orthogonal-system> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "so S is an orthogonal set. Since no member of S is the zero element, S is independent.",
        "u _ {0} (x) = 1, \\quad u _ {2 n - 1} (x) = \\cos n x, \\quad u _ {2 n} (x) = \\sin n x, \\quad \\text {for} \\quad n = 1, 2, \\dots ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE. In the real linear space $C(0, 2\\pi)$ with the inner product $(f, g) = \\int_0^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x) dx$ , let $S$ be the set of trigonometric functions",
        "so S is an orthogonal set. Since no member of S is the zero element, S is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "The trigonometric system in C(0, 2pi) is orthogonal" ;
    ex:nameZh "C(0, 2pi) 中的三角函数系是正交系" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:15-10-nonzero-hypothesis-is-essential>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:integral-inner-product-formula> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "In the real space C(0, 2pi) with the integral inner product, the functions u_0(x) = 1, u_(2n-1)(x) = cos(nx), u_(2n)(x) = sin(nx) satisfy the integral of u_n u_m over [0, 2pi] equal to 0 whenever m is not n, so S is an orthogonal set. Apostol then draws the independence conclusion in one step: since no member of S is the zero element, S is independent. This is the worked instance of Theorem 15.10, and it shows the nonzero hypothesis being checked by hand rather than assumed." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The orthogonal complement of a subspace V contains every vector that is perpendicular to V",
        "We conclude that the nullspace complement $N ( A ) ^ { \\perp }$ is exactly the row space" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "This orthogonal subspace is denoted by" ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal complement" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交补" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The orthogonal complement of a subspace V contains every vector perpendicular to V. The nullspace complement N(A)^perp is exactly the row space C(A^T)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-vectors> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero",
        "\\| \\boldsymbol {v} \\| ^ {2} + \\| \\boldsymbol {w} \\| ^ {2} = \\| \\boldsymbol {v} + \\boldsymbol {w} \\| ^ {2}." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交向量" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:strang:pythagorean-law-orthogonality> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero. The test is v^T w = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection of b onto a subspace Sis the closest vector pin",
        "We compute projections onto n-dimensional subspaces in three steps as before" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "With $n = 1$ (one vector ${ \\bf { a } } _ { 1 } )$ this is projection onto a line." ;
    ex:nameEn "projection of a vector onto a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量在子空间上的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:best-approximation-by-the-projection>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "Projecting b onto the subspace spanned by independent columns of A (its column space): find the closest combination p by the same three steps, find x-hat, then p = A x-hat, then P." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:spanning-a-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION A set of vectors spans a space if their linear combinations fill the space.",
        "The column space is spanned by the columns.",
        "The vectors $v _ { 1 } , \\ldots , v _ { r }$ span a space if their combinations fill that space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Spanning a space" ;
    ex:nameZh "张成空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "A set of vectors spans a space if their linear combinations fill the space. The columns of a matrix span its column space; they might be dependent." .

<urn:studium:node:x:best-approximation-by-the-projection> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "3 The projection of b onto a subspace Sis the closest vector pin $S ; b - p$ is orthogonal to S",
        "Then the projection of x on S is nearer to x than any other element of S. That is, if s is the projection of x on S, we have",
        "for all $t$ in $S$ ; the equality sign holds if and only if $t = s$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The projection is the unique nearest element of the subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "投影是子空间中唯一的最近元" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> ;
    ex:section "15.15",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "Among all elements of a finite-dimensional subspace S, the projection of x on S minimises the distance to x, and it is the only minimiser. The proof splits x - t into the orthogonal parts (x - s) and (s - t) and applies the Pythagorean identity." .

<urn:studium:node:x:unique-coordinates-in-a-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The coefficients in this equation determine an n-tuple of numbers $(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{n})$ that is uniquely determined by x.",
        "There is one and only one way to write v as a combination of the basis vectors." ;
    ex:nameEn "Coordinates relative to a basis exist and are unique" ;
    ex:nameZh "基下坐标的存在与唯一" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Relative to a fixed basis, every element has exactly one representation as a linear combination of the basis elements: existence comes from spanning, uniqueness from independence. Both books prove uniqueness by subtracting two representations and applying independence to the difference." .

<urn:studium:node:x:zero-element-destroys-independence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. If $O \\in S$ , then $S$ is dependent.",
        "EXAMPLE 4. The empty set is independent.",
        "We can never allow the zero vector into a basis, because then linear independence is lost." ;
    ex:nameEn "A family containing the zero element is dependent" ;
    ex:nameZh "含零元的族必相关" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "15.07",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Any family that contains the zero element is dependent, because that element alone admits a nontrivial vanishing combination. Consequently the zero element can never belong to a basis, and the empty set is the (only) basis of the zero space." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In a complex linear space, an inner product $(x, y)$ is a complex number satisfying the same axioms as those for a real inner product, except that the symmetry axiom is replaced by the relation",
        "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "an inner product $(x, y)$ is a complex number satisfying the same axioms as those for a real inner product, except that the symmetry axiom is replaced by the relation",
        "satisfying the same axioms as those for a real inner product" ;
    ex:nameEn "Inner product in a complex linear space (Hermitian symmetry)" ;
    ex:nameZh "复线性空间上的内积(Hermite 对称)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In a complex linear space an inner product (x, y) is a complex number satisfying the same axioms as a real inner product, except that symmetry is replaced by (x, y) = conjugate of (y, x). The scalar multiplier in the homogeneity axiom may be any complex number, and the companion relation (x, cy) = conjugate(c) (x, y) follows." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:components-relative-to-ordered-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If $x \\in V$ , we can express $x$ as a linear combination of these basis elements:",
        "determined by Equation (15.4) are called the components of x relative to the ordered basis" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "If $x \\in V$ , we can express $x$ as a linear combination of these basis elements:",
        "are called the components of x relative to the ordered basis $(e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n})$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Components of x relative to an ordered basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "x 相对于有序基的分量" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:unique-coordinates-in-a-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:ordered-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "Writing x in V as a linear combination x = sum c_i e_i over an ordered basis (e_1, ..., e_n), the components of the resulting ordered n-tuple (c_1, ..., c_n) are called the components of x relative to that ordered basis." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 7. If $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are distinct real numbers, the $n$ exponential functions",
        "are independent. We can prove this by induction on $n$ . The result holds trivially when $n = 1$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In each case the underlying linear space V is the set of all real-valued functions defined on the real line.",
        "are independent. We can prove this by induction on $n$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Independence of exponential functions with distinct exponents" ;
    ex:nameZh "指数不同的指数函数是独立的" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "EXAMPLE 7: if a_1, ..., a_n are distinct real numbers, the n exponential functions u_k(x) = e^{a_k x} are independent in the space of real-valued functions on the real line. The proof is by induction on n, multiplying the vanishing relation by e^{-a_M x} and letting x tend to infinity." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property",
        "Axiom 6 tells us that each x has at least one negative, namely $(-1)x$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" ;
    ex:nameEn "Negative of an element" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素的负元" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "By Axiom 6, for every x in V the element (-1)x satisfies x + (-1)x = O, so each x has at least one negative, namely (-1)x." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Dividing each of $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ by its norm we get an orthonormal basis consisting of the two vectors",
        "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Dividing each of $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ by its norm we get an orthonormal basis consisting of the two vectors",
        "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm.",
        "if the first k elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are independent, then the corresponding elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{k}$ are nonzero." ;
    ex:nameEn "Normalizing an element" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素的规范化(单位化)" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis> ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:vanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm-notation> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Dividing a nonzero element y_i of a Euclidean space by its norm ||y_i||, which converts an orthogonal set or orthogonal basis into an orthonormal one. It is the second half of the standard construction behind Theorem 15.14 and produces the normalized Legendre polynomials in Example 2." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Every finite-dimensional linear space has a finite basis. If the space is Euclidean, we can always construct an orthogonal basis.",
        "The set $\\{y_{1}, y_{2}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for this subspace.",
        "if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Every finite-dimensional linear space has a finite basis. If the space is Euclidean, we can always construct an orthogonal basis.",
        "a general theorem whose proof shows how to construct orthogonal sets in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional",
        "if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal basis" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "A basis of a Euclidean space whose elements form an orthogonal set of nonzero elements. Applying the Gram-Schmidt process to a basis x_1, ..., x_k of a finite-dimensional Euclidean space produces an orthogonal basis y_1, ..., y_k of that same space, so every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has one." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is a plane through the origin, as shown in Figure 15.2, then $S^{\\perp}$ is a line through the origin perpendicular to this plane.",
        "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S.",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one.",
        "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal complement of a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "子空间的正交补" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:s-perp-notation>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "When S is a subspace of a Euclidean space V, the subspace S perp of all elements orthogonal to S is called the orthogonal complement of S. Geometrically, the orthogonal complement of a plane through the origin in V_3 is the line through the origin perpendicular to it." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-to-a-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "An element in V is said to be orthogonal to S if it is orthogonal to every element of S.",
        "Let S be a subset of a Euclidean space V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "An element in V is said to be orthogonal to S if it is orthogonal to every element of S.",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one.",
        "Let S be a subset of a Euclidean space V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Element orthogonal to a set" ;
    ex:nameZh "与集合正交的元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-complement> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "An element of a Euclidean space V is said to be orthogonal to a subset S of V if it is orthogonal to every element of S. This lifts orthogonality between two elements (15.11) to orthogonality between an element and an entire set." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:space-of-continuous-functions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 8. The set of all functions continuous on a given interval. If the interval is $[a, b]$ , we denote this space by $C(a, b)$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following sets is a function space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Space of continuous functions on an interval" ;
    ex:nameZh "区间上连续函数空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Example 8: the set of all functions continuous on a given interval is a function space; when the interval is [a, b] this space is denoted by C(a, b)." .

<urn:studium:node:d:orthogonal-decomposition-existence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "First we prove that an orthogonal decomposition (15.16) actually exists.",
        "Given x, define the elements s and $s^{\\perp}$ as follows:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "First we prove that an orthogonal decomposition (15.16) actually exists." ;
    ex:nameEn "Existence half of Theorem 15.15: four independent verification obligations" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.15 存在性一半:四项互相独立的验证义务" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Exhibiting a decomposition splits into four obligations that are discharged separately and rest on different prior results: obtain a finite orthonormal basis of S (this is where finite-dimensionality of S is consumed, via Gram-Schmidt); define s as the sum of the projections of x along that basis; check s lies in S (it is a linear combination of basis elements); and check s perp = x - s is orthogonal to S, which itself needs the reduction from all of S to the basis elements. Only the second obligation is a construction; the other three are verifications, and the choice of basis is the only arbitrary input, which is why the uniqueness half, once proved, is what makes the construction independent of that choice." .

<urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let V denote a nonempty set of objects, called elements. The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups.",
        "We turn now to a detailed description of these axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:nameEn "Ten axioms listed in three groups" ;
    ex:nameZh "十条公理分三组列出" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The definition consists of exactly ten axioms, presented in three groups: two closure axioms, four axioms for addition, and four axioms for multiplication by numbers." .

<urn:studium:node:d:thm-15-3a-the-scalar-zero-annihilates-every-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Proof of (a). Let $z = 0x$ . We wish to prove that $z = O$ . Adding $z$ to itself and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "z + z = 0 x + 0 x = (0 + 0) x = 0 x = z." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Adding $z$ to itself and using Axiom 9, we find that",
        "Proof of (a). Let $z = 0x$ . We wish to prove that $z = O$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3(a): the scalar zero annihilates every element" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.3(a):标量 0 消灭任何元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "For every x in a linear space, 0x = O. The statement links the number zero to the element zero, two objects that the axioms otherwise keep apart, and it is proved from Axiom 9 alone together with cancellation." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$v ^ { \\mathrm { T } } w = 0$ for all v in V and all w in W.",
        "their dot product is zero: $\\boldsymbol { v } \\boldsymbol { \\cdot w } = \\boldsymbol { v } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } \\boldsymbol { w } = \\boldsymbol { 0 }$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Dot product written as v^T w" ;
    ex:nameZh "点积记号 v^T w" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The dot product of v and w is written v . w = v^T w, so the orthogonality condition is v^T w = 0." .

<urn:studium:node:x:least-squares-is-a-projection> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Given an element x in V, to determine an element in S whose distance from x is as small as possible.",
        "It contains the error $e = b - A x$ in the \"least-squares\" solution. Least squares is the key application of linear algebra in this chapter." ;
    ex:nameEn "Minimising a squared residual is a projection problem" ;
    ex:nameZh "最小化平方残差就是投影问题" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:best-approximation-by-the-projection> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "When an element cannot be represented exactly by the available generators, the best representation in the norm induced by the pairing is obtained by projecting onto the subspace they span. The unsolvable exact problem is thus replaced by a solvable orthogonality condition." .

<urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Example 2 Two walls look perpendicular but those two subspaces are not orthogonal!",
        "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S.",
        "Those pairs of subspaces are not only orthogonal, they are orthogonal complements." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal pair of subspaces versus orthogonal complement" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交子空间对 vs 正交补" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "The name 'orthogonal complement' is reserved for the case where the orthogonal set is taken against a subspace, and in that case the subspace and its complement together exhaust the ambient space. Mutual orthogonality of two subspaces on its own forces only that they intersect in the zero element." .

<urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If a vector v is orthogonal to itself then v is the zero vector.",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself.",
        "When a vector is in two orthogonal subspaces, it must be zero. It is perpendicular to itself." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonality defined as a vanishing pairing" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交即配对为零" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ex:section "15.11",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Two elements are orthogonal when their pairing is zero. The zero element is orthogonal to everything, and it is the only element orthogonal to itself -- a fact that comes from positive definiteness and is used as a uniqueness device in both books." .

<urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-of-the-residual> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The left nullspace is important in projections. That nullspace of $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ contains the error vector",
        "The n right angles give the n equations for",
        "Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$ , which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The residual is orthogonal to the approximating subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "残差与逼近子空间正交" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:equivalent <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The difference between an element and its projection on a subspace is orthogonal to that entire subspace. This single condition characterises the projection and is what both books use to identify it." .

<urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "2 The error $e = \\pmb { b } - \\pmb { p }$ is perpendicular to a: Right triangle b p e has $| | \\boldsymbol { p } | | ^ { 2 } + | | \\boldsymbol { e } | | ^ { 2 } = | | \\boldsymbol { b } | | ^ { 2 }$",
        "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal. This proves (15.17)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Pythagorean identity for orthogonal summands" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交分量的勾股恒等式" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "If two elements are orthogonal, the squared norm of their sum is the sum of their squared norms, because the cross terms in the expansion vanish." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:cauchy-schwarz-inequality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Moreover, the equality sign holds if and only if x and y are dependent.",
        "THEOREM 15.8. In a Euclidean space V, every inner product satisfies the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality:",
        "| (x, y) | \\leq \\| x \\| \\| y \\|." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "EXAMPLE. Applying Theorem 15.8 to the space $C(a, b)$ with the inner product $(f, g) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(t) g(t) dt$ , we find that the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality becomes",
        "In a Euclidean space V, every inner product satisfies the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality",
        "THEOREM 15.8. In a Euclidean space V, every inner product satisfies the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality:",
        "The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that $|(x,y)| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ and $|(\\overline{x,y})| \\leq \\| x\\| \\| y\\|$ , so we have",
        "The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows that the quotient on the right of (15.6) lies in the interval $[-1, 1]$",
        "When the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is expressed in terms of norms, it becomes",
        "When we proved the corresponding result for vectors in $V_{n}$ (Theorem 12.3), we were careful to point out that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (Theorem 15.8)" ;
    ex:nameZh "Cauchy-Schwarz 不等式(定理 15.8)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:d:cauchy-schwarz-for-integrals> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-3>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.8: in a Euclidean space V, every inner product satisfies |(x, y)|^2 <= (x, x)(y, y) for all x and y in V, with equality if and only if x and y are dependent. Expressed in terms of norms it reads |(x, y)| <= ||x|| ||y||." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Note: Equation (15.11) is named in honor of M. A. Parseval (circa 1776–1836), who obtained this type of formula in a special function space.",
        "The next theorem shows that in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with an orthonormal basis the inner product of two elements can be computed in terms of their components.",
        "assume that $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V$ . Then for every pair of elements $x$ and $y$ in $V$ , we have" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Taking the inner product of both members of Equation (15.10) with y and using the linearity property of the inner product, we obtain (15.11).",
        "assume that $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V$ " ;
    ex:nameEn "Parseval's formula (Theorem 15.12)" ;
    ex:nameZh "Parseval 公式(定理 15.12)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.12: if {e_1, ..., e_n} is an orthonormal basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space V of dimension n, then for every pair x, y in V the inner product equals the sum of (x, e_i) times the conjugate of (y, e_i) (Parseval's formula). Taking x = y gives ||x||^2 as the sum of |(x, e_i)|^2." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Moreover, the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula",
        "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal. This proves (15.17)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Finally, we prove that the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula.",
        "Moreover, the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula",
        "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal. This proves (15.17)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Pythagorean formula for an orthogonal decomposition" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交分解的勾股公式" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:parsevals-formula> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:pythagorean-law-orthogonality> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Part of Theorem 15.15: if x = s + s perp is the orthogonal decomposition of x relative to a finite-dimensional subspace S, then the square of the norm of x equals the sum of the squares of the norms of s and s perp, because the cross terms in the expansion of (s + s perp, s + s perp) vanish by orthogonality." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Linear spaces, as defined above, are sometimes called real linear spaces to emphasize the fact that we are multiplying the elements of V by real numbers." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A real linear space has real numbers as scalars; a complex linear space has complex numbers as scalars.",
        "Linear spaces, as defined above, are sometimes called real linear spaces to emphasize the fact that we are multiplying the elements of V by real numbers." ;
    ex:nameEn "Real linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "实线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-linear-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space as defined by the ten axioms is sometimes called a real linear space, to emphasize that the elements of V are multiplied by real numbers. Its scalars are the real numbers." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) The element $y_{k}$ is orthogonal to every element in the subspace $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{k - 1})$ .",
        "(b) The subspace spanned by $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is the same as that spanned by $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ :",
        "THEOREM 15.13. ORTHOGONALIZATION THEOREM. Let $x_1, x_2, \\ldots,$ be a finite or infinite sequence of elements in a Euclidean space $V$ ," ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) The element $y_{k}$ is orthogonal to every element in the subspace $L(y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{k - 1})$ .",
        "For $j\\leq r$ , the inner product of $y_{r+1}$ with $y_{j}$ is given by",
        "If $y_{j} = O$ , then $y_{r+1}$ is orthogonal to $y_{j}$ for any choice of $a_{j}$ , and in this case we choose $a_{j} = 0$ .",
        "THEOREM 15.13. ORTHOGONALIZATION THEOREM. Let $x_1, x_2, \\ldots,$ be a finite or infinite sequence of elements in a Euclidean space $V$ ,",
        "Therefore, as a corollary of Theorem 15.13 we have the following.",
        "a general theorem whose proof shows how to construct orthogonal sets in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional",
        "and let $L(x_1, \\ldots, x_k)$ denote the subspace spanned by the first $k$ of these elements.",
        "whose proof shows how to construct orthogonal sets in any Euclidean space, finite or infinite dimensional." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonalization theorem (Theorem 15.13)" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交化定理(定理 15.13)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span-notation-l-of-s>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "For any finite or infinite sequence x_1, x_2, ... in a Euclidean space V there is a corresponding sequence y_1, y_2, ... in V such that for every k: (a) y_k is orthogonal to every element of L(y_1, ..., y_{k-1}); (b) L(y_1, ..., y_k) = L(x_1, ..., x_k); and (c) the sequence is unique except for scalar factors. Its proof is constructive and by induction, taking y_1 = x_1 and subtracting suitable multiples of the earlier y_i from x_{r+1}." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.14. Every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis.",
        "Therefore, as a corollary of Theorem 15.13 we have the following." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Every finite-dimensional linear space has a finite basis. If the space is Euclidean, we can always construct an orthogonal basis.",
        "THEOREM 15.14. Every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis.",
        "THEOREM 15.15. ORTHOGONAL DECOMPOSITION THEOREM. Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V.",
        "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm.",
        "if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis (Theorem 15.14)" ;
    ex:nameZh "每个有限维欧几里得空间都有正交规范基(定理 15.14)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:normalizing-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "Theorem 15.14 states that every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis. It is obtained as a corollary of Theorem 15.13: orthogonalize a finite basis by the Gram-Schmidt process, then normalize each resulting element." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-16-approximation-theorem> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.16. APPROXIMATION THEOREM. Let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space V, and let x be any element of V.",
        "Then the projection of x on S is nearer to x than any other element of S.",
        "for all $t$ in $S$ ; the equality sign holds if and only if $t = s$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space V, and let x be any element of V.",
        "Proof. By Theorem 15.15 we can write $x = s + s^{\\perp}$ , where $s \\in S$ and $s^{\\perp} \\in S^{\\perp}$ .",
        "Since $s - t \\in S$ and $x - s = s^{-} \\in S^{\\perp}$ , this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$",
        "THEOREM 15.16. APPROXIMATION THEOREM. Let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space V, and let x be any element of V.",
        "Then the projection of x on S is nearer to x than any other element of S.",
        "this is an orthogonal decomposition of $x - t$ , so its norm is given by the Pythagorean formula" ;
    ex:nameEn "Approximation Theorem (Theorem 15.16): the projection is the nearest element of the subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "逼近定理(定理 15.16):投影是子空间中距离最近的元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-subspace-formula> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:best-approximation-by-the-projection> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-in-a-finite-dimensional-subspace> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "If S is a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space V and x is in V, then the projection s of x on S satisfies ||x - s|| <= ||x - t|| for every t in S. Equality holds if and only if t = s, so the projection is the unique best approximation to x in S. The proof splits x - t = (x - s) + (s - t) into orthogonal parts and applies the Pythagorean formula." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-uniqueness-of-negatives> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.2. UNIQUENESS OF NEGATIVE ELEMENTS. In any linear space every element has exactly one negative. That is, for every x there is one and only one y such that $x + y = O$",
        "Therefore $y_{1} = y_{2}$ , so $x$ has exactly one negative, the element $(-1)x$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Adding $y_{2}$ to both members of the first equation and using Axioms 5, 4, and 3, we find that",
        "Axiom 6 tells us that each x has at least one negative, namely $(-1)x$",
        "Proof. Axiom 6 tells us that each x has at least one negative, namely $(-1)x$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.2: uniqueness of negative elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.2:负元的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:negative-of-an-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-4-associative-law-for-addition>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "In any linear space every element has exactly one negative: for every x there is one and only one y such that x + y = O, and that y is the element (-1)x." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-10-existence-of-identity> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 10: existence of a multiplicative identity scalar" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理10:单位标量存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For every x in V we have 1x = x. It forbids the scalar 1 from acting nontrivially, and so rules out a scalar multiplication that has lost all contact with its argument: under the usual existential form of the inverse axiom, the degenerate rule ax = O for every a and x satisfies the other nine axioms and is excluded by Axiom 10 alone." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-8-distributive-law-for-addition-in-v> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 8. DISTRIBUTIVE LAW FOR ADDITION IN $V$ . For all $x$ and $y$ in $V$ and all real $a$ , we have" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 8. DISTRIBUTIVE LAW FOR ADDITION IN $V$ . For all $x$ and $y$ in $V$ and all real $a$ , we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 8: distributive law for addition in V" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理8:对 V 中加法的分配律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For all x, y in V and all real a we have a(x + y) = ax + ay. It forbids a scalar from failing to distribute over addition inside V, that is, forbids the map x -> ax from being non-additive; it is also the axiom that carries the proof of Theorem 15.3(b), so without it aO could be an element other than O." .

<urn:studium:node:d:degree-exactly-n-is-not-a-linear-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." ;
    ex:nameEn "The polynomials of degree exactly n are not a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数恰为 n 的多项式不构成线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-exactly-n> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Requiring the degree to equal n rather than to be at most n destroys the structure, and Apostol attributes the failure to the closure axioms. The set is the section's only negative example among the polynomial sets, and it isolates the difference between an inequality constraint and an equality constraint on a defining invariant." .

<urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-proof-is-induction-on-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "We can prove this by induction on $n$ . The result holds trivially when $n = 1$ . Therefore, assume it is true for $n - 1$ exponential functions and consider scalars" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "We can prove this by induction on $n$ . The result holds trivially when $n = 1$ . Therefore, assume it is true for $n - 1$ exponential functions and consider scalars" ;
    ex:nameEn "Proof architecture: induction on n with a one-coefficient kill per level" ;
    ex:nameZh "证明架构:对 n 归纳,每层杀掉一个系数" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:independence-of-exponential-functions> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:exponential-independence-hypothesis-distinct-exponents> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "The architecture has three parts with a fixed dependency order: a base case n = 1 that needs nothing; an elimination step that picks the largest exponent a_M, normalizes by e^{-a_M x}, and takes x to +infinity to force c_M = 0; and a descent step that deletes the M-th term and calls the hypothesis on the remaining n - 1 functions. Only the elimination step uses the hypothesis that the exponents are distinct -- it is what makes a_k - a_M strictly negative for k not M -- while the recursion itself is independent of it, so the recursion depth equals the number of functions and each level kills exactly one coefficient." .

<urn:studium:node:d:finite-basis-spanning-clause> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V" ;
    ex:nameEn "A basis must span the space" ;
    ex:nameZh "基必须张成整个空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "The second of the two clauses. Spanning is what makes the representation of an element exist at all; drop it and an independent set represents only the elements of its own span." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-additivity-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity)." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 2: additivity (distributivity) in the second argument" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理2:对第二个变元的可加性(分配性)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom (2): (x, y + z) = (x, y) + (x, z) for all x, y, z. Apostol states additivity in the SECOND slot only; additivity in the first slot is not an axiom but a consequence of (2) together with symmetry. What it forbids: any product that fails to split a sum in its second argument. Dropping only this axiom kills every 'pair the identity with a basis element and expand the sum' argument, i.e. the proofs of Theorem 15.10, Theorem 15.11 and Parseval's formula." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-subspaces> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Subspaces V and Ware orthogonal if every v in Vis orthogonal to every win W.",
        "Two subspaces V and $W$ of a vector space are orthogonal if every vector v in V is perpendicular to every vector w in $W$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "$v ^ { \\mathrm { T } } w = 0$ for all v in V and all w in W.",
        "every vector v in V is perpendicular to every vector w in $W$" ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal subspaces" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-vectors> ;
    ex:section "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Two subspaces V and W are orthogonal if every vector v in V is perpendicular to every vector w in W, i.e. v^T w = 0 for all such pairs." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-line> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "These three steps will lead to all projection matrices: find ${ \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } ,$ , then find the vector",
        "When b is projected onto a line, its projection pis the part of b along that line" ;
    ex:nameEn "projection of a vector onto a line" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量在一条直线上的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ex:section "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "The projection of b onto the line through a is its closest point p, the part of b along that line. It is found in three steps: find the number x-hat, then the vector p, then the matrix P." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:row-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION The row space of a matrix is the subspace of $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ spanned by the rows.",
        "The columns span the column space. Here is a new subspace-which is spanned by the rows.",
        "The row space contains all combinations of the rows. This row space of A is the column space of $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION The row space of a matrix is the subspace of $\\mathbf { R } ^ { n }$ spanned by the rows.",
        "Four Fundamental Subspaces 1. The row space is $C(A^{\\mathrm{T}})$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{n}$. 2. The column space is $C(A)$, a subspace of $\\mathbf{R}^{m}$." ;
    ex:nameEn "Row space of a matrix" ;
    ex:nameZh "矩阵的行空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:strang:four-fundamental-subspaces> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:spanning-a-space> ;
    ex:section "3.4",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "The row space of a matrix is the subspace of R^n spanned by its rows: all combinations of the rows. It is the column space of A transpose, and it is a subspace of R^n while the column space lives in R^m." .

<urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "## $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } }$ A is invertible if and only if A has linearly independent columns.",
        "3. The nullspace N(A) contains only the zero vector x = 0.",
        "But since the basis elements are independent, this implies $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for each i" ;
    ex:nameEn "What independence of the generating family is used for" ;
    ex:nameZh "生成族独立性的用处" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:unique-coordinates-in-a-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.3",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "Independence of a generating family is exactly the condition that makes the representation of an element by that family unique. In matrix form it is full column rank, equivalently a trivial nullspace, equivalently invertibility of A-transpose-A." .

<urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$N ( A )$ is the orthogonal complement of the row space $C ( A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } )$",
        "The point of \"complements\" is that every x can be split into a row space component",
        "Then every element x in V can be represented uniquely as a sum of two elements, one in S and one in $S^{\\perp}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Unique splitting of an element along a subspace and its perp" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素沿子空间与其垂空间的唯一分解" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:pythagorean-identity-for-orthogonal-parts> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Given a subspace S of a space with a pairing, and provided S is finite-dimensional (Apostol) or the ambient space is finite-dimensional (Strang), every element of the ambient space is the sum of one element of S and one element of S-perp, and this splitting is unique. Uniqueness follows because the difference of two such splittings lies in both S and S-perp, hence is orthogonal to itself." .

<urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The projection of b onto the line through a is the vector $p = { \\widehat { x } } a = { \\frac { a ^ { \\mathrm { T } } b } { a ^ { \\mathrm { T } } a } } a$",
        "When P projects onto one subspace, $I - P$ projects onto the perpendicular subspace.",
        "is called the projection of x along y. In the Gram-Schmidt process (15.15), we construct the element $y_{r+1}$ by subtracting from $x_{r+1}$ the projection" ;
    ex:nameEn "Projection along a single nonzero direction" ;
    ex:nameZh "沿单个非零方向的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ex:section "15.13",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "For a nonzero direction y, the projection of x along y is the multiple of y whose coefficient is the pairing of x with y divided by the pairing of y with itself. The remainder x minus that multiple is orthogonal to y." .

<urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Figure 3.3: Complete solution = one particular solution + all nullspace solutions.",
        "The set of solutions of a nonhomogeneous differential equation does not satisfy the closure axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "Solutions of a homogeneous linear condition form a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "齐次线性条件的解集是子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.03",
        "3.3" ;
    ex:statement "The set of solutions of a homogeneous linear condition is closed under addition and scalar multiplication, hence a subspace; the solutions of the corresponding inhomogeneous condition are one particular solution plus that subspace." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x.",
        "AXIOM 7. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For every x in V and all real numbers a and b, we have",
        "Axioms for multiplication by numbers" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 7. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For every x in V and all real numbers a and b, we have",
        "Axioms for multiplication by numbers",
        "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups.",
        "We wish to prove that $z = O$ . Adding $z$ to itself and using Axiom 9, we find that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms for multiplication by numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "数乘公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The third group of axioms (Axioms 7 to 10): a(bx) = (ab)x, a(x + y) = ax + ay, (a + b)x = ax + bx, and 1x = x for every x in V and all real a and b." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-on-a-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "is called the projection of $x$ on the subspace $S$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ ,",
        "Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let",
        "Since s is a linear combination of the basis elements, s lies in S.",
        "We prove next that the projection of $x$ on $S$ is the solution to the approximation problem stated at the beginning of this section.",
        "be an orthonormal basis for $S$ .",
        "s = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}",
        "s = \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {n} (x, e _ {i}) e _ {i}, \\quad s ^ {\\perp} = x - s.\\tag{15.18}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Projection of an element on a subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素在子空间上的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:best-approximation-problem> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "15.15" ;
    ex:statement "If {e_1, ..., e_n} is an orthonormal basis for a finite-dimensional subspace S of a Euclidean space V and x is in V, the element s given by the sum of (x, e_i) e_i is called the projection of x on the subspace S. It is exactly the S-component of the orthogonal decomposition of x, and Theorem 15.16 (in Section 15.15) shows it solves the approximation problem." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Different sets may span the same subspace. For example, the space $V_{2}$ is spanned by each of the following sets of vectors",
        "For example, which spaces can be spanned by a finite set of elements?",
        "The space of all polynomials is spanned by the infinite set of polynomials" ;
    ex:nameEn "Spanning set of a space" ;
    ex:nameZh "张成空间的集合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A set spans a space when every element of the space is a finite linear combination of its elements. Different sets may span the same subspace, and Apostol asks which spaces can be spanned by a finite set and with how few elements." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.15. ORTHOGONAL DECOMPOSITION THEOREM. Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V.",
        "Then every element x in V can be represented uniquely as a sum of two elements, one in S and one in $S^{\\perp}$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "First we prove that an orthogonal decomposition (15.16) actually exists.",
        "Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V.",
        "Moreover, the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula",
        "Since S is finite-dimensional, it has a finite orthonormal basis, say $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$ .",
        "Since s is a linear combination of the basis elements, s lies in S.",
        "Since the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself, we must have $s - t = O$ .",
        "THEOREM 15.15. ORTHOGONAL DECOMPOSITION THEOREM. Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V.",
        "so $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to $e_j$ . Therefore $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to every element in $S$",
        "we consider the inner product of $s^{\\perp}$ and any basis element $e_{j}$ .",
        "which means that $s^\\perp \\in S^\\perp$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal decomposition theorem (Theorem 15.15)" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交分解定理(定理 15.15)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:fundamental-theorem-part-2> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:pythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-complement>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-decomposition>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.14" ;
    ex:statement "Let V be a Euclidean space and S a finite-dimensional subspace. Then every x in V can be written uniquely as x = s + s perp with s in S and s perp in S perp, and the norm of x obeys the Pythagorean formula. Existence is proved by taking a finite orthonormal basis of S and setting s equal to the sum of the projections of x along the basis elements; uniqueness follows because the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-7> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(a) Any set of independent elements in $V$ is a subset of some basis for $V$ .",
        "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V.",
        "THEOREM 15.7. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ . Then we have the following:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(a) Any set of independent elements in $V$ is a subset of some basis for $V$ .",
        "(b) Any set of n independent elements is a basis for V.",
        "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ .",
        "THEOREM 15.7. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space with $\\dim V = n$ . Then we have the following:",
        "The proof of (a) is identical to that of part (b) of Theorem 12.10.",
        "The proof of (b) is identical to that of part (c) of Theorem 12.10." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.7 (independent sets extend to bases; n independent elements form a basis)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.7(独立集可扩为基;n个独立元构成基)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:basis-of-rn-from-invertible-matrix> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:independence-criterion-and-its-consequences>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:right-count-upgrades-one-basis-property-to-both> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-b>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-10-part-c>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.7: let V be a finite-dimensional linear space with dim V = n. Then (a) any set of independent elements in V is a subset of some basis for V, and (b) any set of n independent elements is a basis for V. The proofs are those of parts (b) and (c) of Theorem 12.10." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:triangle-inequality> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(d) $\\|x + y\\| \\leq \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$ (triangle inequality).",
        "The equality sign holds in (d) if $x = O$ , if $y = O$ , or if $y = cx$ for some $c > 0$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(d) $\\|x + y\\| \\leq \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|$ (triangle inequality)." ;
    ex:nameEn "Triangle inequality" ;
    ex:nameZh "三角不等式" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Part (d) of Theorem 15.9: ||x + y|| <= ||x|| + ||y|| for all elements x and y of a Euclidean space. The equality sign holds if x = O, if y = O, or if y = cx for some c > 0. The proof expands ||x + y||^2 and applies the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality." .

<urn:studium:node:d:norm-definition-as-square-root> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "is called the norm of the element $x$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "The norm is defined as the square root of (x, x)" ;
    ex:nameZh "范数定义为 (x, x) 的平方根" ;
    ex:nodeType "notation" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:self-inner-product-is-real-in-complex-case> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Definition: in a Euclidean space V the nonnegative number ||x|| = (x, x)^(1/2) is called the norm of x. Two things are asserted at once and both rest on axioms: that the number under the root is nonnegative, so the real square root exists, and that the nonnegative branch is the one taken, so ||x|| >= 0 by construction rather than by proof." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Correct language: \"The sequence of vectors is linearly independent.\" Acceptable shortcut: \"The vectors are independent.\"",
        "DEFINITION The sequence of vectors $v _ { 1 } , \\ldots , v _ { n }$ is linearly independent if the only combination that gives the zero vector is" ;
    ex:nameEn "Linear independence of a sequence of vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量序列的线性无关" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "A sequence of vectors v1,...,vn is linearly independent if the only combination x1v1+...+xnvn that gives the zero vector is the one with all x's equal to zero. Otherwise the vectors are dependent. This definition applies to any sequence of vectors in any vector space." .

<urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The words \"independence\" and \"basis\" and \"dimension\" are not at all restricted to column vectors.",
        "When a theorem is deduced from the axioms of a linear space, we obtain, in one stroke, a result valid for each concrete example." ;
    ex:nameEn "Linear-algebraic vocabulary applies to objects that are not column vectors" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性代数词汇不限于列向量" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "15.03",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "Independence, spanning, basis and dimension are defined for elements of an arbitrary linear space; matrices, polynomials and continuous functions are legitimate 'vectors'. Nothing in these four notions refers to coordinates or to columns of numbers." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a real Euclidean space V, the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is defined to be that number $\\theta$ in the interval $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ which satisfies the equation",
        "\\cos \\theta = \\frac {(x , y)}{\\| x \\| \\| y \\|}.\\tag{15.6}" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. In a real Euclidean space V, the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is defined to be that number",
        "\\cos \\theta = \\frac {(x , y)}{\\| x \\| \\| y \\|}.\\tag{15.6}" ;
    ex:nameEn "Angle between two elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "两元素之间的夹角" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-euclidean-space> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In a real Euclidean space V the angle between two nonzero elements x and y is the number theta in [0, pi] with cos theta = (x, y) / (||x|| ||y||). The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality shows the quotient lies in [-1, 1], so exactly one such theta exists." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 1. In $V_{n}$ let $(x, y) = x \\cdot y$ , the usual dot product of $x$ and $y$ .",
        "In our study of $V_{n}$ , we defined lengths and angles in terms of the dot product." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In our study of $V_{n}$ , we defined lengths and angles in terms of the dot product." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dot product in V_n" ;
    ex:nameZh "V_n 中的点积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-vn-as-a-prior-vector-space> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The dot product x . y of two vectors in V_n, defined in Chapter 12 by the sum of the products of corresponding components. It is the concrete product in terms of which lengths and angles in V_n were defined, and it is the model that the inner product generalizes; in V_n one may take (x, y) = x . y." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:gram-schmidt-process> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In the Gram-Schmidt process (15.15), we construct the element $y_{r+1}$ by subtracting from $x_{r+1}$ the projection of $x_{r+1}$ along each of the earlier elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ .",
        "The construction is called the Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process, in honor of J. P. Gram",
        "These formulas describe the Gram-Schmidt process for constructing an orthogonal set of nonzero elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ which spans the same subspace as a given independent set" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "For $j\\leq r$ , the inner product of $y_{r+1}$ with $y_{j}$ is given by",
        "In $V_{4}$ , find an orthonormal basis for the subspace spanned by the three vectors",
        "In the Gram-Schmidt process (15.15), we construct the element $y_{r+1}$ by subtracting from $x_{r+1}$ the projection of $x_{r+1}$ along each of the earlier elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{r}$ .",
        "These formulas describe the Gram-Schmidt process for constructing an orthogonal set of nonzero elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ which spans the same subspace as a given independent set",
        "if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Gram-Schmidt process" ;
    ex:nameZh "Gram-Schmidt 正交化过程" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:applies-to <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:projection-along-an-element> ;
    ex:section "15.13" ;
    ex:statement "The explicit construction y_1 = x_1 and y_{r+1} = x_{r+1} - sum_{i=1}^{r} ((x_{r+1}, y_i)/(y_i, y_i)) y_i (formula 15.15), which turns an independent set x_1, ..., x_k in a Euclidean space into an orthogonal set of nonzero elements y_1, ..., y_k spanning the same subspace. Each step subtracts from x_{r+1} its projection along every earlier y_i." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:polynomials-of-degree-at-most-n> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(Whenever we consider this set it is understood that the zero polynomial is also included.)",
        "EXAMPLE 7. The set of all polynomials of degree $\\leq n$ , where n is fixed." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following sets is a function space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Space of polynomials of degree at most n" ;
    ex:nameZh "次数不超过 n 的多项式空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Example 7: the set of all polynomials of degree <= n, with n fixed, is a function space. The zero polynomial is understood to be included in this set." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-5> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.5. Let S be an independent set consisting of k elements in a linear space V",
        "Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Since S is independent and spans V, Theorem 15.5 tells us that every set of $k + 1$ elements in V is dependent.",
        "THEOREM 15.5. Let S be an independent set consisting of k elements in a linear space V",
        "Then every set of $k + 1$ elements in $L(S)$ is dependent.",
        "When $V = V_{n}$ , Theorem 15.5 reduces to Theorem 12.8.",
        "and let $L(S)$ be the subspace spanned by S." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.5 (k+1 elements in the span of k independent elements are dependent)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.5(k个独立元张成空间中任意k+1个元相依)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-8>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.5: if S is an independent set of k elements in a linear space V and L(S) is the subspace spanned by S, then every set of k + 1 elements in L(S) is dependent. The proof of the corresponding result for V_n (Theorem 12.8) uses only the linear space axioms and so carries over." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-6> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Let S and T be two finite bases for V. Suppose S consists of k elements and T consists of m elements.",
        "Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Let S and T be two finite bases for V.",
        "THEOREM 15.6. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space.",
        "THEOREM 15.6. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space. Then every finite basis for $V$ has the same number of elements." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.6 (all finite bases have the same number of elements)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.6(有限基的元素个数相同)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:all-bases-have-the-same-size> ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:all-bases-are-equinumerous> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.6: if V is a finite-dimensional linear space, then every finite basis for V has the same number of elements. The proof compares two bases of k and m elements and uses Theorem 15.5 twice to get m <= k and k <= m." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:v-n-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. Let $V = V_{n}$ , the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space.",
        "the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." ;
    ex:nameEn "The space V_n of n-tuples of real numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "n 元实数组空间 V_n" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "Example 3: V = V_n, the vector space of all n-tuples of real numbers, with addition and multiplication by scalars defined in the usual way in terms of components." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-3-commutative-law-for-addition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 3. COMMUTATIVE LAW. For all x and y in V, we have $x + y = y + x$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 3. COMMUTATIVE LAW. For all x and y in V, we have $x + y = y + x$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 3: commutative law for addition" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理3:加法交换律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For all x, y in V we have x + y = y + x. It forbids the order of the summands from mattering; without it the element O of Axiom 5 would be guaranteed only as a right neutral element, and the proof of Theorem 15.1 would break down, since that proof closes by rewriting O_1 + O_2 as O_2 + O_1." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-6-existence-of-negatives> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 6: existence of negatives" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理6:负元存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For every x in V the element (-1)x satisfies x + (-1)x = O. It forbids irreversible addition: without it no equation x + y = O need be solvable, subtraction and cancellation both fail, and the closing step of the proof of Theorem 15.3(a), which adds -z to both members, is not available." .

<urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V.",
        "Rank Theorem: The number of independent columns =the number of independent rows",
        "The main theorem in this chapter connects rank and dimension. The rank of a matrix is the number of pivots. The dimension of a subspace is the number of vectors in a basis." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dimension of a space versus rank of a matrix" ;
    ex:nameZh "空间的维数与矩阵的秩" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:all-bases-are-equinumerous> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.5" ;
    ex:statement "Dimension is the common size of all bases of a space. Rank is an attribute of a matrix, equal to the number of pivots, and it happens to equal the dimension of that matrix's column space and of its row space. The two words are not interchangeable: only one of them is defined for a space and only one is defined for a matrix." .

<urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION The columns of A are linearly independent when the only solution to Ax = 0 is x = 0.",
        "DEFINITION The sequence of vectors $v _ { 1 } , \\ldots , v _ { n }$ is linearly independent if the only combination that gives the zero vector is",
        "The set $S$ is called independent if it is not dependent. In this case, for all choices of distinct elements $x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{k}$ in $S$ and scalars $c_{1},\\ldots ,c_{k}$ ," ;
    ex:nameEn "Linear independence as the uniqueness of the vanishing combination" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性无关:零组合的唯一性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ex:section "15.07",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "A family of elements is independent when the only finite linear combination of them equal to the zero element is the one with all coefficients zero. Dependence is the existence of a single witnessing relation with some coefficient nonzero." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 3. COMMUTATIVE LAW. For all x and y in V, we have $x + y = y + x$",
        "AXIOM 4. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For all $x, y$ , and $z$ in $V$ , we have $(x + y) + z = x + (y + z)$",
        "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Axioms for addition",
        "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms for addition" ;
    ex:nameZh "加法公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The second group of axioms (Axioms 3 to 6): addition in V is commutative and associative, there is a zero element O with x + O = x, and every x has the property x + (-1)x = O." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A real linear space has real numbers as scalars; a complex linear space has complex numbers as scalars.",
        "Sometimes a linear space is referred to as a linear vector space or simply a vector space; the numbers used as multipliers are also called scalars." ;
    ex:nameEn "Scalar" ;
    ex:nameZh "标量(数量)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The numbers used as multipliers of the elements of V are called scalars. A real linear space has real numbers as scalars, a complex linear space has complex numbers as scalars." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-10-orthogonal-sets-are-independent> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In particular, in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V.",
        "THEOREM 15.10. In a Euclidean space V, every orthogonal set of nonzero elements is independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.10. In a Euclidean space V, every orthogonal set of nonzero elements is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal sets of nonzero elements are independent (Theorem 15.10)" ;
    ex:nameZh "非零元素的正交集是独立的(定理 15.10)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element-forces-dependence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.10: in a Euclidean space V, every orthogonal set of nonzero elements is independent. In particular, in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V." .

<urn:studium:node:d:function-space-scalar-multiple-is-pointwise> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Multiplication of a function f by a real scalar a is defined as follows: af is that function whose value at each x in the domain of f is $af(x)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Multiplication of a function f by a real scalar a is defined as follows: af is that function whose value at each x in the domain of f is $af(x)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Multiplication of a function by a scalar is defined pointwise" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数的数乘按点定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-lives-on-the-intersection-of-domains> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "For a real a, the element af is the function whose value at each x of the domain of f is af(x), so the domain is unchanged and only the values are rescaled. Scalar multiplication is thus multiplication in R carried out argument by argument." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-homogeneity-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number.",
        "Note: Taking $c = 0$ in (3), we find that $(O, y) = 0$ for all $y$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In the homogeneity axiom, the scalar multiplier c can be any complex number." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 3: homogeneity in the first argument" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理3:对第一个变元的齐次性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom (3): c(x, y) = (cx, y) for every scalar c. What it forbids: any product on which scalars cannot be pulled out of the first slot. Dropping only this axiom destroys the norm identity ||cx|| = |c| ||x|| (Theorem 15.9c), the extraction of the coefficients c_i in the proofs of Theorems 15.10 and 15.11, and the corollary (O, y) = 0. Because the display of axiom (3) occupies a line of only 21 characters, the anchor below is the sentence that names the axiom and uses it." .

<urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-to-a-whole-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "An element in V is said to be orthogonal to S if it is orthogonal to every element of S.",
        "DEFINITION The orthogonal complement of a subspace V contains every vector that is perpendicular to V.",
        "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one." ;
    ex:nameEn "Being orthogonal to a whole set, and the perp of a set" ;
    ex:nameZh "与整个集合正交,以及集合的垂空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "An element is orthogonal to a set when it is orthogonal to every member of the set. The collection of all such elements is a subspace, and being orthogonal to a set is the same as being orthogonal to its span." .

<urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "$A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } ( \\pmb { b } - A \\pmb { \\widehat { x } } ) = \\mathbf { \\widehat { 0 } }$ in its famous form $A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } A { \\widehat { \\mathbf { x } } } = A ^ { \\mathrm { T } } b$",
        "Start with n vectors $\\mathbf { a } _ { 1 } , \\ldots , \\mathbf { a } _ { n }$ in $\\mathbf { R } ^ { m }$ . Assume that these",
        "and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ . If $x \\in V$ , the element $s$ defined by the equation" ;
    ex:nameEn "Projection of an element onto a finite-dimensional subspace" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素在有限维子空间上的投影" ;
    ex:nodeType "method" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:x:projection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ex:section "15.14",
        "4.2" ;
    ex:statement "Given a finite-dimensional subspace S and an element x of the ambient space, the projection of x on S is the unique element p of S such that x minus p is orthogonal to S. It is the S-component of the orthogonal splitting of x, and it does not depend on how S is presented." .

<urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In a general linear space, we write $(x, y)$ instead of $x \\cdot y$ for inner products, and we define the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula.",
        "This example shows that there may be more than one inner product in a given linear space.",
        "Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero: $\\boldsymbol { v } \\boldsymbol { \\cdot w } = \\boldsymbol { v } ^ { \\mathrm { T } } \\boldsymbol { w } = \\boldsymbol { 0 }$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "The bilinear pairing that carries length and angle" ;
    ex:nameZh "承载长度与角度的配对" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ex:section "15.10",
        "4.1" ;
    ex:statement "Geometry is imported into a linear space by a pairing of two elements that is conjugate-symmetric (simply symmetric in the real case), positive-definite, and linear in the first argument while conjugate-linear in the second (simply bilinear in the real case). Length is the square root of the pairing of an element with itself, and orthogonality is the vanishing of the pairing." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A subset S of V is called an orthogonal set if $(x, y) = 0$ for every pair of distinct elements x and y in S.",
        "so S is an orthogonal set. Since no member of S is the zero element, S is independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A subset S of V is called an orthogonal set if $(x, y) = 0$ for every pair of distinct elements x and y in S.",
        "so S is an orthogonal set. Since no member of S is the zero element, S is independent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal set" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:strang:basis> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:d:trigonometric-orthogonal-system> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "A subset S of a Euclidean space V is called an orthogonal set if (x, y) = 0 for every pair of distinct elements x and y in S. Example: the trigonometric system in C(0, 2pi) with the integral inner product is an orthogonal set." .

<urn:studium:node:d:function-space-addition-is-pointwise> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The elements of V are real-valued functions, with addition of two functions f and g defined in the usual way:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The elements of V are real-valued functions, with addition of two functions f and g defined in the usual way:" ;
    ex:nameEn "Function space addition is defined pointwise" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数空间的加法按点定义" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "In every function space of this section the sum f + g is the function whose value at x is f(x) + g(x). Addition of elements is therefore not a new operation at all but addition of real numbers applied at each argument separately." .

<urn:studium:node:x:basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION A basis for a vector space is a sequence of vectors with two properties:",
        "DEFINITION. A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V.",
        "Note about the language of linear algebra We never say \"the rank of a space\" or \"the dimension of a basis\" or \"the basis of a matrix\"." ;
    ex:nameEn "Basis: an independent spanning family" ;
    ex:nameZh "基:独立且张成" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "A basis of a space is a family that is both independent and spanning. The two clauses are logically independent of each other, and together they are what makes representation of every element possible and unique." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. Let $S$ be a nonempty subset of a linear space $V$ . An element $x$ in $V$ of the form",
        "is called a finite linear combination of elements of S. The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Finite linear combination of elements of a set" ;
    ex:nameZh "集合中元素的有限线性组合" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "For a nonempty subset S of a linear space V, an element x of V of the form x = sum of c_i x_i with all x_i in S and c_i scalars is called a finite linear combination of elements of S." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-11-components-relative-to-an-orthogonal-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "In particular, if $S$ is an orthonormal basis, each $c_{j}$ is given by",
        "THEOREM 15.11. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dimension $n$ , and assume that $S = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$ .",
        "then its components relative to the ordered basis $(e_1, \\ldots, e_n)$ are given by the formulas" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Proof. Taking the inner product of each member of (15.7) with $e_{j}$ , we obtain",
        "assume that $S = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$ " ;
    ex:nameEn "Components relative to an orthogonal basis (Theorem 15.11)" ;
    ex:nameZh "关于正交基的分量公式(定理 15.11)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-basis>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.11: if V is a finite-dimensional Euclidean space of dimension n and S = {e_1, ..., e_n} is an orthogonal basis, then the components of x relative to the ordered basis are c_j = (x, e_j)/(e_j, e_j). In particular, for an orthonormal basis c_j = (x, e_j), so x is the sum of (x, e_i)e_i." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(b) $\\| x\\| >0$ if $x\\neq O$ (positivity).",
        "THEOREM 15.9. In a Euclidean space, every norm has the following properties for all elements $x$ and $y$ and all scalars $c$ :",
        "The next theorem gives fundamental properties of norms that do not depend on the choice of inner product." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.9. In a Euclidean space, every norm has the following properties for all elements $x$ and $y$ and all scalars $c$ :" ;
    ex:nameEn "Fundamental properties of norms (Theorem 15.9)" ;
    ex:nameZh "范数的基本性质(定理 15.9)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.9: in a Euclidean space, every norm satisfies, for all x, y and all scalars c: (a) ||x|| = 0 if x = O; (b) ||x|| > 0 if x is not O (positivity); (c) ||cx|| = |c| ||x|| (homogeneity); (d) ||x + y|| <= ||x|| + ||y|| (triangle inequality). These properties do not depend on the choice of inner product." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-2-closure-under-multiplication-by-real-numbers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 2: closure under multiplication by real numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理2:实数乘法封闭" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For every x in V and every real a the product ax lies in V. It forbids a set that is stable under addition but not under rescaling: the n-tuples with integer components are closed under addition, yet halving one of them leaves the set, so they violate Axiom 2 and form no linear space." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-9-distributive-law-for-addition-of-numbers> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 9. DISTRIBUTIVE LAW FOR ADDITION OF NUMBERS. For all $x$ in $V$ and all real $a$ and $b$ , we have" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 9. DISTRIBUTIVE LAW FOR ADDITION OF NUMBERS. For all $x$ in $V$ and all real $a$ and $b$ , we have" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 9: distributive law for addition of numbers" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理9:对数的加法的分配律" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-multiplication-by-numbers> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For all x in V and all real a, b we have (a + b)x = ax + bx, which ties addition of scalars to addition in V. It forbids the two additions from drifting apart: the rule a . x = |a|x on V_n obeys Axioms 7, 8 and 10 yet sends (1 + (-1)) . x to O while 1 . x + (-1) . x is 2x. This is the axiom that carries the proofs of Theorem 15.3(a) and (c)." .

<urn:studium:node:strang:basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION A basis for a vector space is a sequence of vectors with two properties:",
        "The basis vectors are linearly independent and they span the space.",
        "Two vectors $\\mathrm { c a n ' t }$ span all of $\\mathbf { R } ^ { 3 }$ even if they are independent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The basis vectors are linearly independent and they span the space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Basis for a vector space" ;
    ex:nameZh "向量空间的基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:spanning-a-space> ;
    ex:section "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "A basis for a vector space is a sequence of vectors that are linearly independent and that span the space: not too many and not too few. A space has infinitely many different bases." .

<urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION A set of vectors spans a space if their linear combinations fill the space.",
        "Different sets may span the same subspace.",
        "We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S, and denote it by $L(S)$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Span of a set of elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "集合的张成" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "15.06",
        "3.4" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all finite linear combinations of the elements of a set S is a subspace, the smallest one containing S. Different sets can have the same span, so a span does not determine its generators." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:integral-inner-product-on-continuous-functions> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "EXAMPLE 3. Let $C(a, b)$ denote the linear space of all real-valued functions continuous on an interval $[a, b]$ . Define an inner product of two functions $f$ and $g$ by the formula",
        "The function values $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ play the role of the components $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ , and integration takes the place of summation." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Define an inner product of two functions $f$ and $g$ by the formula",
        "This formula is analogous to Equation (15.5) which defines the dot product of two vectors in $V_{n}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Integral inner product on C(a, b)" ;
    ex:nameZh "连续函数空间 C(a,b) 上的积分内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "On C(a, b), the linear space of all real-valued functions continuous on [a, b], an inner product of f and g is defined by the integral of f(t)g(t) over [a, b]. The function values f(t) and g(t) play the role of the components, and integration takes the place of summation." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthonormal-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1.",
        "Dividing each $u_{n}$ by its norm, we obtain an orthonormal set $\\{\\varphi_0,\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2,\\ldots \\}$ where $\\varphi_{n} = u_{n} / \\| u_{n}\\|$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1.",
        "orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1" ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthonormal set" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交规范集" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "An orthogonal set is called orthonormal if each of its elements has norm 1. Dividing each element of an orthogonal set by its norm produces an orthonormal set." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-4-subspace-criterion> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "If S is a subspace, it satisfies all the axioms for a linear space, and hence, in particular, it satisfies the closure axioms.",
        "Now we show that if S satisfies the closure axioms it satisfies the others as well.",
        "THEOREM 15.4. Let S be a nonempty subset of a linear space V. Then S is a subspace if and only if S satisfies the closure axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The next theorem gives a simple criterion for determining whether or not a subset of a linear space is a subspace.",
        "The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.4 (closure test for a subspace)" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理15.4(子空间的封闭性判别)" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:solution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "THEOREM 15.4: if S is a nonempty subset of a linear space V, then S is a subspace if and only if S satisfies the closure axioms. The proof shows the remaining axioms are inherited from V or follow from closure." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-1-closure-under-addition> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y, denoted by $x + y$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y, denoted by $x + y$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 1: closure under addition" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理1:加法封闭" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "For every pair x, y in V the sum x + y is again a unique element of V. It forbids two different pathologies: an addition that carries a pair out of the set, as adding two polynomials of degree exactly n can do, and a sum that is many-valued or undefined, without which an expression such as x + y + z would not denote one element at all." .

<urn:studium:node:d:hermitian-symmetry-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, y) = \\overline {{(y , x)}},\\tag{1'}",
        "cept that the symmetry axiom is replaced by the relation",
        "where $(y, x)$ denotes the complex conjugate of $(y, x)$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, y) = \\overline {{(y , x)}},\\tag{1'}",
        "cept that the symmetry axiom is replaced by the relation" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 1': Hermitian symmetry replaces symmetry in a complex space" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理1':复空间中 Hermite 对称取代对称性" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-symmetry-axiom> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:complex-inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In a complex linear space the symmetry axiom (1) is replaced by (x, y) = conjugate of (y, x), labelled (1') by Apostol. Every other axiom is kept verbatim. What it forbids: plain symmetry, which in the complex case is incompatible with strict positivity, since plain symmetry plus complex homogeneity in the first slot would force (cx, cx) = c^2 (x, x) and thus a negative or non-real value of (x, x) for suitable c." .

<urn:studium:node:d:inner-product-positivity-axiom> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 4: strict positivity, (x, x) > 0 if x is not the zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理4:严格正定性,x 非零时 (x, x) > 0" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:d:self-inner-product-is-real-in-complex-case> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The fourth axiom: (x, x) > 0 whenever x is not the zero element. What it forbids: semi-definite forms. If the strict inequality is weakened to (x, x) >= 0, one gets a positive SEMI-definite form on which there may exist nonzero elements with (x, x) = 0, hence nonzero elements of norm zero. Then Theorem 15.9(b) fails, ||x|| = 0 no longer characterizes x = O, the angle quotient (x,y)/(||x|| ||y||) can have zero denominator for nonzero arguments, the step '(x_1, x_1) is not 0 since x_1 is not O' in Theorem 15.10 fails, and the denominator (e_j, e_j) in Theorem 15.11 may vanish." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:closure-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y",
        "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax.",
        "Closure axioms",
        "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:nameEn "Closure axioms" ;
    ex:nameZh "封闭性公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:d:ten-axioms-listed-in-three-groups> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "The first group of axioms (Axioms 1 and 2): every pair x, y in V has a unique sum x + y in V, and every x in V together with every real number a has a product ax in V." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-elementary-algebraic-properties> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.3. In a given linear space, let $x$ and $y$ denote arbitrary elements and let $a$ and $b$ denote arbitrary scalars. Then we have the following properties:",
        "The next theorem describes a number of properties which govern elementary algebraic manipulations in a linear space.",
        "We shall prove (a), (b), and (c) and leave the proofs of the other properties as exercises." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "THEOREM 15.3. In a given linear space, let $x$ and $y$ denote arbitrary elements and let $a$ and $b$ denote arbitrary scalars.",
        "let $x$ and $y$ denote arbitrary elements and let $a$ and $b$ denote arbitrary scalars" ;
    ex:nameEn "Theorem 15.3: elementary algebraic properties of a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "定理 15.3:线性空间中的初等代数性质" ;
    ex:nodeType "theorem" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:scalar> ;
    ex:section "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "For arbitrary elements x, y and arbitrary scalars a, b in a linear space: 0x = O; aO = O; (-a)x = -(ax) = a(-x); ax = O implies a = 0 or x = O; ax = ay with a nonzero implies x = y; ax = bx with x nonzero implies a = b; -(x + y) = -x - y; and x added to itself n times equals nx." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:dimension> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V.",
        "EXAMPLE 1. The space $V_{n}$ has dimension $n$ . One basis is the set of $n$ unit coordinate vectors.",
        "If $V = \\{O\\}$ , we say V has dimension 0." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V.",
        "Different sets may span the same subspace. For example, the space $V_{2}$ is spanned by each of the following sets of vectors: $\\{i,j\\}$ , $\\{i,j,i+j\\}$ , $\\{O,i,-i,j,-j,i+j\\}$ ." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dimension of a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间的维数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:rank-theorem> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:dimension> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:dimension-versus-rank> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "If a linear space V has a basis of n elements, the integer n is called the dimension of V. The space consisting of the zero element alone is said to have dimension 0." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-dimensional-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "THEOREM 15.6. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional linear space.",
        "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of O alone." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis" ;
    ex:nameEn "Finite-dimensional linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "有限维线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space V is called finite dimensional if it has a finite basis, or if V consists of the zero element O alone." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that",
        "Axiom 5 tells us that there is at least one zero element." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "零元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.02",
        "15.04" ;
    ex:statement "Axiom 5 postulates an element of V, denoted by O, with x + O = x for all x in V. In a function space the zero element is the function whose values are everywhere zero." .

<urn:studium:node:d:axiom-5-existence-of-zero-element> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that",
        "x + O = x \\quad f o r a l l x i n V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "AXIOM 5. EXISTENCE OF ZERO ELEMENT. There is an element in V, denoted by O, such that" ;
    ex:nameEn "Axiom 5: existence of a zero element" ;
    ex:nameZh "公理5:零元素存在" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:axioms-for-addition> ;
    ex:section "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "There is an element O in V with x + O = x for all x in V. It forbids a set with no additive neutral element: with no O there is nothing for a negative to add up to in Axiom 6, the difference y - x cannot be defined, and the later machinery of vanishing linear combinations, dependence, span and subspace has no target to vanish to." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-span> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V.",
        "We call this the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S, and denote it by $L(S)$" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and hence is a subspace of V.",
        "is called a finite linear combination of elements of S. The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S" ;
    ex:nameEn "Subspace spanned by a set (linear span)" ;
    ex:nameZh "由集合张成的子空间(线性张成)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:spanning-a-space> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:span-of-a-set> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-linear-combination> ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "The set of all finite linear combinations of elements of S satisfies the closure axioms and is therefore a subspace of V; it is called the subspace spanned by S, or the linear span of S. If S is empty the span is defined to be the set consisting of the zero element alone." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:orthogonal-elements> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero.",
        "The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V; it is the only element orthogonal to itself." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero.",
        "two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero" ;
    ex:nameEn "Orthogonal elements" ;
    ex:nameZh "正交元素" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-vectors> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:orthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:angle-between-two-elements> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.11" ;
    ex:statement "In a Euclidean space V, two elements x and y are called orthogonal if their inner product is zero. The zero element is orthogonal to every element of V, and it is the only element orthogonal to itself." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:subspace> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Given a linear space V, let S be a nonempty subset of V.",
        "If S is also a linear space, with the same operations of addition and multiplication by scalars, then S is called a subspace of V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Subspace of a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间的子空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ex:section "15.06" ;
    ex:statement "Given a linear space V, a nonempty subset S of V is called a subspace of V if S is itself a linear space under the same operations of addition and multiplication by scalars." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. A set S of elements in a linear space V is called dependent if there is a finite set of distinct elements in S",
        "and corresponding set of scalars $c_{1}, \\ldots, c_{k}$ , not all zero, such that" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets.",
        "\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {k} c _ {i} x _ {i} = O." ;
    ex:nameEn "Dependent set in a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间中的相依集(线性相关集)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-chapter-12-finite-dependence-definition> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "A set S in a linear space V is dependent if some finite set of distinct elements of S admits scalars, not all zero, whose corresponding linear combination equals the zero element O." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "The inner product can be used to introduce the metric concept of length in any Euclidean space.",
        "When we use the term Euclidean space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "A real linear space with an inner product is called a real Euclidean space.",
        "The inner product can be used to introduce the metric concept of length in any Euclidean space." ;
    ex:nameEn "Euclidean space" ;
    ex:nameZh "欧几里得空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:orthogonal-vectors> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space equipped with an inner product. When the term Euclidean space is used without further designation the space may be real or complex, and the theorems of the chapter hold in either case. The inner product is what introduces the metric concept of length into such a space." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:finite-basis> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V.",
        "is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V." ;
    ex:nameEn "Finite basis for a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间的有限基" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:strang:basis> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:basis> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:spanning-set> ;
    ex:section "15.08" ;
    ex:statement "A finite set S of elements in a linear space V is called a finite basis for V if S is independent and spans V." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:function-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Multiplication of a function f by a real scalar a is defined as follows: af is that function whose value at each x in the domain of f is $af(x)$ .",
        "The following examples are called function spaces. The elements of V are real-valued functions, with addition of two functions f and g defined in the usual way:" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "The reader can easily verify that each of the following examples satisfies all the axioms for a real linear space.",
        "The zero element is the function whose values are everywhere zero." ;
    ex:nameEn "Function space" ;
    ex:nameZh "函数空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:apostol:real-linear-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:zero-element> ;
    ex:section "15.03" ;
    ex:statement "A function space is a linear space whose elements are real-valued functions, with (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x), with af the function whose value at x is af(x), and with the everywhere-zero function as zero element." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:independent-set> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "For example, the elements in an independent set are called independent elements.",
        "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets.",
        "The set $S$ is called independent if it is not dependent." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "However, the present definition is not restricted to finite sets.",
        "If S is a finite set, the foregoing definition agrees with that given in Chapter 12 for the space $V_{n}$ .",
        "The set $S$ is called independent if it is not dependent." ;
    ex:nameEn "Independent set in a linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间中的独立集(线性无关集)" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:contrasts <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-chapter-12-finite-dependence-definition>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:linear-independence> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:linear-independence> ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:dependent-set> ;
    ex:section "15.07" ;
    ex:statement "A set S is called independent if it is not dependent: for all choices of distinct elements of S and scalars, a vanishing linear combination forces every scalar to be zero. The term is also applied to the elements themselves, and unlike the Chapter 12 definition it is not restricted to finite sets." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:norm> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "Since it may be possible to define an inner product in many different ways, the norm of an element will depend on the choice of inner product.",
        "is called the norm of the element $x$ ." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "DEFINITION. In a Euclidean space V, the nonnegative number $\\|x\\|$ defined by the equation",
        "Since it may be possible to define an inner product in many different ways, the norm of an element will depend on the choice of inner product." ;
    ex:nameEn "Norm of an element" ;
    ex:nameZh "元素的范数" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:requires <urn:studium:node:apostol:euclidean-space>,
        <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "In a Euclidean space V the norm of x is the nonnegative number ||x|| = (x, x)^(1/2). It is the metric notion of length in a Euclidean space, and since an inner product can be defined in many different ways, the norm of an element depends on the choice of inner product." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:linear-space> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "Briefly, a linear space is a set of elements of any kind on which certain operations (called addition and multiplication by numbers) can be performed.",
        "In this chapter we discuss a general mathematical concept, called a linear space, which includes all these examples and many others as special cases.",
        "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." ;
    ex:nameEn "Linear space" ;
    ex:nameZh "线性空间" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:vectors-need-not-be-columns> ;
    ex:section "15.01",
        "15.02" ;
    ex:statement "A linear space is a nonempty set V of elements of any kind on which addition and multiplication by numbers can be performed, subject to ten axioms listed in three groups. The nature of the elements and of the operations is not specified; only the axioms are required." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product-axioms> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "(2) $(x,y + z) = (x,y) + (x,z)$ (distributivity, or linearity).",
        "(x, x) > 0 \\quad i f \\quad x \\neq O",
        "That is, we state a number of properties we wish inner products to satisfy and we regard these properties as axioms." ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "Properties (a), (b) and (c) follow at once from the axioms for an inner product.",
        "That is, we state a number of properties we wish inner products to satisfy and we regard these properties as axioms." ;
    ex:nameEn "Axioms for an inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "内积公理" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:implies <urn:studium:node:apostol:theorem-15-9-properties-of-norms> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ns1:other <urn:studium:node:apostol:ext-theorem-12-2> ;
    ns1:part-of <urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "The four properties an inner product is required to satisfy for all x, y, z and all real scalars c: (1) (x,y) = (y,x) (commutativity, or symmetry); (2) (x, y+z) = (x,y) + (x,z) (distributivity, or linearity); (3) c(x,y) = (cx,y); and (x,x) > 0 if x is not the zero element. Apostol states these properties and regards them as axioms." .

<urn:studium:node:apostol:inner-product> a ex:Node ;
    ex:anchorQuote "A real linear space V is said to have an inner product if for each pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique real number $(x, y)$ satisfying the following axioms",
        "we define the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula" ;
    ex:edgeEvidence "In a general linear space, we write $(x, y)$ instead of $x \\cdot y$ for inner products, and we define the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula.",
        "We shall introduce first a generalization of the dot product, which we call an inner product" ;
    ex:nameEn "Inner product" ;
    ex:nameZh "内积" ;
    ex:nodeType "concept" ;
    ns1:generalizes <urn:studium:node:apostol:dot-product-in-vn>,
        <urn:studium:node:strang:dot-product-notation> ;
    ns1:is-a <urn:studium:node:x:the-pairing-that-carries-geometry> ;
    ex:section "15.10" ;
    ex:statement "A real linear space V is said to have an inner product if to each pair of elements x, y there corresponds a unique real number (x, y) satisfying four axioms: symmetry, distributivity (linearity), homogeneity in the scalar, and positivity for nonzero x. Apostol defines the product axiomatically rather than by a specific formula." .

